import{_ as s,P as o,d as r,e as c,f as n,H as l,q as a,y as i}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Part 11 Event-Driven Dynamic Projection","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Part 11 Event-Driven Dynamic Projection","xkChars":0,"xkWords":5580,"xkLessons":{"intro-11-part":{"title":"Part Eleven: Event-Driven Dynamic Projection","lead":"Dynamic projection is not continuing a fluent story; it asks what is currently known, where an event type changes things first, and through which conditions it affects somewhere else. Begin by preserving the status of facts, hearsay, plans, and model assumptions.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2267–2270; source anchor part_11; short quotation translated from Chinese: ‘Actions that actually occur may change reality whether or not they were authorized.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","This part starts from a joint state with explicit evidence status. It connects actors, circles, relations, material conditions, and experiential-meaning conditions for a task, without claiming observation of the world’s full state. Every item retains its source, window, support, and unknowns; subsequent inference cannot merge them into equally reliable starting points.","The introduction lists five entry types: observed, reported, planned, hypothetical, and simulated events. Observed events still have measurement-contract limits; a report may truly occur while its content remains unchecked; plans may affect preparation without being executed; hypotheses and simulations support only their conditional consequences. Updating event status with new evidence must retain relations and versions rather than erase original uncertainty.","After an event, distinguish at least directly confirmable state differences, conditional consequences inferred from mechanisms, and signals supporting or weakening the corresponding paths. Receiving a message, for example, is a separately checkable reception event; changed belief, action, or institutional fields require corresponding material. Observing two successive events does not automatically identify the channel between them.","Actual action may change reality even without authorization. Analysis must record actions and consequences that actually occur, separately retaining normative evaluation, legal or institutional authority, and execution permission for this occasion. No right to act does not mean no action; action taken does not confer the right. Recording facts neither excuses the action nor permits the model to implement it.","This connects to §11.3’s notation: u(t) specifically denotes externally authorized actions that actually occur; observed or reported unauthorized actions enter e(t) with their event status and separately recorded authority status. Unperformed unauthorized scenarios may be analyzed while remaining hypothetical or simulated. Separate notation must neither delete reality nor apply the same shock twice.","The following sections set event contracts, joint updating, the nine-step closed loop, propagation and cascades, path graphs, variable candidates, counterfactuals and recursion, stopping, and write-back. Part Twelve then takes responsibility for preregistered foresight and bounded choice. A conditional path obtained now does not establish predictive benefit or authority for real adoption."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Action occurs","leftText":"The corresponding event and consequences exist in the world.","rightTitle":"Action authorized","rightText":"Authority and occasion-specific permission need separate checks; neither implies the other."},{"leftTitle":"A report exists","leftText":"A person or source actually made a statement.","rightTitle":"The report’s contents are true","rightText":"Requires content verification, not inference from the statement’s existence."},{"leftTitle":"Conditional projection","leftText":"Possible later states given initial conditions and rules.","rightTitle":"Established foresight capability","rightText":"Additionally needs preregistration, baselines, evaluation, and write-back."},{"leftTitle":"Simulated state","leftText":"A state generated by model rules.","rightTitle":"Real-world observation","rightText":"A record from actual material, with a different evidence status."}],"boundaries":["Do not merge unlawfulness or unauthorized-status judgments with whether an action occurred; no case-specific legal judgment is made here.","Recording harmful or unauthorized action does not authorize the model to replicate it.","Direct observation may also omit or mismeasure; it cannot become an absolute fact of unlimited scope.","Generating the same simulation many times adds no new real-world sources."],"questions":["An action occurred without permission: should it be deleted, or how should it be recorded?","A report was received and believed; does that establish the report’s truth?","Does a complete conditional-path graph prove improved predictive capability?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/figures/intro-11-part.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"intro-11-part","start_line":2339,"end_line":2342,"startLine":2339,"endLine":2342}],"outputId":"intro-11-part","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Determine the current joint state, evidence cutoff, and gaps; specify whether the task explains, replays, or develops conditional consequences.","Register one of the five event statuses for each input, separating occurrence of reporting from truth of content.","Record direct changes, then infer through conditionally supported channels, listing supportive and contrary signals.","Judge occurrence and authority separately; actual unauthorized events cannot be removed from reality’s record.","Retain exploratory hypotheses and simulation results at their respective levels without writing them back as actual facts.","Conditional consequences, future capability, and action authority each carry their own later proof or procedural responsibilities."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: zero authority while the state actually changes","text":"A fictional door has state d=0 (closed) at t0; a credible direct record establishes that an actor opens it at t1, giving d=1. A separate explicit premise says the action lacked this occasion’s permission, J=0. Correct recording retains both d:0→1 and J=0 and analyzes it as an observed event. A rule that ‘J=0 makes changing d impossible’ conflicts with the stipulated facts.","assumptions":"Door, record, and permission are teaching assumptions; no actual door opening, sanction, or legal classification is involved.","boundary":"Shows logical independence of fact and authority, not permission to execute unauthorized action."},{"title":"Logical construction: believing a report does not make it true","text":"A fictional report r=1 means a statement ‘the door is open’ was actually received. Actual door state may still be d=0 or d=1. Suppose additionally that the listener initially believed it closed and, under this example’s rule, directly trusts the report, changing belief b from 0 to 1. Both d values fit the same r,b. Report arrival and conditional belief change can therefore be discussed without inventing an open-door fact.","assumptions":"The listener’s trust is an explicit rule here, not a claim that everyone necessarily believes reports; actual belief change needs separate material.","boundary":"Report or belief values cannot directly recover the actual door state."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"No authorization existed, so the action that actually occurred should not enter the model.","effect":"Mistaking an authority limit for factual nonexistence omits actual risks and consequences.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model generates the same future three times, so it has become an observed outcome.","effect":"Repeated generation adds model material, not external facts.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"An action occurred without permission: should it be deleted, or how should it be recorded?","answer":"Retain the actual event and consequences, separately recording permission and normative status; under §11.3 it is an observed/reported event, not authorized action u.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"A report was received and believed; does that establish the report’s truth?","answer":"No. Reports, beliefs, and actual states have different verification targets.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a complete conditional-path graph prove improved predictive capability?","answer":"No. Preregistration, matched baselines, and evaluation against actual outcomes are still required.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/figures/intro-11-part.png","alt":"Observed/reported: sources, windows, disputes, with content checked accordingly; planned: preparation and expectations may change, without equaling execution; hypothetical/simulated: conditions and model version, not written back as actual facts; teaching fact d=0→1: an actual action whose fact cannot be deleted; teaching fact J=0: no permission for this occasion, with occurrence providing no exemption; projection output: state differences, channels, branches, supportive and contrary signals. The upper row lists five event entry types; the lower illustrates separate values for facts and authority. Conditional projection does not itself authorize actual action.","caption":"The upper row lists five event entry types; the lower illustrates separate values for facts and authority. Conditional projection does not itself authorize actual action."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the selections, switches, or clicks described below are ideas for a later design only. Six cards are not a developmental sequence; the five event types are grouped visually but retain separate identities.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This part’s introduction specifies material status and responsibility boundaries; logical constructions suffice to show nonimplications. Specific historical material appears in later event and path units rather than being forced into normative proof here."},"openQuestions":["This guide executes no real action and obtains no new authority over any actual object."],"taskId":"S-11-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full text, lines 2213–2230; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["intro-11-part: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis","11.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"events","title":"Historical research draft: original event status, updates, and stopping","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.2–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2231–2276; §11.10, lines 2414–2419","claim_support":["Introduction and 11.1: five event identities, u/e boundaries, late material, and suspension of local dependencies"],"limits":["Check actual authority and facts independently; field structures certify no content.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"forecast","title":"Historical research draft: original conditional foresight and use evaluation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§12.1–12.4, Chinese source lines 2450–2517","claim_support":["Introduction and 11.1: conditional paths are not established predictive capability; probability, research priority, and analytical recommendations have different statuses"],"limits":["No actual prospective evaluation was performed.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow","title":"Snow’s 1855 original: Broad Street timing records","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Printed pp. 51–52, discussion after Table I and ‘The pump-well in Broad Street, Golden Square’","claim_support":["11.1: handle removal on September 8, declining onset beforehand, limitations from population departure, and uncertainty retained by the author"],"limits":["Later-fatal-case material is not an all-infection curve; the independent increment of handle removal is not identified, gaps are not treated as zero effect, and the original is a retrospective account."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"Part Eleven: Event-Driven Dynamic Projection; Chinese source lines 2267–2270; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"ff3fd1a2ec1e76843c504c961c8ba2da1be62f07ac13facaf7b82a6ac4aaf010","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.1":{"title":"11.1 Projection versus Narrative Continuation","lead":"A story often selects a smooth ending; structural projection must state which other endings fit current material equally well. Its credibility lies not in always sounding certain but in traceable conditions at every step and knowing where to stop when key conditions are missing.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2271–2284; source anchor section-11-1; short quotation translated from Chinese: ‘If multiple continuations fit the available evidence, retain branching.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","Narrative may select one coherent continuation for literary purposes; structural projection on factual tasks cannot treat coherence as support. Preserve conditions, transmission channels, competing paths, and failure meanings: what permits continuation, which observation refutes, and which gap leaves judgment unresolved. More branches are not inherently better, but grounded differences cannot be flattened by a fluent story.","Minimum input has eight items: frozen snapshot, event records, mechanism hypotheses, parameter ranges, clock policy, propagation policy, unknowns, and stopping conditions. Freezing identifies the version used in this run, not absolute correctness. Explicit unknowns may also be frozen. Late material creates a new run rather than flowing backward into the old starting point.","The joint snapshot freezes actors, circles, relations, M/Ψ, clocks, and evidence cutoff, preventing post-result object changes or access to future information. M and Ψ separately retain material and experience–meaning conditions. Genuinely inapplicable items may have reasons stated; symmetrical forms do not justify invented psychological states.","Events freeze type, time, source, reached objects, and channels, preventing rumors, plans, observations, and simulations from being mixed. Mechanisms freeze direction, carrier, delay, threshold, and failure conditions, preventing an explanation from retrospectively reshaping itself for every result. If only a candidate mechanism is available, say candidate; the procedural act of freezing cannot promote it to causal fact.","Parameters may be points, intervals, grades, or explicitly unknown, depending on material and task. Intervals do not automatically carry probability distributions, nor do two branches automatically split equally. Clock policy specifies sequence, update rhythm, and concurrency handling; propagation policy specifies transmissible channels and blocking conditions, rather than allowing every node to update simultaneously after one event.","Stopping conditions cover insufficient information, risk, use beyond scope, and resource limits. A local channel gap pauses dependent inferences, while independently established direct facts or bounded total effects may remain. Failure of shared premises or overall use conditions stops all relevant runs. Stopping does not force unknown into failure or erase known facts.","Minimum output also has eight items: path graph, state differences at each step, support status, early signals, contrary signals, residuals, variable candidates, and write-back requirements. The graph shows grounded subsequent differences; state differences specify fields; support status distinguishes factual and conditional status; the two signal types strengthen and weaken paths respectively, rather than collecting only favorable news.","Residuals are differences unexplained by the current representation and mechanism, not arbitrary random noise or immediately nameable hidden motives. Variable candidates turn gaps into testable questions. Write-back requirements specify when and from which sources results are appended, how original branches, competing paths, and baselines are compared, and how failures remain recorded.","If material permits several continuations, retain parallel branches. Without grounded probabilities or rankings, do not force an ordering. Consequence severity or a tight observation window may prioritize investigation of one branch, but research priority is not occurrence probability. Clear supported scope is stronger than unsupported decimals or directions creating an impression of certainty."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Frozen input","leftText":"Identifies this run's version, knowns, and unknowns.","rightTitle":"True or sufficient input","rightText":"Still requires evidence and applicability checks; freezing is not certification."},{"leftTitle":"Candidate mechanism","leftText":"Testable hypotheses about direction, carriers, and conditions.","rightTitle":"Identified mechanism","rightText":"Requires corresponding distinguishing material, not inference from narrative coherence."},{"leftTitle":"Branch interval","leftText":"A range of still-compatible values or continuations.","rightTitle":"Probability distribution","rightText":"Needs generation or calibration grounds; two branches are not automatically 50% each."},{"leftTitle":"Research priority","leftText":"Consequences, timing, or distinguishability determine what to examine first.","rightTitle":"Path-probability ranking","rightText":"Asks which is likelier and requires separate evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Local stopping","leftText":"Stops extending the part dependent on a missing condition.","rightTitle":"Erasing every result","rightText":"Independent facts and results may remain."}],"boundaries":["Complete methodological fields, complete diagrams, and freezing itself are not real-world truth.","Do not choose for actors, supply inner states, or invent new variable values to complete a narrative.","Unknown parameters may support conditional ranges without supporting directions or rankings.","Retrospective reconstruction cannot masquerade as contemporaneously available information or a successful prediction made before results.","An unidentified additional effect is not zero effect and does not cancel independently established evidence for other questions."],"questions":["For c=1 and c=2, what are queue lengths after two windows?","If Monday's capacity record arrives Tuesday, may it be backfilled into Monday's old forecast to claim a contemporaneous hit?","If only one intermediate channel is unknown while endpoint change was directly observed, should the endpoint fact be deleted?","What can be done when branches cannot be ranked?","Does freezing a mechanism hypothesis prove it?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/figures/section-11-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Snow retained the decline before pump-handle removal","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Snow retained the decline before pump-handle removal","text":"In the 1855 second edition, Snow records removal of the Broad Street pump handle on September 8, 1854, while explicitly noting that incidence had already declined markedly before use of the water stopped and discussing population departures. The history cannot therefore be reduced to “decline began only after handle removal.”","sourceLabel":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera, 1855 second edition","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Original printed pp.51–52: onset-date discussion following Table I and the passage beginning “The pump-well in Broad Street, Golden Square”","boundary":"Table I compiles onset and death information for eventual fatalities, not a curve of all infections. This section estimates no independent removal effect, nor infers complete ineffectiveness from insufficient identification. The book is retrospective and does not establish that this lesson reconstructed the complete information set available then.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.1","start_line":2343,"end_line":2356,"startLine":2343,"endLine":2356}],"outputId":"section-11-1","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Freeze eight input categories, declaring each item's factual, candidate, scenario, or unknown status.","Specify the table's five content groups—joint snapshot, events, mechanisms, parameters, and stopping—and additionally expand clock and propagation policies.","Generate state differences stepwise from the starting point under explicit rules, not narrative order in place of channels.","Retain grounded competing branches and incomparable items, listing early and contrary signals separately.","Register unexplained differences as residuals and several distinguishable candidates, without inventing actual values.","Pause only paths affected by a gap, or stop all relevant runs when shared premises or overall use conditions fail.","Append actual outcomes under predetermined requirements and evaluate old runs; new information cannot rewrite old success."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: retain two queue paths from one starting point","text":"Let fictional q0=2 items, with 1 additional arrival at t0. Each window first adds registered new tasks, then a single service point processes exactly min(queue,c) items. Capacity c remains fixed over two windows but is known only as c∈{1,2}; there are no new arrivals in window two. Thus q1=max(0,2+1−c). For c=1, q1=2 and q2=1; for c=2, q1=1 and q2=0. Both paths fit the starting point. A smooth story of “definitely empty next window” cannot be selected, nor may each branch receive 50% without probability material. Actual first-window completions distinguish the branches.","assumptions":"Homogeneous tasks, a work-conserving service rule, fixed c, no hidden entries/exits, and common within-window order are explicit. Numbers are teaching constructions.","boundary":"If first-window records show 3 completions or a queue greater than 2, at least one original condition needs review. c cannot quietly be changed while retaining success of the original run."},{"title":"Teaching construction: late information creates a new version","text":"Fictional V1 closes information at noon Monday, knowing only c∈{1,2}. Tuesday brings a credible record establishing actual Monday c=2, allowing reconstruction of the past and creation of V2. V1 retains its contemporaneous two branches and unknown. Backfilling V2's c=2 into V1 and claiming a unique Monday prediction of an empty queue rewrites the starting point after results.","assumptions":"Dates, records, and credibility are teaching assumptions, not actual research logs.","boundary":"Retrospective explanation may be valuable, but its information set differs from the original foresight evaluation."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The smoothest story is that increased resources lead everyone to cooperate, so only the cooperation branch is retained.","effect":"Missing role, incentive, channel, or competing-path material cannot be replaced by coherence to eliminate branching.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Listing two possible unknown-capacity values means each branch has probability one half.","effect":"A candidate set does not automatically supply a probability measure; separate grounds are needed.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Cases decline after pump closure, so prior changes are omitted from the account.","effect":"Deleting an existing pretrend mistakes a before/after story for proof of an independent incremental effect.","sourceIds":["snow"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"For c=1 and c=2, what are queue lengths after two windows?","answer":"Respectively 1 and 0, calculated window by window under work-conserving updates. Retain both when only the starting range is known.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If Monday's capacity record arrives Tuesday, may it be backfilled into Monday's old forecast to claim a contemporaneous hit?","answer":"No. A new version or replay may be created, but the old information cutoff remains.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If only one intermediate channel is unknown while endpoint change was directly observed, should the endpoint fact be deleted?","answer":"No. Retain direct change or independently identified results, limiting only the unsupported propagation explanation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What can be done when branches cannot be ranked?","answer":"State scope and unknowns, give distinguishing observations, conditions, contrary signals, and stopping points. Research priority may have separately explicit reasons.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does freezing a mechanism hypothesis prove it?","answer":"No. Freezing supplies an evaluation reference; mechanism truth still requires corresponding evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/figures/section-11-1.png","alt":"Frozen starting point, constructed: q0=2, another 1 arrives, c∈{1,2}, unknown retained. Branch one: c=1, first-window processing 1, q1=2. Branch two: c=2, processing 2, q1=1. Second window without arrivals: min(q1,1) processed, q2=1; or min(q1,2) processed, q2=0. All values are explicit teaching constructions. Branches have no automatic 50% probabilities; first-window completions distinguish them, and late material does not overwrite the old run.","caption":"All values are explicit teaching constructions. Branches have no automatic 50% probabilities; first-window completions distinguish them, and late material does not overwrite the old run."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. A five-node, four-edge conditional tree unfolds two windows stepwise. Any later interaction would switch only capacity conditions; the present diagram is static.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The same primary narrative explicitly preserves pretrends and competing conditions, directly showing why structural projection cannot select only a smooth retrospective chain. This section offers no current medical advice."},"openQuestions":["No complete historical counterfactual comparison was obtained. The additional handle-removal effect remains outside this example's identification scope, and the queue construction provides no effect evidence for historical data."],"taskId":"S-11-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full text, lines 2213–2230; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["intro-11-part: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis","11.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"events","title":"Historical research draft: original event status, updates, and stopping","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.2–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2231–2276; §11.10, lines 2414–2419","claim_support":["Introduction and 11.1: five event identities, u/e boundaries, late material, and suspension of local dependencies"],"limits":["Check actual authority and facts independently; field structures certify no content.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"forecast","title":"Historical research draft: original conditional foresight and use evaluation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§12.1–12.4, Chinese source lines 2450–2517","claim_support":["Introduction and 11.1: conditional paths are not established predictive capability; probability, research priority, and analytical recommendations have different statuses"],"limits":["No actual prospective evaluation was performed.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow","title":"Snow’s 1855 original: Broad Street timing records","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Printed pp. 51–52, discussion after Table I and ‘The pump-well in Broad Street, Golden Square’","claim_support":["11.1: handle removal on September 8, declining onset beforehand, limitations from population departure, and uncertainty retained by the author"],"limits":["Later-fatal-case material is not an all-infection curve; the independent increment of handle removal is not identified, gaps are not treated as zero effect, and the original is a retrospective account."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.1 Projection versus narrative continuation; Chinese source lines 2271–2284; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"d864c46c95076c54f61233b66133ad46d8a5c11f15ecffb8d1af81d766d81aca","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.2":{"title":"11.2 Event contract","lead":"“Occurred,” “someone says it occurred,” “planned,” and “suppose it occurred” have very different implications for the next judgment. An event contract gives each input a status card so a retold claim cannot become an unverified fact after repeated transmission.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2285–2298; original anchor section-11-2. Short quotation translated from Chinese: “Occurrence of a report does not mean its content is true.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","The five event types distinguish use and evidential status, not a low-to-high credibility ranking. One real process may produce different records: publication of a plan is a real publication event, while its future action remains unexecuted; publication of a simulation report is a real transmission event, while its internal failure does not thereby become real. Distinguish the recorded objects, cross-referencing when necessary without duplicating impacts.","Observed events may enter explanation, replay, and forward projection, but only within their observation contracts. Sources, observation positions, omissions, and measurement errors are required. Seeing “complete” on a screen directly supports that display or record; external execution still needs task-appropriate execution material. Visible interfaces are not categorically invalid: the observed field determines the evidence ceiling.","Reported events permit analysis of belief propagation and conditional paths. The report's occurrence may be established while content truth remains under review. Reporter position, accessible information, disputes, and source lineage matter; repeated forwarding is not multiple independent witnesses. Reports may change recipient expectations, but actual reach and belief need evidence; identical reactions cannot be prescribed for everyone.","Planned events may change preparation, resource reservations, and strategic interaction without meaning the future action is complete. Register proposer, object, planned time, authorization, resources, and cancellation conditions. Approved plans may remain unexecuted because of resources or subsequent events; unapproved plans may genuinely affect expectations. Register plan existence, execution authority, and actual occurrence separately.","Hypothetical events serve counterfactual and scenario comparisons, requiring conditions, purpose, and feasibility. Conditions may distinguish mechanisms without claiming actual permission or inevitability. Infeasible scenarios used as logical boundaries must retain that status rather than become actual recommendations.","Simulated events belong only to specified model runs, requiring model version, initial conditions, parameters, and prohibited-extrapolation boundaries. Calculated trajectories expose conditional consequences, but repeated sampling still comes from the same model. Without new external material, it cannot raise the evidential status of a claim that a real event occurred.","Fourteen minimum-record items each bear responsibility: event ID preserves reference; occurrence time/window locates the object event; observation time states when it became known; source supplies grounds; affected actors and affected circles separately retain scope; channel explains reach; direct change specifies actual altered fields; evidence status marks support and unknowns; disputes retain inconsistent material; competing explanations prevent a single-story monopoly; reversibility distinguishes what can be undone; duration prevents extrapolation from an instant; subsequent observation specifies review. Event type must also remain explicit, not hidden in narrative tone.","Occurrence time may be an interval or a report's claimed time; invent no precise timestamp. Observation time differs from occurrence time, and §11.3.2 additionally distinguishes model-entry time. Late material may revise today's understanding of the past, but cannot give past forecasts records unavailable then.","Affected objects are not automatically “everyone relevant.” A message reaching a department does not establish every member read it; changed quantity fields do not establish changed trust, authority, and relations. Reversibility also has multiple dimensions: replenishable resources do not mean transmitted information, elapsed deadlines, and damaged trust can be fully restored.","After verification supplies new material, append sources and correction relationships and revisit branches depending on the content. Later falsity of content does not erase the report's occurrence or actual effects; later truth does not erase its then-unverified status in earlier evaluations. None of this analysis independently authorizes real collection, experiments, or interventions."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Observed record","leftText":"Information obtained under specified observation rules.","rightTitle":"Complete actual state","rightText":"May contain unobserved fields, errors, and hidden changes."},{"leftTitle":"Plan approved","leftText":"A permission or decision has been established.","rightTitle":"Plan executed","rightText":"Still requires actual-action and outcome records."},{"leftTitle":"Report content false","leftText":"Corresponding counterevidence supports falsity; mere lack of support remains unverified or unresolved.","rightTitle":"Report did not occur","rightText":"The report and its propagation effects may still be real."},{"leftTitle":"Hypothetical event","leftText":"A conditional object stipulated by the analyst.","rightTitle":"Simulated event","rightText":"An internal update in a model run. Neither is a real-world observation."},{"leftTitle":"Recoverable resources","leftText":"A specified quantity can be replenished through later action.","rightTitle":"Completely reversible event","rightText":"Deadline, propagation, and relational consequences may be irrecoverable."}],"boundaries":["The five statuses are not a ladder automatically climbed with retelling count.","Check observational credibility under its contract. Report count cannot replace independent sources.","Plan publication, approval, resource preparation, execution, and effects are distinct events. Retain windows when precise dates are unavailable.","Actual unauthorized actions must still be recorded; simulating an action creates no execution authority.","Complete fields supply a checkable structure, not a guarantee that every fact is true or all required material is present."],"questions":["Starting with 10 items and an unverified report of 4 removed, why does this example output {6,10}, not 8?","May approval of a plan directly fill “executed”?","What do Toy-E1's 09:00, 10:00, and 10:05 each represent?","Do two forwards of the same message necessarily correspond to only one real event?","If report content is false, may its effects on beliefs and preparation still be studied?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-2/figures/section-11-2.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Submission, use, and approval in Maryland's administrative report","href":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Documents/JCRs/2023/eligibilityredetprocessJCRfinal4-24.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Submission, use, and approval in Maryland's administrative report","text":"Printed p.9 of Maryland's 2024 report separately records submission of a mitigation plan to CMS on September 13, 2023, use of the updated automatic-renewal system process beginning in November 2023, and CMS approval in February 2024. This section retains all three as distinct events and windows. Actual use rests on the report's explicit statement, not automatic inference from plan submission.","sourceLabel":"Maryland Department of Health, FY2024 fourth-quarter renewal review report, April 2024","sourceUrl":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Documents/JCRs/2023/eligibilityredetprocessJCRfinal4-24.pdf","sourceLocator":"Medicaid Mitigation of Ex Parte Renewals, printed p.9, measure 3 and the “updated ex parte systems process” paragraph after measure 5","boundary":"This lesson relies on an official retrospective administrative report, not firsthand system observation. Month-level times are not expanded into invented dates. The three times do not independently establish illegality or absent authority before approval, or all coverage, treatment, or health consequences. Only 2023–2024 historical material is explained.","href":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Documents/JCRs/2023/eligibilityredetprocessJCRfinal4-24.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.2","start_line":2357,"end_line":2370,"startLine":2357,"endLine":2370}],"outputId":"section-11-2","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["First identify the recorded object and its five-way status. An actual publication/report event and its content may have different factual statuses.","Complete the fourteen minimum record items and explicitly add the type; preserve unknown fields honestly.","Record occurrence, observation, and necessary model-entry times separately; also separate permissions, resources, and execution.","Limit eligible projections and evidential ceilings by type; simulated states are not actual observations.","Verify sources and disputes, distinguish forwarding from independent witnessing, and avoid applying an event repeatedly.","Append relationships for content corrections, updating dependent branches separately while preserving the report's own consequences.","Design later observations only within necessity, privacy, and authorization limits; analytical need creates no permission."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: five statuses for the same “decrease of 4 items”","text":"Suppose fictional initial inventory is 10 items with no other inflows or outflows. Correct direct counting confirms removal of 4, yielding 6. If only an unverified report says “4 removed,” and this example allows only truth or falsity with no removal when false, inventory belongs to {6,10}. If removal of 4 is planned for tomorrow, current inventory remains 10; only the conditional post-execution value is 6. Hypothetical removal yields conditional consequence 6; a simulator calculating 6 under the same rule produces only a model value. Identical numbers do not confer identical evidential status.","assumptions":"Inventory units, correct counting, no other changes, and only two report possibilities are explicit construction premises; no actual warehouse or experiment is involved.","boundary":"An unverified report cannot automatically yield mean 8 because no truth probabilities are supplied; simulated output 6 is not actual inventory 6."},{"title":"Teaching event card: separating claimed time from observation time","text":"Fictional ID=Toy-E1, type=reported; claimed occurrence day 1 at 09:00, report received at 10:00, model entry at 10:05; source=fictional team message Toy-S1; affected actor=fictional custodian A, circle=fictional warehouse group W; channel=received team message; direct change=message received, physical inventory awaiting verification; evidence status=report exists, content unverified; dispute=stocktake incomplete; competing explanations=actual removal or message error; reversibility=stock can be replenished, but propagated information cannot be presumed fully retractable; duration=removal and effect duration unknown; subsequent observation=check stocktake and withdrawal records within this example's permitted scope.","assumptions":"All IDs, people, times, and permission scopes are teaching constructions, never actual event cards or valid authorization documents.","boundary":"A completed card does not prove its content; the direct change cannot be written as an established inventory reduction of 4."},{"title":"Set construction: two forwards do not add two physical shocks","text":"A fictional original message about removal of 4 items is forwarded twice; all three materials refer to one candidate event. If eventually verified as one removal, inventory changes 10→6. Applying a reduction of 4 for every message yields −2, duplicating the shock and potentially violating inventory bounds. If two independent withdrawals actually occurred, create separate events and verify their times and quantities.","assumptions":"Message lineage and one withdrawal are stipulated here. Actual deduplication requires evidence; textual similarity alone cannot justify merging.","boundary":"Material counts differ from event counts, but deduplication needs cannot cancel actual repeated events."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Three people forwarding one notice equals three independent fact checks.","effect":"Source lineage must be checked; dependent forwards may establish propagation without generating independent witnesses.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A plan says submission in September, so actual system execution in November can follow directly from the plan.","effect":"Actual-use records are needed; date order alone is not execution evidence.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A report is later falsified, so preparation actions it caused earlier should also be deleted.","effect":"Content truth differs from actual consequences of the report's propagation; retain the latter when evidenced.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Starting with 10 items and an unverified report of 4 removed, why does this example output {6,10}, not 8?","answer":"Only two candidate states are given, without truth probabilities; 8 would secretly turn an unknown into an equally weighted average.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"May approval of a plan directly fill “executed”?","answer":"No. Approval is a permission or decision event; execution requires separate action records.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What do Toy-E1's 09:00, 10:00, and 10:05 each represent?","answer":"Respectively, the report's claimed occurrence time, receipt time, and model-entry time; a claimed time is not a verified occurrence time.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Do two forwards of the same message necessarily correspond to only one real event?","answer":"Counts alone cannot decide. This construction deduplicates because a shared lineage is explicit; reality requires checking candidate-event identity and may contain several similar events.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If report content is false, may its effects on beliefs and preparation still be studied?","answer":"Yes, but arrival, belief, and actual response each require material and cannot be presumed universally.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-2/figures/section-11-2.png","alt":"Observed: confirmed under an observation contract, still checking source and error; reported: reporting occurred, content not automatically verified; planned: expectations and preparations may change, but execution is not implied; hypothetical: declare conditions and purpose, not a real fact; simulated: model version and parameters, internal states only; each record: fourteen minimum fields plus type, without inventing unknowns; historical 2023-09-13: mitigation-plan submission, source administrative report; historical 2023-11: updated process begins use, month-level window; historical 2024-02: CMS approves the plan, with no illegality inferred from order. The upper two rows concern event statuses and recording responsibilities; the lower row shows three distinct events on page 9 of the Maryland report, with no arrows implying causation or permissions.","caption":"The upper two rows concern event statuses and recording responsibilities; the lower row shows three distinct events on page 9 of the Maryland report, with no arrows implying causation or permissions."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. Of nine cards, the first five are event types, the sixth contract fields, and the last three sourced historical dates—not a ladder directly upgrading plans into execution.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The same page explicitly distinguishes institutional events and times, making it suitable for checking non-equivalence among plan existence, actual execution, and approval; it provides no current eligibility or legal advice."},"openQuestions":["The administrative report does not supply all individual execution logs, arrival records, or health consequences for this lesson; those fields remain unchecked. No complete legal authorization chain for the plan is invented."],"taskId":"S-11-2","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2231–2244; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.2: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"time","title":"Historical research manuscript: original event sources, three times, and deduplication","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2269–2276","claim_support":["11.2: reference to separate occurrence/observation/model-entry times, late corrections, and deduplication of events sharing a source"],"limits":["Similar descriptions alone cannot justify merging events.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"authorization","title":"Historical research manuscript: original separate columns for facts and authorized actions","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Part XI introduction, Chinese source lines 2213–2216; §11.3, lines 2245–2260","claim_support":["11.2: an actual unauthorized action is still an event; u specifically means authorized and occurred, and simulation cannot supply permissions"],"limits":["No actual operation or legal adjudication is performed.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"maryland","title":"Maryland Medicaid FY2024 fourth-quarter renewal review report","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Documents/JCRs/2023/eligibilityredetprocessJCRfinal4-24.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"April 2024 report; cover letter 2024-05-14","locator":"Printed p. 9 (PDF p. 13), item 3 and the updated ex parte systems process passage after item 5","claim_support":["11.2: plan submitted 2023-09-13, updated process used from 2023-11, plan approved 2024-02"],"limits":["Based on a retrospective administrative report; no exact day is added, no effects for all individuals inferred, and no legal status assigned to preapproval conduct."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.2 Event Contract; Chinese source lines 2285–2298; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-2/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"5bf7fcbef78b3e0231b405d83c629b18ec6e0b8eac1c3c98077fdd607896558c","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.3":{"title":"11.3 Joint-State Update Equation","lead":"The update equation is a responsibility checklist: the states, events, actions, disturbances, assumptions, and history used at this step should each be traceable. Writing F does not discover the world's unique operating formula; putting unknowns inside symbols cannot pretend to explain them.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2299–2314; original anchor: section-11-3; short quotation, translated from the Chinese: “This equation only specifies recording responsibilities.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","The original joint state is Ω(t)=<A(t),C(t),R(t),M(t),Ψ(t),Q(t),E≤t,SP(t),W(t),K(t)>. A retains actors, C circles, R relations, M and Ψ material and experiential-meaning conditions respectively, Q channel constraints, E≤t events through the corresponding time, SP scale profiles, W evidence, and K identity criteria. It is a task-relevant representation, not omniscient access to every variable in the world.","The update is Ω(t+Δ)=F[Ω(t),e(t),u(t),ξ(t)|θ,h]. Δ is the declared progression interval; e is an event; u is an externally authorized action that actually occurred; ξ is an exogenous disturbance or unmodeled residual; θ is frozen mechanisms and parameters; h is a historical term. Each may have internal structure. The function symbol means only “the next step depends on these inputs”; actual rules, parameters, scope, and evidence cannot be omitted.","F is not claimed to be the unique true function. Different mechanisms may be equivalent on current material, and some parameters may have only intervals. Retain multiple candidates, ranges, or unknowns rather than disguising one computed output as the unique real consequence. Specify which terms are observable, inferred, or scenario assumptions only.","The definition of u is a recording convention; it cannot make unauthorized actions disappear from reality. Observed or reported violations, overreach, refusal to execute, or unauthorized proxy action enter e with corresponding event status, separately recording actors, consequences, and permission status. Violations not yet occurring belong only to hypothetical or simulated branches. Factual occurrence, normative judgment, and execution permission remain separate. An actual action referenced in several fields still applies its corresponding state effect only once.","ξ cannot become a universal black box for things one prefers not to explain. Preserve sources and scopes for identified exogenous disturbances; if it is only an unexplained residual, retain that status. Random-error models need grounds. Missing records, refusal to disclose, and unmodeled mechanisms cannot all be called zero-mean noise. Nor does writing h into a formula establish additional historical effects; current-carrier effects and G3 historical increments beyond the current representation still require separate evidence routes.","Each step's outputs distinguish at least four statuses: directly observed state differences, inferences supported by mechanism contracts, exploratory scenario values, and unexplained residuals. All may coexist in Ω without transferring evidential status to one another. A simulated future used as the next round's input retains its conditions and unknowns; it cannot be renamed current reality.","The parent section specifies update symbols and status responsibilities. Section 11.3.1 further separates actual states, observation rules, and identification sets, explaining when existing material suffices for a bounded question. Section 11.3.2 manages three times, late material, source corrections, and concurrent ordering. Together they constrain updating, not add automatic truth certification to F."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"The symbolic position F","leftText":"Specifies dependencies and recording responsibilities.","rightTitle":"The actual mechanism has been found","rightText":"Still requires concrete rules, discriminating evidence, and an applicability domain."},{"leftTitle":"u","leftText":"An externally authorized action that actually occurred.","rightTitle":"Actual unauthorized actions within e","rightText":"May still change reality and are registered as observed/reported."},{"leftTitle":"Unmodeled residual","leftText":"A difference left unexplained by the current account.","rightTitle":"Random noise","rightText":"Has a corresponding probabilistic meaning only under an appropriate error model."},{"leftTitle":"Historical input h","leftText":"A record position whose content and use must be specified.","rightTitle":"Historical increment established","rightText":"Separately requires incremental evidence beyond the current representation; symbols do not create it."},{"leftTitle":"Four statuses of state differences","leftText":"Observed, supported inference, scenario value, and residual, each with a separate trail.","rightTitle":"Same snapshot, same truth status","rightText":"Coexistence in Ω does not give them equal evidential status."}],"boundaries":["Formal completeness is not completeness of the world; a computable function does not prove a correct mechanism.","Do not force unknown parameters into point values or upgrade simulated continuations into real facts.","Keep factual records of actual unauthorized events without using analysis to carry out the action.","If a total effect has been independently identified, retain its bounded result; lack of unique identification of the internal F does not erase it.","ξ and h must not absorb every counterexample and make the model incapable of failure."],"questions":["In the construction s0=5, e=−2, u=1, ξ∈{0,−1}, what is the output's status?","If F_A and F_B both fit 1→2, can the next step be uniquely predicted?","Where does an observed unauthorized action enter this section's notation?","Does adding historical term h mean G3 is supported?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/figures/section-11-3.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.3","start_line":2371,"end_line":2386,"startLine":2371,"endLine":2386}],"outputId":"section-11-3","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Register Ω's ten components individually, with task, scale, evidence, and K; state whether unlisted fields are irrelevant, omitted, or unknown.","Write the F and θ actually used, specifying Δ, input sources, ranges, and failure conditions.","Distinguish e and u by definition. Actual unauthorized actions enter events rather than being deleted; repeated references do not apply them repeatedly.","Declare ξ and h statuses and the effects they can support. Unknowns do not default to randomness, nor history to G3 support.","Preserve each step's observed, inferred, scenario, and residual state differences separately.","Retain multiple Fs or output ranges where needed, constrained by identification sets and event-continuation rules."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: preserving event status and output ranges within one update equation","text":"Let fictional resources s0=5 units, with confirmed but unauthorized consumption e=−2 and authorized replenishment that actually arrives u=+1. Freeze the local rule s1=s0+e+u+ξ, explicitly allowing only two unknown extra-loss scenarios ξ∈{0,−1}, hence s1∈{4,3}. If extra loss is unverified, this output range is a conditional model result, not observed resources of 3. Nor may e be deleted because consumption was unauthorized, yielding 6 or 5.","assumptions":"Units match, events are counted once, and no other inflows/outflows exist. h has no separate effect in this local model; the ξ set is a teaching assumption, not an estimated real distribution.","boundary":"The example does not treat all events as linearly additive or authorize actual resource transfers."},{"title":"Logical construction: identical observations can still admit different Fs","text":"Suppose only one transition x0=1, x1=2 is observed. Both F_A(x)=x+1 and F_B(x)=2x fit it, but next yield 3 and 4 respectively. Without additional conditions, calling either the “unique true F” exceeds the material. Retain both candidates and seek subsequent records capable of distinguishing them.","assumptions":"The two rules are only a finite candidate set; actual models may be more numerous. All dynamics have not been estimated from one datum.","boundary":"Identical current fit guarantees neither identical next steps nor equal one-half real-world probabilities for the models."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Ω and F are fully written, so the model has read every state in the world.","effect":"Complete symbols specify only a representational interface; they do not guarantee complete observations, mechanisms, or hidden variables.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The action in e was unauthorized, so deleting it is more normatively appropriate.","effect":"Normative limits do not permit deleting confirmed facts; otherwise later resource and risk judgments become distorted.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Calling all deviations ξ and all persistence h validates the model.","effect":"Naming provides neither error-model nor historical-identification evidence; preserve counterexamples and unknowns.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"In the construction s0=5, e=−2, u=1, ξ∈{0,−1}, what is the output's status?","answer":"A conditional model range {4,3}, not confirmed actual inventory without additional observation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If F_A and F_B both fit 1→2, can the next step be uniquely predicted?","answer":"No. The two candidates give 3 and 4; preserve branches or obtain distinguishing material.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Where does an observed unauthorized action enter this section's notation?","answer":"It enters e as an observed/reported event, with permission status independently recorded; it does not enter u, defined as authorized and occurred.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does adding historical term h mean G3 is supported?","answer":"No. Check current carriers and additional historical increments separately; symbols cannot generate truth.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/figures/section-11-3.png","alt":"Joint state Ω: actors, circles, conditions, and so on, including evidence and K; explicit rule F|θ,h: actual mechanisms and parameters checked separately, unknowns retained; subsequent Ω: observation / inference / scenario / residual, four noninterchangeable statuses; constructed initial s0=5, e=−2 actual unauthorized consumption, u=+1 authorized and arrived; constructed disturbance range ξ∈{0,−1}, without real-world probability assignments; constructed consequence s1∈{4,3}, not an actual observation. The original equation specifies responsibilities for each input; the lower row is an explicit additive teaching model, where actual unauthorized consumption remains in the factual record.","caption":"The original equation specifies responsibilities for each input; the lower row is an explicit additive teaching model, where actual unauthorized consumption remains in the factual record."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. Six cards depict the general recording interface and a local model separately, avoiding treatment of scenario ranges as measured results.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit formally explains an update equation and input statuses. Explicit constructions check non-implications; historical cases cannot prove a unique F exists. Specific observation and event questions appear in the two subsections."},"openQuestions":["Actual F, θ, ξ ranges, and h effects each require material; this mathematical construction estimates no real system parameters."],"taskId":"S-11-3","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.3: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.3.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.3.2: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"history","title":"Historical research manuscript: original current carriers and additional historical increments","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source, §4.5, lines 620—640","claim_support":["11.3: h does not automatically acquire historical-increment truth; current-carrier and G3 routes remain separate"],"limits":["A symbolic position cannot replace real-world identification.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"choice","title":"Historical research manuscript: original sufficient answers and feasible choices","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§12.10.1–12.10.2, Chinese source lines 2601–2612","claim_support":["11.3.1–11.3.2: same answers to questions differ from action feasibility; shared resources affect joint feasibility"],"limits":["Identification needs create neither observation nor execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow","title":"Snow 1855 on death times and registration times","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Printed pp. 40–41, account of the Broad Street outbreak and registration the following week","claim_support":["11.3.2: some deaths in September's first two days were not registered until the next week, illustrating different event and record clocks"],"limits":["Individual model-entry times were not obtained; death dates are not infection dates, and no transmission parameters are estimated from this."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.3 Joint-State Update Equation; Chinese source lines 2299–2314; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"628789f79173830397ff7dba95ea97b57d83eac37a67447db7e043a1cfb9a273","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.3.1":{"title":"11.3.1 States, Observations, and Identification Sets","lead":"We obtain records, not the world itself. The key is not whether every hidden variable can be guessed, but whether all situations still compatible with the records already give the same answer to the current question.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2315–2322; source anchor section-11-3-1; short quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘The current evidence is already sufficient for that question.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical draft.","Let z(t) denote the actual state to be inferred and o(t) the obtained record, with observation rule o(t)=H[z(t),m(t),ε(t)]. H describes record generation; m includes who can enter the record, when measurement occurs, and which fields are protected from disclosure; ε is used only with a corresponding error model. Selection, protective missingness, and unknown mechanisms cannot all be lumped into random measurement error.","Numerical data may use additive error under appropriate conditions, while text and categories follow their record-generation rules. If observation coverage expands from half to all, record counts may rise while object counts remain unchanged; changed coding rules may also alter category frequencies. Ω must distinguish object changes from changes in how we observe them.","Given an explicit model and permitted errors, Z(o) is the set of states still compatible with the record. It is neither an unspecified confidence interval nor automatically an equal-probability distribution over states. Changing the model or error range changes the set; reported answers must retain this conditional status.","For task question q, if q takes the same value on every state in Z(o), the evidence suffices to answer this bounded question even if many components of z remain unidentified. If values differ, retain the range, identifiable direction, or genuinely unknown parts. Direction cannot be assumed either: if both positive and negative values remain compatible, do not force a direction merely to ‘say something.’","This is not ‘no difference found in the sample means everything is identical.’ Finite samples, measurement noise, and model limits may make differences hard to detect. Claiming a common answer across the set requires proving its range under model constraints or appropriate sufficiency, equivalence, and sensitivity support. Nonsignificance cannot directly become zero effect or sufficient identification.","The next observation should prioritize state differences that would change the current answer or action comparison. If the task asks only for a total, knowing exact container proportions may be unnecessary; if it asks whether both endpoints have a minimum reserve, distribution matters. The same evidence's value changes with the question; no variable table is eternally most complete and necessary.","Observation design remains constrained by purpose, necessity, privacy, and protection. Being able to identify a state does not confer authority to collect corresponding personal information. Without filling every latent variable, there may already be a commonly acceptable action; or the answer may be sufficient while no feasible action exists. Sufficient conditions for action are separately addressed in §12.10.1; authorization cannot be silently completed here."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"z: actual state","leftText":"The world state the task seeks to infer.","rightTitle":"o: observational record","rightText":"Generated by rules, selection, timing, and error conditions."},{"leftTitle":"The question is identified","leftText":"q gives the same answer across compatible states.","rightTitle":"The full state is recovered","rightText":"Requires uniqueness of all relevant hidden variables, often unnecessary for the task."},{"leftTitle":"Set-valued range","leftText":"The possible answer range under an explicit model.","rightTitle":"Probability or confidence","rightText":"Additionally requires a corresponding probability model and inferential procedure."},{"leftTitle":"No difference detected","leftText":"Limited evidence reveals no difference.","rightTitle":"Sufficiency or equivalence established","rightText":"Requires corresponding rules, tolerance, and sensitivity; there is no automatic upgrade."},{"leftTitle":"An observation is informative","leftText":"It may narrow current branches.","rightTitle":"Authority to observe","rightText":"Also requires purpose, protection, and authority conditions."}],"boundaries":["Unlisted states may exist beyond the model's domain; set-based answers retain modeling boundaries.","Text, categories, refusal to disclose, and selective records do not all admit additive noise.","An identifiable total and unidentified distribution can coexist without canceling each other.","If compatible answers include positive and negative directions, direction is also unknown.","‘Wanting to recover the entire state’ does not justify demanding unnecessary or unauthorized information."],"questions":["Given only nonnegative integers x+y=10, how many elements does Z contain, and what can be said about the total and the difference's direction?","Why does the Boolean check 3≤x≤7 suffice to determine whether both containers have at least 3?","If coverage rises from half to all while the true count stays 20, why does the record change from 10 to 20?","Does uniquely identifying a state justify collecting all personal information?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/figures/section-11-3-1.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.3.1","start_line":2387,"end_line":2394,"startLine":2387,"endLine":2394}],"outputId":"section-11-3-1","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Declare z, H, m, and the applicable error model, separating actual state from record generation.","Construct Z(o) under these conditions, retaining unidentified components without assuming probabilities.","Write the current question as q(z), and calculate or establish its range across the entire compatible set.","Give a bounded answer when all agree; otherwise retain ranges/directions/unknowns. Failure to detect a finite-sample difference is not proof of identical answers.","Select the minimal observation that changes the answer or option comparison, rather than increasing precision indiscriminately.","Check observational purpose, risk, and authority; information needs do not generate permission to collect."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: a known total with an unknown distribution","text":"Two fictional containers hold nonnegative integer amounts x and y, with correct total record o=x+y=10 and no other information. Z(o)={(0,10),(1,9),…,(10,0)} has 11 points. The total question q1=x+y is always 10 and already answerable; first-container quantity q2=x ranges from 0 to 10; difference q3=x−y ranges from −10 to 10 with both signs, so direction is unknown. Knowing x is unnecessary for recognizing total 10.","assumptions":"No omissions, error-free total measurement, exactly two containers, and fixed integer units are all teaching assumptions.","boundary":"If the real total itself has measurement error, enlarge the compatible set; constant 10 cannot carry over."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: choose less observation according to the question","text":"Continuing the example, change the task to ‘does each container hold at least 3 units?’ Since y=10−x, this is equivalent to 3≤x≤7. If a permitted check in this example reliably returns the Boolean ‘is x in [3,7]?’ it answers the task without measuring x exactly. If the task changes to how long each container will last, that Boolean information may be insufficient.","assumptions":"Error-free observation and permission for the check are constructed premises, not authority for real data collection; actual equipment costs are not compared.","boundary":"Information sufficiency is question-relative; the Boolean record cannot be called sufficient for every later task."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: the observation rule changes while the object does not","text":"Let true object count z stay at 20, with model recording rule o=mz. Coverage m changes from 1/2 to 1 without other error, so the record changes 10→20 while the true count stays 20. If only o=10 is observed and m∈{1/2,1} is known, z may be 20 or 10. Both growing records and identical records require interpretation through coverage rules.","assumptions":"The proportion is this example's exact record-generation rule; actual random sample counts are not equated to their expectations.","boundary":"This does not rule out actual growth; it only refutes the claim that record changes necessarily equal object changes."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The total is 10 but the distribution is unknown, so no question can be answered.","effect":"The total question has the same answer on all 11 states; a bounded answer can hold.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The sample difference is nonsignificant, so compatible mechanisms are identical for every question.","effect":"Nondetection does not establish a common answer across the set; sufficient grounds specific to the question and tolerance are required.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Record counts doubled, so the true object count must have doubled.","effect":"Changing coverage rules alone produces the same record pattern in this construction.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Given only nonnegative integers x+y=10, how many elements does Z contain, and what can be said about the total and the difference's direction?","answer":"11 elements. Total 10 is identified; the difference ranges from −10 to 10, with unknown direction.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does the Boolean check 3≤x≤7 suffice to determine whether both containers have at least 3?","answer":"Given y=10−x, combining both conditions yields exactly that interval; exact x is unnecessary for this task.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If coverage rises from half to all while the true count stays 20, why does the record change from 10 to 20?","answer":"The record-generation rule changes; the object need not grow.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does uniquely identifying a state justify collecting all personal information?","answer":"No. Information needs do not confer collection authority, and the current task may require only a bounded distinction.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/figures/section-11-3-1.png","alt":"Observation generation: o=H[z,m,ε], with a grounded error model; compatible-state set Z(o): follows the model and error range, not an automatic probability distribution; current question q: one answer across the full set → bounded sufficiency, different answers → range or unknown; teaching example: x+y=10, nonnegative integers, 11 states, no unique full state; total question: 10 at every point, without recovering the distribution; difference question: x−y∈[−10,10], with sign still unknown. The bottom row is an explicit container construction; an identified total and unknown distribution can coexist, and collection needs create no authority.","caption":"The bottom row is an explicit container construction; an identified total and unknown distribution can coexist, and collection needs create no authority."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not yet been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a design proposal for later. Six cards separate observation generation, compatible sets, and task answers, without assigning equal probabilities to unknown states.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section formally argues about observation mappings and identified sets. Three explicit constructions directly test sufficiency for different questions; real cases lacking complete observation contracts are not forced into service as proofs of set identification."},"openQuestions":["A real Z(o) requires actual H, selection processes, and error ranges; this lesson has no complete measurement model for a specific object."],"taskId":"S-11-3","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.3: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.3.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.3.2: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"history","title":"Historical research manuscript: original current carriers and additional historical increments","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source, §4.5, lines 620—640","claim_support":["11.3: h does not automatically acquire historical-increment truth; current-carrier and G3 routes remain separate"],"limits":["A symbolic position cannot replace real-world identification.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"choice","title":"Historical research manuscript: original sufficient answers and feasible choices","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§12.10.1–12.10.2, Chinese source lines 2601–2612","claim_support":["11.3.1–11.3.2: same answers to questions differ from action feasibility; shared resources affect joint feasibility"],"limits":["Identification needs create neither observation nor execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow","title":"Snow 1855 on death times and registration times","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Printed pp. 40–41, account of the Broad Street outbreak and registration the following week","claim_support":["11.3.2: some deaths in September's first two days were not registered until the next week, illustrating different event and record clocks"],"limits":["Individual model-entry times were not obtained; death dates are not infection dates, and no transmission parameters are estimated from this."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.3.1 States, observations, and identified sets; Chinese source lines 2315–2322; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"1ebd849c1ef1586a679d7eb2efee9d11a43dc94865cfc500198c3b407aefe60f","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.3.2":{"title":"11.3.2 Event handling and state continuation","lead":"A new message may mean the world just changed, or that today we learned what happened yesterday. An updating system must separate those cases while handling corrections, repeated relays, and order conflicts, without replacing actual event order with message-arrival order.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2323–2330; source anchor section-11-3-2; short quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘Both kinds of updating can jointly change Ω.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical draft.","Retain three times for every input: event occurrence, record acquisition, and model entry. Occurrence may be known only as a window or a reported claim; do not invent exact seconds. Late evidence can change current judgments about the past, but the information available then cannot be rewritten, or replay will masquerade as prediction made at the time.","For corrected sources, append the correction relationship and recalculate states and paths depending on that source. Dependencies determine recalculation scope; unrelated conclusions are not all revoked. Retain old versions so the basis of the original judgment and where it failed remain visible.","Multiple items may relay the same event. Retain multiple sources with one candidate event identity to avoid applying the shock repeatedly. Similar wording may also describe distinct events; similar names alone cannot merge them. The times, objects, sources, and event identities supporting deduplication may themselves remain unchecked.","Evidence updating revises knowledge of z(t); mechanism advancement estimates changes from time t onward. If a credible record that ‘work stopped yesterday’ arrives today, today's events are learning and updating model evidence; the stoppage belongs to yesterday. Rerunning a model to obtain ‘work may stop’ produces only new simulation material, not an actual work-stoppage event.","Both operations may change Ω, but for different reasons. More accurate knowledge of yesterday's inventory is not another warehouse delivery today; projecting future inventory by an explicit rule is not seeing the future. The same state field can therefore carry both an object time and a knowledge version.","Concurrent events can be combined in arbitrary order only if operations commute, shared resources do not conflict, and neither changes the other's conditions. Check commutation on the relevant complete state and permitted domain; identical final totals alone do not establish identical authority, resource occupation, or intermediate harm.","When two actions compete for capacity, overwrite the same record, or change each other's available actions, retain order branches or explicit concurrency rules. Nearby timestamps do not establish simultaneity, and an executor returning first does not establish earlier real occurrence; transmission and processing delays may differ. If order is unidentified, output the corresponding result set rather than secretly choosing an order for reproducibility."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Evidence update","leftText":"Revising judgments about past or current states today.","rightTitle":"Mechanism advancement","rightText":"Estimating subsequent changes from a state according to rules."},{"leftTitle":"Message-arrival order","leftText":"Affected by transmission and processing delays.","rightTitle":"Actual causal order","rightText":"Requires event-time and channel evidence; neither order can be directly inferred from the other."},{"leftTitle":"Multiple sources","leftText":"Multiple records or relays.","rightTitle":"Multiple events","rightText":"Distinguishable actual occurrences, which may be interdependent; check event identity and deduplication."},{"leftTitle":"A final field is identical","leftText":"Local results happen to agree.","rightTitle":"Complete operations may be combined arbitrarily","rightText":"Also requires no conflicts in resources, conditions, or other key states."},{"leftTitle":"Correct dependent branches","leftText":"Repair relevant judgments.","rightTitle":"Every conclusion becomes invalid","rightText":"Local results independent of the erroneous source remain."}],"boundaries":["Late facts must not be fed back into old prediction information sets; replay is valuable but has a different status.","Regenerating a model narrative alone is not a new real event or observation.","Deduplication does not delete sources; retain source lineage and grounds for merging. Similar events may genuinely occur more than once.","Executor completion order cannot be treated as real causal order.","Arbitrary combination is conditionally permissible, not the default for every near-simultaneous event."],"questions":["Starting at 1 with f=x+1 and g=2x, what do the two orders produce?","If a credible record of yesterday's work stoppage arrives today, which day contains the evidence update and which the real event?","Why can two tasks with different completion fields still not be directly merged in parallel?","How is recalculation scope determined after a source correction?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/figures/section-11-3-2.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Snow records deaths and registration at different times","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Snow records deaths and registration at different times","text":"Snow's 1855 book explains that some deaths in the relevant area during the first two days of September 1854 were not registered until the following week. Death records from hospitals and other locations also required distinctions in data scope. This section uses only the first fact to show that actual event time and record-creation time cannot be collapsed into one clock.","sourceLabel":"John Snow, 1855 second edition","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Original printed pp.40–41, the paragraph containing ‘not registered till the week following’ in the Broad Street outbreak discussion","boundary":"This is Snow's retrospective reconstruction of registration delays. This lesson lacks model-entry times for individual death records, does not equate death dates with infection dates, and does not calculate transmission parameters for the entire epidemic.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.3.2","start_line":2395,"end_line":2402,"startLine":2395,"endLine":2402}],"outputId":"section-11-3-2","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["For every input, distinguish occurrence, acquisition, and model-entry times, including precision and evidence.","Treat new evidence as knowledge updating, register real events at their object time, and separately retain conditional status for model advancement.","Append source corrections and recalculate along dependencies, retaining independent local results and old versions.","Check candidate event identity for deduplication, neither applying repeated shocks nor casually merging similar events.","For concurrency, check commutation, resources, and effects on conditions, specifying the permitted domain and intermediate states.","When arbitrary combination is impossible, retain order or concurrency branches and output uncertainty ranges and discriminating evidence."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: operation order changes the answer","text":"A fictional state x starts at 1, with f(x)=x+1 and g(x)=2x. Applying f then g gives g(f(1))=4; applying g then f gives f(g(1))=3, so the operations do not commute. If evidence establishes only that both occurred in overlapping windows with unknown order, the final candidates are {3,4}; whichever tool message returns first cannot select the answer.","assumptions":"Operation definitions, absence of other updates, and overlapping windows are teaching assumptions; no real probabilities for the two orders are given.","boundary":"Coincidence at a particular initial value or agreement in one field also cannot generally establish commutation."},{"title":"Teaching construction: different fields may still compete for one resource","text":"Fictional tasks A and B each need one on-duty worker continuously for one hour and must finish within the same hour. Only one worker is available, cannot work in parallel, and neither task can be split. Their completion flags occupy different fields and appear independently updatable to complete; once shared capacity is included, both cannot finish within the window. Retain branches prioritizing A or B, or another actually feasible schedule.","assumptions":"The window, indivisibility, inability to parallelize, and capacity are constructed premises; no real staffing arrangement or authorization exists.","boundary":"Different fields do not guarantee independent resources; adding staff next hour also cannot restore a completion window already missed."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a local source correction does not erase independent records","text":"A fictional inventory is 5. Report E1, ‘2 were taken yesterday,’ produces knowledge version V1 with inventory 3 and subsequent predictions based on it. An independent system has its own reliable temperature record of 20. Today E1 is verified as erroneous; append correction V2, restore the inventory estimate to 5, and recalculate dependent projections. Temperature 20 is not revoked by the unrelated E1 correction. Updating the model today does not physically add 2 items back to the world.","assumptions":"The fictional evidence explicitly specifies E1's error, no other inventory changes, and temperature evidence independent of E1. Real dependencies require checking; different names alone do not establish independence.","boundary":"Correcting knowledge is not new physical replenishment. If the original removal was real and items were later returned, record a separate real event."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Two operations have close timestamps, so they may be processed arbitrarily in return order.","effect":"Commutativity, resources, and conditions require checking; the f/g construction gives different consequences.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Two news reports say work stopped yesterday, so the model should apply two stoppages.","effect":"First check whether they concern the same candidate event; more material does not mean more real occurrences.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Correcting an inventory message requires withdrawing an independent temperature record.","effect":"Recomputation follows dependencies; independent local facts should not be collaterally deleted.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Starting at 1 with f=x+1 and g=2x, what do the two orders produce?","answer":"f then g gives 4, while g then f gives 3; unknown order should therefore preserve {3,4}.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a credible record of yesterday's work stoppage arrives today, which day contains the evidence update and which the real event?","answer":"Evidence is obtained today; the stoppage is registered as yesterday's event, with model entry possibly at another time.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can two tasks with different completion fields still not be directly merged in parallel?","answer":"They may share capacity or change one another's conditions; examine the complete task constraints.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How is recalculation scope determined after a source correction?","answer":"Recompute states and paths actually supported by that source, retaining independent results and old versions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/figures/section-11-3-2.png","alt":"Event occurrence time: perhaps known only as a window, not acquisition time; record acquisition time: late material updates knowledge without changing the old information set; model-entry time: versions and correction relationships, with local dependency recomputation; teaching f then g: 1→2→4, g(f(1))=4; teaching g then f: 1→2→3, f(g(1))=3; unknown order: retain {3,4}, as tool-return order is insufficient. f=x+1 and g=2x are mathematical constructions; the three times imply neither fixed delays nor strictly increasing order.","caption":"f=x+1 and g=2x are mathematical constructions; the three times imply neither fixed delays nor strictly increasing order."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Six cards separate evidence clocks from operation order; no unique causal sequence is drawn without evidence.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The same primary historical material explicitly distinguishes death occurrence from registration time, illustrating late-arriving material. This section adds the record-clock perspective rather than the effect of handle removal."},"openQuestions":["Actual event merging and concurrent order require specific logs, sources, and resource material; the historical case lacks each item's model-entry time, so no complete three-time table may be fabricated."],"taskId":"S-11-3","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.3: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.3.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.3.2: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"history","title":"Historical research manuscript: original current carriers and additional historical increments","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source, §4.5, lines 620—640","claim_support":["11.3: h does not automatically acquire historical-increment truth; current-carrier and G3 routes remain separate"],"limits":["A symbolic position cannot replace real-world identification.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"choice","title":"Historical research manuscript: original sufficient answers and feasible choices","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§12.10.1–12.10.2, Chinese source lines 2601–2612","claim_support":["11.3.1–11.3.2: same answers to questions differ from action feasibility; shared resources affect joint feasibility"],"limits":["Identification needs create neither observation nor execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow","title":"Snow 1855 on death times and registration times","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Printed pp. 40–41, account of the Broad Street outbreak and registration the following week","claim_support":["11.3.2: some deaths in September's first two days were not registered until the next week, illustrating different event and record clocks"],"limits":["Individual model-entry times were not obtained; death dates are not infection dates, and no transmission parameters are estimated from this."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.3.2 Event handling and state continuity; Chinese source lines 2323–2330; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"47d2c50d1301ca763b0cdc6f73ce9be46e4f2d8268f63e6c2dd4d5bdfda49282","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4":{"title":"11.4 Nine-step projection loop","lead":"The nine steps connect a dynamic analysis: establish its starting point, explain how events change conditions, and let real outcomes correct earlier judgments. They do not guarantee that reality follows the diagram or require filling nine boxes with invented knowledge.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2331–2332; source anchor section-11-4; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “Nine-step projection loop.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","This parent section contains only a navigational heading; substantive requirements lie in nine subsections. Step one freezes the starting point; two checks actors and circles; three separates M and Ψ by task; four declares clocks and channel constraints. These prepare a checkable starting point and grounds for propagation, not replaceable by an attractive relationship diagram.","Step five registers actual status of events or actions; six analyzes changes along supported channels; seven preserves branches at genuine uncertainties; eight records signals, residuals, and candidates; nine adds write-back from later actual outcomes. Each output becomes a bounded premise for later judgments; completing the procedure does not make false premises true.","A closed loop does not feed expected outcomes back as starting evidence. Save the old run, then incorporate new material in a new version; otherwise what was said at the time and comparison with a simple baseline cannot be recovered. Actual outcomes may support local paths without automatically certifying every mechanism.","These nine steps organize event projection. The book's XK0–XK11 is a broader analytical process, IG's seven gates address thresholds such as individual generation, and dynamic eligibility has its own checks. Counts cannot be merged merely because they are called steps, nor can finishing nine steps imply passing every gate.","The explanations use an explicitly fictional repair queue throughout: 6 pending items at 08:00, originally 2 arrivals and 3 processed per hour. Personnel, events, and later readings are teaching stipulations, not a business investigation. Sharing the starting point across subsections compares responsibilities; it does not turn one construction into several empirical cases. Apollo 13 material is separately inserted as bounded historical evidence, without claiming participants used Xikari or empirical superiority of the nine steps."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Nine-step loop","leftText":"The dynamic-analysis working sequence specified here.","rightTitle":"Nine steps completed means correct","rightText":"No such guarantee: evidence may be missing, mechanisms wrong, or boundaries exceeded."},{"leftTitle":"Write-back","leftText":"New records preserving the starting point and old predictions.","rightTitle":"Backward leakage","rightText":"Presenting later-known information as known at the earlier time."},{"leftTitle":"Construction spanning sections","leftText":"The same fictional system illustrates different responsibilities.","rightTitle":"Independent empirical replication","rightText":"Requires real and correspondingly independent observations."}],"boundaries":["The source parent section contains no separate empirical theorem; do not fabricate a proof for it.","The nine steps may yield bounded scenarios, unknowns, or stopping outcomes; neither point predictions nor real actions are mandatory."],"questions":["Why cannot 4 and 8 be averaged into prediction 6 in this example?","How does the parent section work with the nine subsections?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4","start_line":2403,"end_line":2404,"startLine":2403,"endLine":2404}],"outputId":"section-11-4","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["First check the starting point, objects, channels, and time/resource constraints, avoiding progression on unsettled premises.","Register events as facts or proposals, matching propagation edges to sources individually.","Retain branches that change outcomes and specify signals distinguishing them.","Append actual outcomes after the old run, compare deviations and baselines, and decide local revisions."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a complete procedure can still yield unknown","text":"If the repair queue's 08:00 state and arrival rate are known but actual processing is only known to be either 1 or 3 items/hour, the queue after two hours is respectively 8 or 4. The nine steps can explicitly preserve both branches and arrange a capacity check; procedural completeness does not automatically make the answer 6.","assumptions":"Each hour counts arrivals before processing; no losses or other actions; processing capacity stays fixed over the two hours. All are constructed.","boundary":"Two branches do not imply 50% probability each; midpoint 6 is not established as a possible terminal value in this model."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Once all nine steps contain text, the system has eliminated unknowns.","effect":"Formal completeness is not sufficient discriminating evidence; both branches remain.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why cannot 4 and 8 be averaged into prediction 6 in this example?","answer":"No branch probabilities or grounds for the midpoint being feasible exist; retain conditional branches.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How does the parent section work with the nine subsections?","answer":"The parent supplies sequence; subsections separately specify responsibilities for freezing, objects, channels, clocks, inputs, propagation, branching, signals, and write-back.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4.png","alt":"1 Freeze starting point; 2 Check actors and circles; 3 Separate M and Ψ; 4 Clocks and constraints; 5 Event/action status; 6 Supported propagation; 7 Conditional branching; 8 Signals and residuals; 9 Append actual outcomes. Read by number; crossing an arrow guarantees no sufficiency of evidence.","caption":"Read by number; crossing an arrow guarantees no sufficiency of evidence."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Nine cards provide sequential navigation only; clicking cards could become future page navigation, but this is not currently interactive software.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The parent contains only a navigational heading, not an independent empirical claim; historical evidence belongs in applicable subsections rather than repeated narrative accumulation."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4 Nine-step projection loop; Chinese source lines 2331–2332; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"9e5565599bbdbbb480c418fb2acebe0e122c4799fc67a15ea1184e6e8e0eaeb3","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.1":{"title":"11.4.1 Step one: freeze the current state","lead":"Freezing is a dated snapshot of the analytical starting point, preserving what was known, unknown, and which model version was used. It does not require the world to stop changing.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2333–2336; source anchor section-11-4-1; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “Information acquired after the cutoff cannot be fed backward into the starting point.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Evidence cutoff means “the latest acquisition time of material allowed in this run,” not that all described events occurred then. A record of yesterday's event received today becomes knowledge usable only today; event, acquisition, and model-entry clocks remain separate under 11.3.2.","A snapshot specifies at least objects, task, variables, values and their status, unknowns, disputes, sources, and model version. Equal values may differ in status: measured inventory 6 and inventory stipulated as 6 for projection are not the same evidence. Disputes are not blanks that must vanish; two compatible states may remain.","Freezing the model includes actual rules and key parameters, not just a filename. If a queue originally assumes 3 processed per hour and later 1, specify whether knowledge, actual capacity, or only a new scenario changed. Otherwise a seemingly continuous curve may splice different models.","Late evidence can of course be used, but in a new run version. The old version evaluates judgments under information then available; the new version serves the current task. Both may coexist. Reconstructing an accident from later evidence is retrospective explanation, not a relabeled preaccident prediction.","Freezing enables comparison, not guaranteed starting accuracy. If material is wrong, append corrections and recompute dependencies; “already frozen” cannot justify refusing correction, nor may the corrected starting point replace old records to erase the original error."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Freeze a knowledge snapshot","leftText":"Bounds the information used in this projection.","rightTitle":"Freeze the world","rightText":"Reality may continue changing."},{"leftTitle":"Prediction made at the time","leftText":"Uses only pre-cutoff material.","rightTitle":"Retrospective reconstruction","rightText":"May use later evidence, but must be relabeled accordingly."},{"leftTitle":"Unknown","leftText":"Insufficient material to assign a value.","rightTitle":"Zero or did not occur","rightText":"Needs corresponding observation; it does not come from a blank."}],"boundaries":["Complete snapshot fields do not imply error-free observations.","Later discovery of wrong old inputs does not prevent a corrected version despite freezing.","Do not replace old predictions with hindsight evidence to inflate accuracy."],"questions":["Why must V1 and V2 both be retained?","If inventory 6 proves a recording error after freezing, may it no longer be corrected?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Apollo 13: a fault not yet understood at the time","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Apollo 13: a fault not yet understood at the time","text":"When the 1970 Apollo 13 accident first occurred, its cause and severity were unknown. The report then describes ground personnel progressively checking electrical and oxygen readings. This section focuses on the temporal development of knowledge: retrospective findings cannot be inserted into the first alarm moment.","sourceLabel":"NASA，Report of Apollo 13 Review Board，1970-06-15","sourceUrl":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","sourceLocator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46; PDF pp.153,155, Understanding the Problem","boundary":"Supports only incomplete initial understanding and subsequent checking. This lesson has no complete second-by-second information sets and did not reconstruct prediction accuracy at the time.","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.1","start_line":2405,"end_line":2408,"startLine":2405,"endLine":2408}],"outputId":"section-11-4-1","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Specify task, object, and evidence cutoff, preserving actual acquisition times item by item.","Register variable and unknown status, sources, and disputes without zero-filling missing values.","Freeze rules, parameters, and versions so derivational responsibility for the same inputs is traceable.","Create a new run for later material while keeping the old one traceable; corrections propagate along dependencies."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a late fault notice","text":"At 08:00 freeze V1: 6 pending repairs, arrivals 2/hour, processing 3/hour. Under B_next_hour=max(0,B+arrivals−processing), 09:00 projects to 5. At 08:30 a credible notice reports that the machine slowed to 1/hour at 07:50. Create V2 and recalculate 08:00–09:00 as 7. V1 derives from material available then; V2 is a retrospective correction incorporating late information, not proof that 7 was predicted at 08:00.","assumptions":"Fault time, capacity change, and notice credibility are fictional assumptions; no real machine is involved.","boundary":"V2 does not mean the machine slowed again at 08:30; do not count the event twice."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Since the fault is later known to have occurred at 07:50, it can be inserted into material known at 08:00.","effect":"Earlier occurrence does not mean earlier knowledge; preserve knowledge timing.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why must V1 and V2 both be retained?","answer":"V1 answers how judgment was made then; V2 answers how new material revises it. Deleting V1 destroys grounds for evaluation and correction.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If inventory 6 proves a recording error after freezing, may it no longer be corrected?","answer":"Append a correction and create a new version, without overwriting the old run or treating the correction as a new inventory arrival.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-1.png","alt":"V1 | known at 08:00: 6 pending, processing 3/hour, projecting 5 at 09:00; new notice at 08:30: slowdown reportedly at 07:50, event time ≠ knowledge time; V2 | knowledge correction: processing revised to 1/hour, recalculating 7 at 09:00. All repair numbers are teaching constructions; preserve V1 without claiming the fault was known at 08:00.","caption":"All repair numbers are teaching constructions; preserve V1 without claiming the fault was known at 08:00."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns preserve conditions, operations, and output status. The static figure does not indicate a running real system; any future interaction should switch only explicitly stated conditions and update result status correspondingly.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original investigation explicitly distinguishes initial confusion from subsequent judgment, illustrating why evidence timing must be frozen."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.1 Step one: freeze the current state; Chinese source lines 2333–2336; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"1f8fb399fc41f73c2966849143e1f0b837536c32d7e6dcdc17eace461cafd6ab","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.2":{"title":"11.4.2 Step two: identify actors and circles","lead":"A shared roster, label, or one collaboration need not form a circle with stable boundaries; one choice need not reveal fixed personality. First establish whom we discuss and how they connect.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2337–2340; source anchor section-11-4-2; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “Do not forcibly reify merely to complete the graph.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Part Nine brings actors' slow, mid, and fast states and conditional action sets into analysis. Part Ten examines establishing circle-object facts from documents, relationships, and interactions, separately testing grouping gain for the task. This section uses those results, not permanent personality types assigned to everyone.","Actors may be individuals actually having effects or supported collective objects. Before graph inclusion, state evidence for task-relevant responsibilities, capabilities, resources, and relations. A name's appearance or shared job title does not automatically establish decision-making authority for the same action; inferring psychological motives from office also exceeds the boundary.","Candidate circle groupings remain analytically useful for proposing connections, shared resources, and boundaries to check. But a candidate cannot first become an established collective node whose assumed will explains member behavior; that makes object and mechanism circularly certify each other.","Retaining variable candidates when personality evidence is insufficient means “this difference may matter and remains to be distinguished,” not permission to collect more private experiences. Prioritize task-required observable behavior and conditions such as schedules, authorization, and equipment availability; do not misattribute equipment constraints to poor responsibility.","Circle boundaries may be nested, overlapping, or parallel by dimension. Maintenance and purchasing teams sharing a coordinator does not mean they merged into one object; examine people, budgets, permission, and task chains separately. Documents, membership, sustained interaction, or shared exposure can also support corresponding object facts without unified will in every circle. Only grouping-related explanatory or foresight gain claims separately require G1; untested G1 cannot erase independent object facts. When scale changes, reconfirm meanings of nodes and edges.","This step keeps two material accounts: facts about agents' and circles' identities, membership, and relationships; and gains from using those groupings in the current projection task. Reliable documents and interaction records may support the former; only formal support for the latter checks G1. When both are missing, retain candidates, but do not misstate absence of G1 as absence of these people in reality."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Task role","leftText":"Responsibilities, capabilities, and authority supported by materials.","rightTitle":"Personality essence","rightText":"Cannot be presumed from one instance of role performance."},{"leftTitle":"Candidate grouping","leftText":"An analytical division whose common mechanism awaits checking.","rightTitle":"Supported circle object","rightText":"Requires corresponding object boundaries and operating materials."},{"leftTitle":"Membership overlap","leftText":"The same person participates in two groups.","rightTitle":"The two circles are identical","rightText":"Additionally requires identity grounds for the declared dimension."}],"boundaries":["Useful grouping is not established entity status; provisional nonreification also does not mean members are unconnected.","Do not fill default motives when psychological materials are absent or treat an individual's weaknesses as the entire system's bottleneck.","Actual collection remains constrained by purpose, necessity, and authority."],"questions":["Can a group be recognized as a stable circle from a membership roster alone?","If repairs slow down, may ‘employees lack responsibility’ be assigned first?"],"diagramPath":"","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.2","start_line":2409,"end_line":2412,"startLine":2409,"endLine":2412}],"outputId":"section-11-4-2","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["List agents relevant to the current outcome and their checkable roles and conditions.","Check candidate circles' connections, boundaries, recurring mechanisms, and resources; do not reify them directly from names.","Separate supported states from candidate personality or collective variables.","Retain candidate status for unestablished groupings rather than letting them carry established causation."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: one person across groups is not unified capacity","text":"Fictional repair team Alpha has technicians A and B; procurement team Beta has purchaser C and coordinator B. A is qualified to repair, C has procurement authority, and B only transmits paperwork. Roster overlap supports only overlap in personnel; C cannot be counted as repair capacity, nor B regarded as authorized to approve purchases. If all that is known is that the three share a chat group, even these groupings remain to be checked.","assumptions":"Roles and authority are wholly stipulated in the construction; no actual person's capabilities or psychology are judged.","boundary":"Communication in one group may supply an information channel but does not directly establish shared budgets, unified will, or action permission."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Because B belongs to both groups, they can be merged into one agent with unlimited complementarity.","effect":"Shared membership does not remove skill, authority, time, or resource constraints.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a group be recognized as a stable circle from a membership roster alone?","answer":"No. The roster may provide candidate entry, but task-relevant connections, boundaries, and operating materials are still required.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If repairs slow down, may ‘employees lack responsibility’ be assigned first?","answer":"No. First check distinguishable conditions such as equipment, part arrival, qualifications, and scheduling; personality inferences require independent support.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[],"diagramBrief":"The original object-admission diagram remains available as a historical download; the current text separately explains object facts and grouping gain in the task.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit explains object admission and candidate status; materials sufficient to establish a particular actual team under the book's complete circle contract have not been obtained. Apollo role rosters cannot masquerade as completed circle identification; an explicit construction shows nonentailment."},"openQuestions":["Actual team boundaries, resource allocation, recurring connections, and authorization records were not provided; stable circle-object status is not granted here."],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.2 Step two: identify agents and circles; Chinese source, lines 2337—2340; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"The relevant explanation in this section has been revised for v2; the original research attachments and the scope of external-source review retain their historical status.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"ee2fc2df6d6af00e2307d3cabe8687486e26a4fd4ab5a491963400dc8e543ae6","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.3":{"title":"11.4.3 Step three: separate dual-channel conditions","lead":"Material feasibility and how people understand, experience, or accept something are two kinds of conditions. When one kind of material is missing, pause inferences depending on it without necessarily stopping every other independent judgment.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2341—2344; source anchor section-11-4-3; short quotation: ‘pause only propagation that depends on the missing channel’ (translated from Chinese). The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","M records task-relevant material conditions such as quantities, equipment, work hours, interfaces, and budgets; Ψ records experience–meaning conditions such as expressed feelings, interpretations of meaning, or understandings of justification. These are neither mutually exclusive worlds nor default mirror images, and evidence for one does not automatically establish the other.","A cross-channel bridge explains which material change affects which experience–meaning variable through what distinguishable mechanism, or conversely how it produces action and material consequences. Restored repair equipment does not mean restored employee trust; a survey expressing support does not mean actual increased processing capacity. Connection requires a corresponding bridge and temporal materials.","Missing and not applicable differ. If the question asks only the number of items a specified machine can complete under fixed settings, personal experience may be inapplicable to that local calculation, with reasons stated. If the question asks willingness to work overtime, experience, meaning, and autonomous choice may alter the answer; missingness cannot be filled as willingness through an assumption of symmetry.","A local pause protects evidential boundaries. Unknown willingness to work overtime does not prevent reporting checked machine capacity, but it blocks continued inference along ‘the machine has spare capacity → employees will stay → the backlog must clear’. Separate the supported capacity path from the behavioral path awaiting checking, preventing both spread of unknowns throughout the system and bypassing of unknowns.","Do not collect experiential materials for formal completeness. Questions about psychology, health, or private life require actual necessity and appropriate boundaries; marking task-irrelevant Ψ fields not applicable is more honest than filling guesses or creating intrusive surveys."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Material capability","leftText":"The scope equipment and resources can support under given conditions.","rightTitle":"Willingness or identification","rightText":"Concerns agents' experience, interpretation, and choice, requiring separate materials."},{"leftTitle":"Unknown","leftText":"Relevant but sufficient materials have not yet been obtained.","rightTitle":"Not applicable","rightText":"Does not affect the declared local question, with reasons given."},{"leftTitle":"Local pause","leftText":"Stops inferences dependent on a missing bridge.","rightTitle":"Complete shutdown of work","rightText":"Analysis unaffected by the gap may continue."}],"boundaries":["The M/Ψ distinction serves the task and does not require equally long columns for every object.","Self-reported meaning has its own evidential status and cannot be replaced directly by machine data.","If behavioral feedback changes capacity, an earlier ‘not applicable’ judgment should be rechecked."],"questions":["If a machine can process 6 items in two hours, why is the actual completed quantity still unknown?","When may Ψ be marked not applicable?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-3.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.3","start_line":2413,"end_line":2416,"startLine":2413,"endLine":2416}],"outputId":"section-11-4-3","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Fix the question first, then list task-relevant M and Ψ variables item by item.","For cross-channel inferences, state the bridge, direction, time, and material responsibilities.","Mark missing relevant fields unknown; give reasons for inapplicability of irrelevant fields.","Pause only paths actually dependent on missing fields, preserving independently established results."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: one result known, another unknown","text":"A fictional device has two idle hours, processes at most 3 items per hour, and has sufficient materials. Its equipment ceiling is therefore 6 items. Actual completion also requires a qualified person present; all that is known is that their shift has ended, not whether they are willing and authorized to work overtime. The equipment-capacity ceiling of 6 may be given, but not guaranteed actual completion of 6. Unknown willingness and unknown permission also cannot replace each other.","assumptions":"Equipment capacity and hours are constructed; the capacity ceiling guarantees neither actual demand, behavior, nor authorization.","boundary":"If the question only calculates the device's theoretical ceiling, experience need not be collected; if it asks actual completion, corresponding behavioral and authority conditions are required."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"We do not know whether employees are willing to work overtime, so even hourly machine capacity cannot be reported.","effect":"Missingness blocks only the actual-completion path dependent on willingness, without erasing independent equipment conditions.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The machine is back to normal, so everyone must trust management again.","effect":"The bridge from material conditions to meaning is missing; it cannot be filled symmetrically.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If a machine can process 6 items in two hours, why is the actual completed quantity still unknown?","answer":"Actual execution conditions such as presence, qualifications, willingness, and authorization remain unmet or unchecked.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"When may Ψ be marked not applicable?","answer":"Strictly limit the question to local capability unaffected by the variable and explain why; recheck if the question expands.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-3.png","alt":"Known M | teaching example: equipment processes 3 items/hour and is available for 2 hours. Ψ and behavioral conditions await checking: willingness, presence, and permission cannot be filled from equipment status. Reportable ceiling: 6; actual completion remains conditional, without deleting the independent capacity result. Values are a teaching construction; the intermediate gap cannot be crossed by assuming willingness.","caption":"Values are a teaching construction; the intermediate gap cannot be crossed by assuming willingness."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns preserve conditions, operations, and output status. The static figure does not indicate a running real system; any future interaction should switch only explicitly stated conditions and update result status correspondingly.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This examines the formal boundary against automatic entailment between the two channels. The spaceflight reports read here do not supply sufficient individual-experience materials; successful rescue is not used to infer trust or motivation backward."},"openQuestions":["Actual overtime choices and experiential materials were not obtained; unknown fields are not filled, and no personnel survey is conducted."],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.3 Step three: separate dual-channel conditions; Chinese source, lines 2341—2344; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"00276d555cf94a22f613fbf773c2d099f580c246eccadeffa2444ecabd351c3a","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.4":{"title":"11.4.4 Step four: declare clocks, thresholds, capacities, and delays","lead":"‘It will get through’ is insufficient: ask how long it takes, how much can pass at once, what is lost en route, and whether the window has already been missed. An arrow without a clock easily turns possible into timely and available stock into adequate supply.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2345—2348; source anchor section-11-4-4; short quotation: ‘mark an update clock for every variable’ (translated from Chinese). The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Variable-update clocks may use fixed intervals, event triggers, batch settlement, or slow, intermediate, and fast cycles at different levels. Inventory checked hourly, instantaneous alarms, and work hours recorded only the next day cannot be treated as simultaneous states without alignment. Observation frequency also differs from actual change frequency.","Thresholds need triggering direction and equality conventions; capacities need units and windows; delays must distinguish execution, transmission, and recording. Register losses, saturation, reversals, and recovery windows only when relevant and supported by materials. Unsupported details remain unknown rather than assuming identical nonlinearities in every system.","A capacity of 3 items per hour does not mean 3 items can be completed immediately; total stock of 10 also does not mean 10 can be obtained at a particular location within 10 minutes. If a channel waits an hour before transport, a recovery action may arrive too late for the current task window. Temporal constraints can change option rankings without changing the eventual total.","Near thresholds, distinguish measurement error and hysteresis: crossing an alarm value may differ from actual harm, and if cooling is still needed after a rise before recovery, recrossing a threshold in reverse cannot instantly erase downtime. These are recording responsibilities, not a claim that every object exhibits hysteresis.","Declaring parameters is not accurately estimating them. Sources may be equipment specifications, current observations, or model assumptions, whose statuses should be separated. High-consequence settings require conservatively retained uncertainty ranges; teaching formulas cannot become real control instructions directly."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Capacity","leftText":"The maximum throughput supportable within a specified window.","rightTitle":"Inventory","rightText":"The quantity available at a time point; the two differ in units and roles."},{"leftTitle":"Recovery conditions","leftText":"Conditions and waiting needed to reenter an available state.","rightTitle":"Alarm cleared","rightText":"A record or threshold status changes, which need not mean capacity has recovered."},{"leftTitle":"Recording clock","leftText":"When a value is obtained or written.","rightTitle":"Object clock","rightText":"When the real variable changes."}],"boundaries":["One fixed step size does not fit every event; subdivide critical windows where needed.","Unchecked threshold parameters cannot justify actual safety action.","Sufficient cumulative supply may still include a temporary shortage during one segment."],"questions":["What are processing 3 items per hour and currently having 3 spare parts, respectively?","Why must it be stated whether the deadline includes equality?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-4.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Apollo 13: consumption rates determine the available window","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Apollo 13: consumption rates determine the available window","text":"The report records emergency power-down of the lunar module reducing electrical-energy demand to about 20% of normal operating values and correspondingly reducing cooling-water use. The added point here is that changing consumption rates can change resource duration; initial inventory alone is insufficient.","sourceLabel":"NASA，Report of Apollo 13 Review Board，1970-06-15","sourceUrl":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","sourceLocator":"Chapter 5, Finding 45(a)—(c), printed p.5-33, PDF page 206","boundary":"This is the report's conclusion for a particular mission, not a universal energy-saving coefficient for other systems; 20% does not mean every consumable fell to exactly 20%.","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.4","start_line":2417,"end_line":2420,"startLine":2417,"endLine":2420}],"outputId":"section-11-4-4","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["For each variable, state its actual change timescale and observation/model-entry clocks.","Express thresholds, capacities, and windows in consistent units, specifying equality at boundaries.","Before propagation, register relevant delays, losses, saturation, and recovery conditions; unknowns do not default to zero.","Check whether an action's arrival still falls within the task window before comparing consequences."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: late replenishment cannot recover a missed window","text":"A fictional repair task requires completing 3 items during 08:00—09:00, each consuming 1 part; stock at 08:00 is 1 part. Replenishment of 2 parts arrives only at 09:00, and the task definition requires completion strictly before 09:00. Even if equipment can process 3 items per hour, parts limit this window to at most 1 item; cumulative parts reaching 3 at 09:00 does not make 3 completed in the earlier window.","assumptions":"Parts cannot be borrowed in advance, each completed item must already have consumed a part, and both arrival time and the strictly exclusive deadline are construction assumptions.","boundary":"If the contract permits completion after arrival at 09:00 or extends the task window, the question must be rewritten; the deadline cannot be silently changed to rescue the conclusion."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The final resource total is sufficient, so demand can be met at every intermediate moment.","effect":"Inventory and throughput are not temporally aligned; an early gap may be irreversible.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What are processing 3 items per hour and currently having 3 spare parts, respectively?","answer":"The former is capacity within a time window, the latter inventory at a time point; they constrain jointly but are not interchangeable.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why must it be stated whether the deadline includes equality?","answer":"Whether resources arriving at the boundary time enter the task window changes feasibility.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-4.png","alt":"Stock at 08:00 is 1, with 1 consumed per item and a target of 3 within the window. Replenishment of 2 arrives only at 09:00; the deadline is strictly before 09:00. The original window allows at most 1; later arrival cannot rewrite the past, and a new window needs a new judgment. Entirely a teaching construction; cumulative inventory of 3 cannot establish completion of 3 in the original window.","caption":"Entirely a teaching construction; cumulative inventory of 3 cannot establish completion of 3 in the original window."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns preserve conditions, operations, and output status. The static figure does not indicate a running real system; any future interaction should switch only explicitly stated conditions and update result status correspondingly.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original report explicitly records the relationship between energy-use rate and cooling-water consumption, supporting the distinction between inventory and time-window constraints."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.4 Step four: declare clocks, thresholds, capacities, and delays; Chinese source, lines 2345—2348; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"8cef3fe65fc8427a09bd113bf3f5b4ad97b7c3ffdb5d726137f444c38a9f215d","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.5":{"title":"11.4.5 Step five: inject events or actions","lead":"First ask whether something happened, then who did it, to whom, with what consequences, and with what authority. Unauthorized acts may still actually occur, while things written into plans may never happen at all.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2349—2352; source anchor section-11-4-5; short quotation: ‘first establish whether the action occurred’ (translated from Chinese). The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","A run may contain multiple events and actions; real chains need not be deleted to ‘look at one at a time’. Each retains independent sources, times, and identities before being connected through grounded temporal order. When order cannot be determined, retain branches under 11.3.2 rather than substituting the order in which tool messages return for real-world order.","Agent, object, consequences, and authorization status are different fields. Observing a machine shut down does not identify its operator; confirming the operator also does not establish their motives or authority. Leave each missing item unknown rather than deleting the entire fact.","Under 11.3's notation, u is reserved for authorized actions that actually occur; observed or reported actions that occurred without authorization may enter e as events, with authority listed separately. This registers world changes without endorsing behavior; multiple references to the same action still apply its corresponding effect only once.","Plans, recommendations, and simulated actions that have not occurred retain conditional status. For example, ‘if another shift is added’ generates hypothetical consequences only and cannot automatically add staff to the baseline state. Approval also does not mean arrival; execution still requires occurrence evidence.","Forming a recommendation for deliberation and executing for someone are two levels of behavior. Recommendations may be offered after making evidence, value premises, costs, authority gaps, and revision conditions explicit. A model actually initiating real action requires separate valid authorization and corresponding protection checks. ‘This option is best’ cannot become an authorization letter, nor can lack of execution authority justify rejecting every bounded analysis."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Occurrence","leftText":"Has corresponding factual evidence.","rightTitle":"Authorized","rightText":"A normative and permission status; neither implies the other."},{"leftTitle":"Approved plan","leftText":"Permission exists, but execution may not yet have occurred.","rightTitle":"Resources actually arrived","rightText":"Occurrence materials are required before changing the baseline state."},{"leftTitle":"Recommendation for deliberation","leftText":"State a conditional comparison of options.","rightTitle":"Implement an action","rightText":"Changes reality and requires independent valid authorization."}],"boundaries":["Lack of authorization is not a reason to delete facts, and registering facts grants no permission.","Unknown ordering of multiple events must not be forced into one sequence.","Do not enter simulated actions in executed-action logs."],"questions":["Starting stock is 5, an unauthorized withdrawal takes 2, and approved replenishment of 3 has not arrived. How should current inventory be recorded?","Can recommendations still be offered without authority to execute?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-5.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.5","start_line":2421,"end_line":2424,"startLine":2421,"endLine":2424}],"outputId":"section-11-4-5","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Check actual-occurrence evidence for each input, distinguishing plans, reports, observations, and simulations.","Register agents, objects, consequences, and authority separately; one field cannot supply the others.","Retain sources and ordering for multiple events, without applying the same event twice.","Recommendations disclose premises and gaps; execution separately obtains valid authorization."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: unauthorized withdrawal and pending replenishment","text":"A fictional warehouse starts with 5 items. Reliable records show someone took 2 without permission; another 3-item resupply is approved but has not arrived. Known current stock is 3: do not delete unauthorized removal and retain 5, or add approved supplies prematurely and record 6. If resupply later actually arrives, register it separately; only then does stock become 6 under these assumptions.","assumptions":"Quantity removed, occurrence evidence, no other stock movements, and resupply status are construction premises; no actual taking is arranged.","boundary":"If removal is only an unverified report, give the report-conditional value rather than claiming a measured stock of 3."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"This removal was unauthorized and should not enter the formal model.","effect":"Deleting an actual change distorts later resource projections; preserve the fact and record its unauthorized status separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"I recommended more staff, so queue capacity can be increased directly.","effect":"Recommendation is not execution; changed capacity needs evidence of corresponding occurrence.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Starting stock is 5, an unauthorized withdrawal takes 2, and approved replenishment of 3 has not arrived. How should current inventory be recorded?","answer":"3 under the explicit assumptions; unarrived supplies enter only a conditional branch.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can recommendations still be offered without authority to execute?","answer":"A bounded recommendation for deliberation is possible with grounds, normative premises, costs, authority gaps, and revision conditions; it cannot be implemented without authorization.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-5.png","alt":"Teaching stock 5, actual removal 2, permission absent; another resupply 3 is approved but unarrived and enters only a conditional branch; current baseline stock 3 becomes 6 only upon arrival. Recording ≠ endorsement or execution. Entirely a teaching construction; recommendation, approval, and actual occurrence cannot substitute for one another.","caption":"Entirely a teaching construction; recommendation, approval, and actual occurrence cannot substitute for one another."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns preserve conditions, operations, and output status. The static figure does not indicate a running real system; any future interaction should switch only explicitly stated conditions and update result status correspondingly.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The core is logical independence of fact and authorization. The Apollo report read does not permit checking authority for every action; successful results are not used to infer complete authorization. Explicit constructions test recording rules instead."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.5 Step Five: Inject Events or Actions; Chinese source lines 2349–2352; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"57fc14b5687d24cee1211bfbb0ffd3517594aaa929e5b289ec934c372d054799","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.6":{"title":"11.4.6 Step Six: Propagate along Declared Channels","lead":"One place changes and another changes later; this is not yet a causal arrow. Preserve known changes first, then ask segment by segment which channel, which period, and which competing explanations were distinguished.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2353–2356; source anchor section-11-4-6; short quotation translated from Chinese: ‘Unknown internal mechanisms remain unknown.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","Direct state differences also have evidence status: keep measured differences, record-supported inferences, and conditional model calculations separately. ‘Direct’ does not automatically mean a causal effect identified by randomized experiment; uncontrolled before-and-after differences may include shared background changes.","Feedback requires material on return and subsequent state or rule changes; a message’s arrival is not learning. Indirect effects need valid intermediate transmission, and cross-circle cascades need each boundary and resource interface checked. Crossing one layer does not automatically inherit the preceding layer’s efficacy.","Separate total effects from internal mechanisms. A bounded intervention effect on a final outcome identified by an appropriate design may be retained despite unclear mediators; unknown mediators cannot be filled into a unique chain. Conversely, knowing one feasible mechanism does not automatically determine actual total-effect magnitude.","Correlation and co-occurrence may prompt investigation, but shared shocks, selection into records, and reverse effects may produce similar patterns. Specific propagation requires matching timing, interfaces, and flow evidence and explaining how alternatives are distinguished; absent material, mark candidate edges.","Mathematical propagation depends on explicit functions, parameters, and feasible domains. Even positive average gains on each edge may yield no total gain when windows, subgroup selection, or intermediate states do not match; 11.6 develops these interface issues. This step cannot mechanically multiply arrows as facts."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Before-and-after state difference","leftText":"Change observed across time.","rightTitle":"Causal intervention effect","rightText":"Requires appropriate identification conditions, not before-and-after comparison alone."},{"leftTitle":"A bounded total effect is identified","leftText":"Answers a limited question about this intervention’s outcome.","rightTitle":"Every mediating edge is identified","rightText":"Requires additional mechanism material."},{"leftTitle":"Return signal","leftText":"Information returns to an earlier node.","rightTitle":"Feedback change and learning","rightText":"Also require subsequent state or rule changes and corresponding learning evidence."}],"boundaries":["Missing mechanism details should neither erase an independently established total effect nor invite invented edges.","Evidence for adjacent edges does not guarantee joint applicability; interfaces and windows must be compatible.","Retain competing explanations when shared background cannot be excluded."],"questions":["Why are successive changes insufficient for a causal edge?","Does an identified total effect permit completing the internal mechanism graph?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-6.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Apollo 13: an evidenced energy–water-use connection","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Apollo 13: an evidenced energy–water-use connection","text":"The report connects reduced Lunar Module power consumption, lower heat production, and reduced cooling-water use. Compared with the consumption-rate window in the preceding subsection, this adds that propagation explanations need specific intermediate links, not simply ‘save electricity → everything improves’.","sourceLabel":"NASA，Report of Apollo 13 Review Board，1970-06-15","sourceUrl":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","sourceLocator":"Chapter 4, Part 5, Return to Earth, printed p. 4-48, final paragraph on PDF page 156","boundary":"The report supports this specific engineering explanation, not attribution of all psychological, organizational, or safety outcomes to saving power; this guide independently estimates no causal-effect magnitude.","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.6","start_line":2425,"end_line":2428,"startLine":2425,"endLine":2428}],"outputId":"section-11-4-6","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Confirm the starting point and direct state differences’ status and timing.","List channel, conditions, scope, sources, and competing explanations for every edge.","Retain identified total effects separately from unknown internal mechanisms.","Store model derivations separately as conditional results without upgrading them to actual observations."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: queue calculation and observation are separate","text":"Fictional B0=6 with 2 arrivals per hour. If a breakdown fixes processing capacity at 1, B1=6+2−1=7 and B2=7+2−1=8. If another branch retains capacity 3, B1=5 and B2=4. This is a conditional difference of 4 items under the specified recurrence. Without an actual breakdown intervention or observations, it cannot be reported as ‘breakdown empirically increased backlog by 4’.","assumptions":"Arrivals precede processing each hour; capacity stays fixed for two hours, no other flows exist, and both branches share initial value and arrivals.","boundary":"If actual breakdown also changes arrivals or measurement rules, this difference cannot transfer directly."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Two simulations differ by 4, so an actual breakdown’s causal effect has been measured as 4.","effect":"A simulation comparison is only a model-conditional difference, lacking actual identification design and observation.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The total effect is reliable, but one internal link is unknown, so the total effect must be deleted too.","effect":"Retain the independently valid bounded result and mark the unknown mechanism separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why are successive changes insufficient for a causal edge?","answer":"Shared background, record selection, and other mechanisms can produce the same sequence; corresponding channels and discriminating material are needed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does an identified total effect permit completing the internal mechanism graph?","answer":"No. Preserve the bounded total effect while leaving unknown mediators unfilled.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-6.png","alt":"Teaching shared start 6, arrivals 2/hour, frozen two-hour window; processing 1 gives 7→8, processing 3 gives 5→4, with both recurrences explicit. Conditional terminal difference 4 is not a measured causal effect; actual channels need separate checks. Each step follows B_next=max(0,B+2−processed quantity); no actual experiment.","caption":"Each step follows B_next=max(0,B+2−processed quantity); no actual experiment."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns preserve conditions, operations, and output status. The static figure does not indicate a running real system; any future interaction should switch only explicitly stated conditions and update result status correspondingly.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original report supplies a specific engineering connection, illustrating the need for concrete channels without replacing a causal experiment this guide did not perform."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.6 Step Six: Propagate along Declared Channels; Chinese source lines 2353–2356; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"a57d79326a39ab0e9f07a7a4f79950fe6d57f078e47792f8bff3ccf7637eb625","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.7":{"title":"11.4.7 Step Seven: Generate Paths at Branch Points","lead":"Branching is not about writing many stories; it preserves conditional differences that may still change the answer. Distinguish ‘only one can occur’ from ‘they may occur together’, then decide between separate and parallel branches.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2357–2360; source anchor section-11-4-7; short quotation translated from Chinese: ‘Separate mutually exclusive paths.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","Branches may arise from condition values, near-threshold states, actors’ choices, external disturbances, or mechanism uncertainty. State triggering differences, not merely emotional labels ‘optimistic, ordinary, pessimistic’. Branches also need one frozen starting point; otherwise the comparison may silently change initial values.","Mutually exclusive paths cannot both count toward one actual result. An indivisible task choosing either machine A or B within one window has exclusive choices; procurement delay and machine failure may coincide, so forcing them into either/or omits their joint branch.","Co-occurrence also does not mean independence. Branches sharing workers or budgets, or affecting one another’s conditions, need joint states and shared-resource constraints. Separate feasibility cannot establish joint feasibility.","Merge nodes require an explicit contract: which key states, remaining resources, history dependencies, authorities, and future actions are sufficiently identical or equivalent for a bounded task? Equal final stock does not automatically permit merging; failure debt or missed commitments may still affect the next step.","Branch counts are not credibility; ten generated scenarios do not automatically form a frequency distribution. Without probability grounds, retain conditional paths and preserve key branches by impact and distinguishability where necessary. Diagram neatness cannot justify deleting a high-loss branch with substantive possibility. Section 11.9 later addresses path lineage and pruning records."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Mutually exclusive","leftText":"Cannot both be true under the same contract.","rightTitle":"Parallel and able to co-occur","rightText":"May happen together, with resources and interactions still checked."},{"leftTitle":"Temporarily equal values","leftText":"A field agrees at this moment.","rightTitle":"Mergeable","rightText":"Conditions relevant to later tasks are sufficiently aligned."},{"leftTitle":"Fraction of generated branches","leftText":"How many stories a model listed.","rightTitle":"Real-world probability","rightText":"Needs data and a probability model, not branch counts."}],"boundaries":["Omitting joint-occurrence branches misses compound risks.","Different mechanisms may yield the same outcome; mechanism tasks do not permit arbitrary merging.","Branches need stopping or checking conditions, not infinite elaboration of every imagination."],"questions":["Why include the jointly occurring outcome 9?","How may the two 7s be handled if only the first-hour result matters?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-7.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Apollo 13: the return path was not one default action","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Apollo 13: the return path was not one default action","text":"The report records several propulsion options considered, with the team choosing to use the Lunar Module descent engine as much as possible given propulsion needs and uncertainty about the damaged Service Module. The sole focus here is that option comparison depends on conditions.","sourceLabel":"NASA，Report of Apollo 13 Review Board，1970-06-15","sourceUrl":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","sourceLocator":"Chapter 4, Part 5, Return to Earth, printed p. 4-48, first half of PDF page 156","boundary":"Not every option’s risks and probabilities are reconstructed; unimplemented options are not recorded as actual paths, and ultimate success does not prove every rejected option would have failed.","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.7","start_line":2429,"end_line":2432,"startLine":2429,"endLine":2432}],"outputId":"section-11-4-7","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Generate branches from specific conditional differences that change the task answer.","Determine exclusivity, co-occurrence, or sequential dependence, constructing joint states when needed.","Keep starting points, triggers, and resource limits traceable for every branch.","Merge only when key states meet explicit merger conditions, without default probabilities."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: two disturbances form four joint conditions","text":"Fictional B0=6 and B1=B0+arrivals−processing after one hour. Arrivals may be 2 or 4, processing 3 or 1. Joint conditions yield (2,3)→5, (2,1)→7, (4,3)→7, and (4,1)→9. Listing only exclusive stories of ‘more arrivals’ and ‘slower processing’ misses their joint 9. The two 7s also arise from different arrival/capacity states; if later conditions persist, current equality at 7 alone does not permit merging.","assumptions":"All four combinations are allowed in the construction; no actual probabilities are given. If conditions persist next hour, both 7s become 8, but flows and intervention responses still differ.","boundary":"If only first-hour backlog matters and subsequent mechanisms genuinely do not, the two 7s may share an outcome class while retaining their source branches."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Both branches reach 7, so they remain equivalent forever.","effect":"Equal terminal values do not guarantee equal resources, mechanisms, or feasible interventions; merging needs a bounded task.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why include the jointly occurring outcome 9?","answer":"Increased arrivals and reduced capacity may co-occur; forced exclusivity misses joint conditions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How may the two 7s be handled if only the first-hour result matters?","answer":"They may share a result-value class while retaining distinct source conditions; mechanism identity cannot be declared.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-7.png","alt":"Start B0=6, arrivals∈{2,4}, processing∈{3,1}; arrivals 2, processing 3 → final 5; arrivals 2, processing 1 → final 7; arrivals 4, processing 3 → final 7; arrivals 4, processing 1 → final 9. All four are permitted teaching conditions; equality at 7 does not automatically merge them, and no actual probabilities are assigned.","caption":"All four are permitted teaching conditions; equality at 7 does not automatically merge them, and no actual probabilities are assigned."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the selections, switches, or clicks described below are ideas for a later design only. Cards enumerate the Cartesian product without crossing arrows obscuring it; future condition switches should retain mechanism labels alongside them.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The report explicitly separates considered options from actual choices, illustrating why conditional branches must not enter occurred history."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.7 Step Seven: Generate Paths at Branch Points; Chinese source lines 2357–2360; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"044707b7a202d61b41c1c33e52d540d2fa098c2ab1104852c74fd7d57b603697","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.8":{"title":"11.4.8 Step Eight: Register Signals, Residuals, and Variable Candidates","lead":"An unexplained model difference calls for checking, not inventing a new real-world variable’s value. Each path should specify which signals support it and which would change the judgment.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2361–2364; source anchor section-11-4-8; short quotation translated from Chinese: ‘Actual values cannot be generated automatically.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","Early signals need observation definitions, windows, sources, and associated branches—for example, first observing an actual processing decline, then whether backlog follows the corresponding path. A supporting signal is not absolute proof; check whether competing paths also produce it. A contrary signal is a distinguishable observation weakening the path, without waiting for the endpoint before recognizing failure.","Residuals first depend on model and observation definitions. Observed 8 versus predicted 7 gives residual 1, indicating only this discrepancy. It may reflect unrecorded arrivals, processing-capacity error, mismatched windows, observation error, or omitted mechanisms. Without discriminating material, ‘difference 1’ cannot establish a real variable called collaboration resistance with value 1.","A variable-candidate ledger should record definition, reason for proposal, observation method, supportive and contrary material, competing explanations, and upgrade conditions. It turns questions into testable work, not a new freely adjustable factor for every residual.","Residuals are not automatically random noise. Inference using means, variances, or distributions needs corresponding sampling and error conditions; selective missingness or changed definitions cannot usually be assigned to independent identically distributed error without explanation. Repetition strengthens reasons to investigate, not direct identification of a mechanism.","Prioritize the smallest discriminating observation that changes the current answer or action comparison. More candidates are not inherently better; if a simple recording-definition issue suffices, check it before adding hidden variables to preserve complexity."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Residual","leftText":"Difference between observation and expectation under the current representation.","rightTitle":"A new variable exists","rightText":"Needs independent definition, measurement, and discriminating support."},{"leftTitle":"Supporting signal","leftText":"Compatible with a path and potentially strengthens its support.","rightTitle":"Unique proof","rightText":"Requires excluding competing explanations for the same signal."},{"leftTitle":"Contrary signal","leftText":"Material capable of weakening the current path.","rightTitle":"Bad news","rightText":"Unpleasantness differs from evidentiary responsibility."}],"boundaries":["No reader or field testing occurred; signal proposals are not claimed validated as effective.","A new variable cannot merely be defined to absorb every error exactly.","If observation definitions change, update the observation model before rushing to change the world’s mechanism."],"questions":["Why can difference 1 not directly identify unrecorded arrivals?","When should contrary signals be registered?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-8.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.8","start_line":2433,"end_line":2436,"startLine":2433,"endLine":2436}],"outputId":"section-11-4-8","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Prewrite observable early and contrary signals for every branch, with windows.","Calculate differences between matched observations and conditional predictions, checking time and measurement first.","Propose candidates and competing explanations for unexplained parts without assigning actual truth values.","Design minimal discriminating material, then upgrade, retain, split, or withdraw candidates according to results."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: difference 1 does not identify its cause","text":"Fictional B0=6, recorded arrivals 2 and processing 1 predict B1=7; a new observation records 8, a difference 8−7=1. Explanation A: one arrival was omitted, actual arrivals 3. Explanation B: one processed item was erroneously recorded, actual processing 0. Both give 8. Actual completion records may distinguish them; directly setting ‘team friction=1’ neither excludes these record explanations nor provides a measurable definition.","assumptions":"The construction assumes an accurate start, no other mechanisms, and provisionally credible observation 8; actual candidates may be more numerous.","boundary":"If completion records also suffer selection or misrecording, a single record cannot again serve as an infallible judge."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model’s unexplained item must result from personality resistance, so resistance is set to 1.","effect":"The remaining difference identifies no specific cause; both variable name and value lack independent support.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why can difference 1 not directly identify unrecorded arrivals?","answer":"Recording one extra completion, or recording unfinished work as completed, can also produce difference 1, among other unlisted mechanisms; discriminating material is needed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"When should contrary signals be registered?","answer":"Preferably alongside the path before viewing outcomes, preventing any result from becoming post hoc support.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-8.png","alt":"Teaching prediction 7, new observation 8, residual=1. Competing explanations: one unrecorded arrival or one incorrectly recorded completion. Check distinguishing material; candidates still need support, rather than filling “friction=1.” Values and candidates are teaching constructions; the difference does not prove a new mechanism.","caption":"Values and candidates are teaching constructions; the difference does not prove a new mechanism."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns preserve conditions, operations, and output status. The static figure does not indicate a running real system; any future interaction should switch only explicitly stated conditions and update result status correspondingly.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section tests the nonimplication from residual to variable through a reproducible construction. Unknown causes in the accident report read are not treated as empirical residuals assignable arbitrary values."},"openQuestions":["Actual residuals require consistently defined observations and frozen predictions. No field data are available here; no error distribution or personality variable is estimated."],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.8 Step eight: register signals, residuals, and variable candidates; Chinese source lines 2361–2364; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"d2fee663093837dca68d5b9411366fb4220537dc46b965aaf3a3d605dc13de29","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.4.9":{"title":"11.4.9 Step nine: outcome write-back","lead":"When outcomes arrive, honest comparison matters: what was right, what was wrong, and which simple method was better. Inserting the ending into the original draft is not learning. Preserving that draft and letting new evidence change the next version makes scrutiny possible.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2365–2368; original anchor section-11-4-9. Short quotation translated from Chinese: “Write-back does not overwrite the original run.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","Write-back first appends actual outcomes with sources, object-event times, and acquisition times, then matches them to the original run. A news narrative or rerunning a model is not equivalent to actual outcomes. If outcomes have not arrived or definitions do not match, retain unresolved status rather than forcing closure.","Support for a path does not make every mechanism true. Different mechanisms can yield the same outcome, and errors may cancel into apparent success. Examine timing, magnitude, key intermediate signals, and contrary material together. Locate local departures through dependencies; one failure does not make the entire framework useless, nor one success universally effective.","Compare simple baselines for the same target, window, available information, and scoring rule. A queue task might initially use the last observation carried forward. If the complex model performs worse, record it honestly and examine whether complexity adds actual gain. Do not select a baseline afterward merely to lose to the model.","Calibration is a long-term or grouped correspondence property of probability predictions, not one hit. If this run offered conditional branches without probabilities, assess coverage, timing, conditions, and distinguishing power; do not invent probabilities afterward to claim good calibration. Part XII further specifies forecast contracts.","Write-back may downgrade mechanisms, split variables, narrow boundaries, or retire items, rather than merely add explanations. State which new evidence changes which dependency, how the next version handles it, and what remains unknown. Source corrections revise epistemic lineage; changed real conditions require event registration. Do not conflate them.","Creating a next version does not establish improved learning. Claiming it is better requires later independent or appropriately held-out testing. Repeatedly tuning to the same outcome for closer fit demonstrates only adaptation to seen material."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"An outcome supports a path","leftText":"The outcome fits that path's conditions.","rightTitle":"A uniquely confirmed mechanism","rightText":"Competing mechanisms may still produce the same outcome."},{"leftTitle":"Fitting a seen outcome","leftText":"Changing the model using the same material.","rightTitle":"Improved generalization in a new version","rightText":"Requires corresponding new testing, not inference from training fit."},{"leftTitle":"Append-only write-back","leftText":"Old predictions and new observations both remain.","rightTitle":"Overwriting the original run","rightText":"Destroys the evaluation baseline and may disguise hindsight."}],"boundaries":["Without prior probabilities, do not fabricate retrospective calibration performance.","Do not record only hits while omitting failures and unresolved cases.","One failure may require local revision without automatically proving the whole theory invalid."],"questions":["With prediction 4, baseline 6, and actual 7, which is better under absolute error?","If endpoint 4 is correct but actual 09:00 value 8 exceeds threshold 7, is the whole path successful?","May scenario branches alone be retrospectively declared well-calibrated probabilities?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-9.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.4.9","start_line":2437,"end_line":2440,"startLine":2437,"endLine":2440}],"outputId":"section-11-4-9","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Append actual outcomes and sources to the frozen run, retaining the original prediction and information set.","Check deviations under original targets, windows, scoring, and baselines; do not change definitions to rescue the model.","Distinguish path support, mechanism identification, probability calibration, and a single hit.","Revise the next version along dependencies, stating downgrading or retirement reasons and awaiting new material for testing."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: a complex model loses to a simple baseline","text":"Fictional pending repairs at 08:00 are 6. Beforehand, freeze a model prediction of 4 at 10:00, a carry-forward baseline of 6, and absolute-error scoring. Later, a consistently defined measurement is 7. Model error is |4−7|=3; baseline error |6−7|=1. The baseline wins this occasion. Changed arrivals or processing conditions may be investigated, but rewriting the original prediction to 7 cannot establish perfection.","assumptions":"All values, measurement status, and scoring are teaching constructions. No actual queue experiment occurred, and one sample does not estimate average performance.","boundary":"Probability calibration would additionally require prior probabilities and a multi-case evaluation contract. This example concerns point-prediction error only."},{"title":"Teaching construction: matching final values can conceal an incorrect temporal path","text":"The original prediction is backlog 5 at 09:00 and 4 at 10:00; observations later give 8 and 4. Endpoint error is 0, but intermediate congestion was missed. If the task's protection condition requires no hourly value above 7, the path judgment failed. Endpoint success cannot conceal an intermediate breach.","assumptions":"Threshold and observations are constructed. Hourly checkpoints are stipulated and do not imply no larger peaks between them.","boundary":"If only an endpoint prediction was declared, its hit may remain; it cannot expand into successful prediction of safety throughout."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The final value was guessed correctly, so every mechanism, path, and calibration is confirmed.","effect":"An endpoint hit does not identify intermediate paths, probability calibration, or a unique mechanism.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Changing the old prediction to the correct value using new material completes a learning loop.","effect":"That overwrites the evaluation reference. Retain the old prediction and test a new version separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"With prediction 4, baseline 6, and actual 7, which is better under absolute error?","answer":"Model error is 3, baseline error 1; the baseline wins this occasion, without establishing long-term superiority.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If endpoint 4 is correct but actual 09:00 value 8 exceeds threshold 7, is the whole path successful?","answer":"No. The endpoint task can succeed while path-protection conditions fail. Register them separately.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"May scenario branches alone be retrospectively declared well-calibrated probabilities?","answer":"No. Prior probabilities and corresponding evaluation data are required. Conditional coverage is not probability calibration.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/figures/section-11-4-9.png","alt":"Teaching prior freezing: model prediction 4, carry-forward baseline 6. Consistently defined actual outcome 7 gives absolute errors 3 and 1; the baseline is better this time. Append explanations and revisions, retain the old prediction, and test a new version separately. Numbers are teaching constructions; one comparison establishes neither long-term advantage nor probability calibration.","caption":"Numbers are teaching constructions; one comparison establishes neither long-term advantage nor probability calibration."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns preserve conditions, operations, and output status. The static figure does not indicate a running real system; any future interaction should switch only explicitly stated conditions and update result status correspondingly.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section needs a traceable prior run, consistently defined actual outcomes, and a baseline. Available historical accident reports lack the nine-step forecast archive needed here, so retrospective success narratives are not passed off as write-back evaluation. Two examples explicitly show executable evaluation contracts."},"openQuestions":["Actual forecast records, outcomes, and baselines have not been supplied. No field calibration or reader-effect experiment was performed."],"taskId":"S-11-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2277–2314; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.3: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.4: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.5: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.6: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","11.4.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1970-06-15","locator":"Chapter 4 Part 5, printed pp.4-44,4-46,4-48 (PDF pp.153,155,156); Chapter 5 Finding 45, printed p.5-33 (PDF p.206)","claim_support":["11.4.1: initially unknown accident cause and severity, followed by progressive checking; 11.4.4: power reduced to about 20% of normal and lower cooling-water use; 11.4.6: specific engineering links among power, heat generation, and cooling water; 11.4.7: comparing propulsion options under power and damage conditions."],"limits":["Only listed passages and associated context were read, not the entire 215-page report. It is a retrospective investigation, not this lesson's advance prediction, and does not validate Xikari as a whole."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source definitions and eligibility across sections","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§11.3–11.3.2, Chinese source lines 2245–2276; §9.1, lines 1873–1889; §9.2–9.2.3, lines 1890–1921; §9.7, lines 1983–1992; §10.1, lines 2035–2050; §10.3.1–10.3.3, lines 2074–2085; §12.3, lines 2491–2498; §12.5, lines 2518–2546","claim_support":["Three times, events versus authorized actions, candidate status of actors and circles, and baseline/calibration responsibilities."],"limits":["Theoretical definitions do not replace real object identification or obtain execution permission.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.4.9 Step nine: outcome write-back; Chinese source lines 2365–2368; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"a54f42ef6191eda19dae102fcaef59f53c7f161f02f2e6cd2b54c3821f86b21c","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.5":{"title":"11.5 Propagation, delays, and thresholds","lead":"A message arriving, being understood, changing a mind, and producing action are different changes. Propagation analysis must specify what changed, through which channel, how long effects take, and within which window they fail.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2369–2384; original anchor section-11-5. Short quotation translated from Chinese: “Net effects must not conceal distributional differences.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","The first level is arrival: a system receives a message, presents it, or a person sees it. This supports only the corresponding arrival fact, not changed subsequent transitions. The second is experience–meaning change, such as evidenced belief revision. Retain that change even without action rather than declaring “nothing happened without behavior.” The third concerns changes to resources, behavior, rules, or relations, each registered by material rather than bundled as “effective.”","These levels are not a mandatory linear staircase. Resources can transfer before a person understands, and reading can alter understanding without behavior. Explain cross-channel bridges individually; exposure rates cannot directly become agreement rates or institutional-implementation rates.","The table's first item, channel, asks what carries the process: circuits, logistics, face-to-face exchange, rule implementation, or platform presentation each have objects and interfaces. Closure, filtering, and substitution change who receives what. An email's existence does not establish that its recipient read it; reading does not establish execution authority.","Capacity is measured per unit time and may saturate, queue, or congest. Delay distinguishes arrival from onset: one-second forwarding, one-day understanding, and one-week approval may coexist. No outcome before the predetermined effect window is not immediate proof of ineffectiveness. No response beyond a reliable maximum delay should count as contrary material. If the maximum delay is unknown, invent no deadline.","Thresholds specify conditions for state transitions, potentially with jumps or hysteresis. Loss concerns resource dissipation, information omission, and meaning transformation; the latter two may lack a common percentage measure. Direction reminds us that A affecting B does not imply equally strong B→A influence; opposing effects may also cancel.","Recovery asks how an object returns or enters a new state after a shock. Elastic recovery, cumulative damage, and lock-in are patterns to distinguish, not defaults for all systems. Separate recovery onset, local availability, and complete recovery. Availability of one core service does not instantly clear backlogs and downstream effects.","The same event may have opposite effects on different circles through different channels. The original resource/public-explanation example describes possible mechanisms, not grounds for declaring a group's shame or distrust without actual experiential material. Register by channel and position. Net totals may serve a specified statistical task without hiding burden bearers or protected constraints.","The requirement is responsibility for propagation statements, not complex simulation every time. A receipt or suitable log may suffice to establish document arrival; changed rules require decision, execution, and actual-outcome material. Task evidence requirements cannot be arbitrarily escalated, nor may an easily obtained indicator silently replace the question."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Signal arrival","leftText":"Corresponding facts of receipt, presentation, or being seen.","rightTitle":"Effective state change","rightText":"Requires evidence of subsequent state or transition changes."},{"leftTitle":"Meaning change","leftText":"Evidenced change in belief, interpretation, or experience.","rightTitle":"Behavior change","rightText":"Requires separate material showing action; neither substitutes for the other."},{"leftTitle":"Recovery begins","leftText":"Some capability becomes available again.","rightTitle":"Complete recovery","rightText":"Relevant interfaces, backlogs, and downstream conditions meet declared standards."},{"leftTitle":"Net effect","leftText":"A total under a specified aggregation rule.","rightTitle":"Distributional structure","rightText":"Which positions benefit and which bear losses must still be shown independently."},{"leftTitle":"Before the effect window","leftText":"Temporary absence of outcomes may fit the delay.","rightTitle":"Established ineffectiveness","rightText":"Requires a reasonable predeclared window and adequate detection capability."}],"boundaries":["The same threshold need not be universal. Thresholds, reversal, and hysteresis need object material or explicit constructions.","Experiential change may hold without action, but does not establish rule implementation.","Zero net effect may hide gains on one side and losses on another; value comparison additionally requires normative premises.","No short-term change is not automatically ineffectiveness, but endlessly extending windows cannot protect a model either.","A source report's recovery standard applies only to its declared services and operations, not simultaneous recovery for every customer."],"questions":["How should a changed view after seeing a notice be recorded if no action follows?","In the hysteresis construction, what is the state after 82 falls to 76?","With Q0=4, arrivals 2 and processing 3 per minute, what are the next four steps?","Why does a net reduction of 2 hours not mean everyone benefits?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-5/figures/section-11-5.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The 2017 S3 incident: local and downstream recovery have different times","href":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The 2017 S3 incident: local and downstream recovery have different times","text":"AWS's review of the February 28, 2017 US-EAST-1 incident records partial-request recovery in the index subsystem beginning at 12:26 PST, relevant read/delete/list APIs operating normally by 13:18, and the separate subsystem required for writes recovering at 13:54. Some dependent services had backlogs requiring additional time. This distinguishes recovery onset, interface availability, and full downstream recovery.","sourceLabel":"AWS incident review","sourceUrl":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/","sourceLocator":"Second main-text paragraph, counting “We’d like…” after the title as the first; sentences beginning “The index subsystem…” and “At this point…”","boundary":"A primary operator review, without individual customer logs. It cannot imply simultaneous restoration for all users or estimate a general recovery distribution.","href":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.5","start_line":2441,"end_line":2456,"startLine":2441,"endLine":2456}],"outputId":"section-11-5","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Specify whether the target change concerns arrival, experience–meaning, or resources/behavior/rules/relations, with its observation definition.","Check channel, capacity, delay, threshold, loss, direction, and recovery individually. Supply material for applicable items and reasons for inapplicable ones.","Separate arrival time, possible effect window, and recovery milestones.","Examine distributional consequences across positions and circles; net values cannot replace every component.","Distinguish conditional mechanisms from observed changes and revise paths under contrary signals."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: separate capacity and delay","text":"A fictional information-processing queue starts with 4 messages, receives 2 per minute, and processes at most 3. Under Q_next=max(0,Q+2−3), queue lengths are 3,2,1 in the first three minutes and 0 in the fourth. If arrival additionally requires a fixed 2 minutes before downstream state changes, clearing the queue does not mean the last message's effect has occurred. Queue waiting and effect delay are registered separately.","assumptions":"Arrivals, processing order, constant capacity, and fixed effect delay are teaching assumptions. Processing is lossless and newly arrived messages may be processed in the same minute.","boundary":"Understanding or executing actual messages need not follow fixed minute delays; this example estimates no human response speed."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: hysteresis is not one threshold applied both ways","text":"A fictional device shuts down at T≥80 and, once stopped, restarts only at T≤70, retaining its prior state between them. For 75→82→76→69, states are running→stopped→still stopped→restarted. Recording only current 76 without prior shutdown misclassifies recovery.","assumptions":"80 and 70 are teaching thresholds, not equipment safety standards. Initial operation at 75 is stipulated.","boundary":"Offers no actual device-control advice and does not claim hysteresis in every system."},{"title":"Teaching construction: net burden reduction cannot hide positional differences","text":"The same fictional resource reallocation reduces group A's weekly waiting by 10 hours but increases group B's by 8. If the task declares the groups' hours additive, net reduction is 2 hours, while B still worsens. If B faces a nonwaivable deadline constraint, net reduction 2 does not automatically make the option acceptable.","assumptions":"Values, comparable units, and causal differences are constructed, not measured in actual groups.","boundary":"Whether waiting across people can be added and which weights to use require separate normative explanation. This example authorizes nothing for stakeholders."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Read receipts reach 100%, so the institution is declared implemented.","effect":"Arrival evidence cannot certify execution or actual write-back.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Temperature falls from 82 to 76, below 80, so operation must have resumed.","effect":"The explicit hysteresis construction still requires reaching 70. Historical state changes the current judgment.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The two groups improve by 2 hours in total, so each group benefits.","effect":"Aggregate conclusions do not imply individual or positional improvement; B's waiting still increases by 8 hours.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"How should a changed view after seeing a notice be recorded if no action follows?","answer":"With adequate material, register experience–meaning change, neither erasing it nor promoting it to behavior or rule change.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"In the hysteresis construction, what is the state after 82 falls to 76?","answer":"Still stopped: restart requires 70 or below. The trigger line 80 cannot simply be applied in reverse.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"With Q0=4, arrivals 2 and processing 3 per minute, what are the next four steps?","answer":"Successively 3,2,1,0. Clearing the processing queue does not establish that downstream effect delay has ended.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does a net reduction of 2 hours not mean everyone benefits?","answer":"B waits 8 additional hours. Retain the aggregate statistic without deleting distributional differences or protective constraints.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-5/figures/section-11-5.png","alt":"Arrival evidence: received/seen, not automatic change. Meaning-change evidence: altered understanding/belief, not automatic action. Structural-change evidence: resources/behavior/rules, checked separately. Teaching hysteresis: start at 75 running, 82 stopped; fall to 76, still above restart threshold and remain stopped; fall to 69, satisfy T≤70 and restart. The upper row contains different evidential responsibilities, not mandatory stages. All lower-row thresholds are teaching constructions.","caption":"The upper row contains different evidential responsibilities, not mandatory stages. All lower-row thresholds are teaching constructions."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. The upper row has no compulsory arrows, avoiding an inevitable arrival→meaning→structure sequence. The lower row demonstrates hysteresis with historical state.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The same actual outage explicitly records staged recovery and downstream backlogs, directly matching the distinction between delay and recovery."},"openQuestions":["No per-customer recovery or experience records were obtained; platform recovery milestones do not establish every user's state."],"taskId":"S-11-5","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2315–2330; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.5: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"aws2017","title":"Summary of the Amazon S3 Service Disruption in the Northern Virginia (US-EAST-1) Region","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Retrospective of the 2017 incident; the page gives no clear separate publication date","locator":"Main text paragraph 2; The index subsystem… and At this point…","claim_support":["2017-02-28: 12:26/13:18/13:54 PST mark different recovery milestones; some downstream backlogs required additional recovery time"],"limits":["The operator's account; raw telemetry and all customer records were not independently obtained. Used only as a historical case, not current service status."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.5 Propagation, Delays, and Thresholds; Chinese source lines 2369–2384; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-5/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-5/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-5/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-5/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"5cced0b1dc1311ee0bb900f87f84867a3acc0e537e646538b00fcd6b85a844d7","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.6":{"title":"11.6 Feedback and Cross-Circle Cascades","lead":"Feedback requires earlier output to change a later state through a return channel; a cascade requires a change in one place to cause changes elsewhere through actual interfaces. Two separately valid local proofs may not combine into a valid whole chain.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2385–2402; original anchor: section-11-6; short quotation, translated from the Chinese: “Average edge effects also cannot be multiplied unconditionally.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Feedback hinges on effective change after return. Returned questionnaires, delivered reports, or received alerts first establish arrival; only changes in subsequent states, transition probabilities, or constraints support feedback here. Feedback may harm, oscillate, or amplify errors; a “closed loop” does not imply learning, improvement, or justification.","Cascades cross circles or evidenced object boundaries and require temporal order and channel evidence. Shared members, bridging, shared resources, institutional downward transmission, and network propagation are candidate carriers, not effective merely because drawn. Two groups becoming tense simultaneously may simply share a shock; shared membership alone cannot establish that emotion passed through that member.","The original table's membership cascade requires actual transmission; nonparticipation or unreceived information may block that path. Resource cascades require accounting boundaries, conversions, and losses for occupation, release, and reallocation; resource isolation or alternative sources may sever dependency. Stopping conditions concern the declared channel, without guaranteeing disappearance of every other channel.","Meaning cascades concern how changes in interpretations of identity, legitimacy, trust, and similar matters form at different positions. Failure to arrive or change experiential-meaning states does not establish that meaning cascade. If meaning changes without action, preserve the meaning change and separately examine behavioral consequences. Institutional cascades distinguish appeals, rule decisions, downward execution, and actual write-back; a published but unimplemented rule is a propagation break.","Platform cascades require material on algorithms, visibility selection, and reflexivity; exposure no longer growing or audiences not responding limits the relevant propagation. Exposure growth is not growth in the proportion of support. Users changing behavior because they know they are evaluated may also change the original predictive environment. Platform display counts cannot directly represent social endorsement.","Effect size depends on position, channels, and thresholds; membership size is only one candidate factor. A small bridge position may have large effects if it controls an irreplaceable interface, while a large population may transmit nothing through blocked channels. This is a structural possibility, not the inverted slogan that “small organizations always matter more.”","The original equal-weight two-subgroup counterexample is crucial: in the first group, an event changes the mediator, but the mediator does not change the outcome; in the second, the mediator can change the outcome, but the event does not affect it. Averaged separately, each edge appears to have a half-unit effect. In actual sequence, no subgroup responds on both segments, so the whole chain is zero. Even studies of both segments in the same population may average away interface heterogeneity.","Therefore check whether upstream outputs' objects, states, times, and units enter downstream effective-input domains, where joint responses hold, and whether capacity, delays, offsets, and parallel paths are compatible. Local results compose only under corresponding composability conditions; “same population” cannot replace support for joint responses.","This does not require knowing every microscopic mechanism before recognizing any bounded effect. If a qualified comparison independently identifies a total intervention effect, preserve its task scope and conditions. Where an edge is unknown, established upstream changes and terminal effects also remain separately. This section adds burdens of proof for specific propagation explanations, not cancellation of bounded empirical knowledge."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Feedback","leftText":"Output changes subsequent states or constraints through a return channel.","rightTitle":"Feedback material received","rightText":"Initially establishes only arrival; subsequent change requires separate checking."},{"leftTitle":"Cascade","leftText":"Change triggers changes in other objects along channels.","rightTitle":"Simultaneous change","rightText":"Shared backgrounds and selection effects remain possible explanations."},{"leftTitle":"Average edge effect","leftText":"The population average for each segment.","rightTitle":"Joint response","rightText":"The scope where upstream and downstream segments both hold in the same connectable objects."},{"leftTitle":"Meaning change","leftText":"Experience or interpretation has changed.","rightTitle":"Institutional or behavioral change","rightText":"Requires additional material on execution and actual write-back."},{"leftTitle":"Unknown local mechanism","leftText":"An edge is unidentified.","rightTitle":"Invalid total effect","rightText":"Does not follow; an independent total effect may still hold."}],"boundaries":["Cascade types may coexist; the table is neither mutually exclusive nor exhaustive of all mechanisms.","Stopping one channel does not automatically stop every parallel path.","Feedback may harm a system; return does not automatically acquire positive value, learning, or legitimacy.","Multiplying average effects may hold under additional conditions; this counterexample rejects only an unconditional guarantee.","This construction contains no actual experimental data and borrows no historical case to certify its parameters."],"questions":["What are the two-group construction's local averages and total effect?","Why can A's first segment not connect to B's second?","How should a reliable total effect with unknown mediators be reported?","Does resource isolation mean every cascade stops?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-6/figures/section-11-6.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The 2017 S3 incident: specific dependencies matter more than a generic whole-network cascade","href":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The 2017 S3 incident: specific dependencies matter more than a generic whole-network cascade","text":"The AWS retrospective explains that the storage-placement subsystem depends on the index subsystem, and failed S3 requests affected several services depending on S3. Compared with 11.5's recovery-time focus, this section emphasizes specifying which interface depends on which service instead of treating all simultaneous anomalies as one cascade.","sourceLabel":"AWS incident review","sourceUrl":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/","sourceLocator":"Main text paragraph 1, the sentences beginning “One of these subsystems…” and “Removing a significant portion…”","boundary":"The operator's report of technical dependencies; circle-object eligibility under Part X still requires separate checks. This example only illustrates technical-interface dependencies and does not identify every causal chain for all customers.","href":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.6","start_line":2457,"end_line":2474,"startLine":2457,"endLine":2474}],"outputId":"section-11-6","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["First define the feedback-return target and cross-object cascade boundaries, checking actual order and channels.","Examine required material and stopping conditions separately for member, resource, meaning, institutional, and platform cascades.","Check whether both effects occur in connectable objects, states, and times, rather than comparing means alone.","Calculate or bound joint responses, then examine capacity, losses, and offsets to avoid unconditional multiplication.","Retain independently identified total effects without inventing unidentified propagation edges."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Explicit expansion of the original mathematical counterexample: two halves do not make one quarter","text":"Let groups g=A,B each have weight 1/2, with binary event E∈{0,1}. Define mediator M_g(E)=a_g E and outcome Y_g(M)=b_g M, with no other paths. Group A has (a,b)=(1,0), B has (a,b)=(0,1). The mean event effect on the mediator is (1+0)/2=1/2; setting the mediator from 0 to 1 gives mean outcome effect (0+1)/2=1/2. But the individual difference from the event's natural transmission to the outcome is a_g b_g: 1×0=0 in A and 0×1=0 in B, for mean total difference 0. Multiplying the two means to obtain 1/4 wrongly connects A's first segment to B's second.","assumptions":"Equal weights, determinism, binary inputs, no direct E→Y path, no interpersonal interference, and sequential segments within each group are mathematical assumptions. Setting the mediator is only a mathematical intervention definition; no actual experiment was conducted.","boundary":"“Average effects on both edges” are not mere observed correlations. Even with known local causal contrasts, the joint-response mismatch prevents multiplication in this example."},{"title":"Mathematical supplement: additional structure that permits multiplication","text":"In the same linear chain, the whole-chain effect is mean ab, while mean a times mean b omits a covariance term: mean ab=mean a×mean b+Cov(a,b). In the counterexample, Cov=0−1/4=−1/4. If every object's a is constant a0 within the same allowed domain, then mean ab=a0 mean b=mean a×mean b; this is one sufficient condition. It does not establish real-world homogeneity or handle nonlinearity, interference, or delay mismatch.","assumptions":"A finite equal-weight population with a and b defined as composable linear response coefficients; covariance is simply an algebraic identity.","boundary":"Observed zero covariance in reality need not suffice to identify these causal coefficients; a formal identity is not an estimation strategy."},{"title":"Teaching construction: return does not mean improvement","text":"A fictional system outputs deviation x each time; its return channel multiplies it by 2 and writes the next state x_next=2x. From x0=1 it proceeds 2,4,8. Return changes subsequent states, satisfying the feedback structure while amplifying deviation. If another system only writes x to a read-only log without changing subsequent rules, the log's existence alone cannot establish effective feedback.","assumptions":"Values and the target “reduce deviation” are constructed; no actual control system is simulated.","boundary":"Actual stability requires specific dynamics, delays, and feasible domains. This example only refutes the claim that feedback necessarily improves things."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Both local average causal effects are 1/2, so the whole-chain effect must be 1/4.","effect":"The original two-group construction has zero joint response throughout; check whether upstream and downstream segments concern connectable objects.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"More members necessarily mean greater cross-circle influence.","effect":"A small bridge may control a crucial interface, while a large group may have no effective propagation through blocked channels.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Meaning changed but no action followed, so the meaning cascade should be marked failed and nonexistent.","effect":"Separate action from meaning. Retain supported meaning changes and stop the behavioral chain where it remains unchecked.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What are the two-group construction's local averages and total effect?","answer":"Both local means are 1/2, individual products are all 0, and the total effect is 0; the mean of products differs from the product of means.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can A's first segment not connect to B's second?","answer":"There is no channel transferring the mediator between groups, and the original construction requires a within-object chain. Joining them changes the model.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should a reliable total effect with unknown mediators be reported?","answer":"Retain the bounded total effect and its identification conditions, with internal edges unknown; neither add invented edges nor erase the result.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does resource isolation mean every cascade stops?","answer":"Only the specified resource path is blocked; parallel member or institutional channels require their own examination.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-6/figures/section-11-6.png","alt":"Group A, weight 1/2: E changes M, a=1; A's second segment: M does not change Y, b=0; A's whole chain: a×b=0. Group B, weight 1/2: E does not change M, a=0; B's second segment: M changes Y, b=1; B's whole chain: a×b=0. Mathematical construction: mean a=mean b=1/2, but mean ab=0. Segments cannot be joined across groups.","caption":"Mathematical construction: mean a=mean b=1/2, but mean ab=0. Segments cannot be joined across groups."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. Each row preserves its group, deliberately omitting cross-row arrows. A possible interaction changes a or b and recalculates mean ab, rather than displaying only multiplied means.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Actual service dependencies illustrate channels and interfaces. This is explicitly only a technical-dependency analogy, without directly treating technical modules as human circles."},"openQuestions":["Joint-response support, parallel offsets, and permissions for actual circles require separate material; the historical retrospective supplies no group parameters for this mathematical counterexample."],"taskId":"S-11-6","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2331–2348; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.6: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"aws2017","title":"Summary of the Amazon S3 Service Disruption in the Northern Virginia (US-EAST-1) Region","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Retrospective of the 2017 incident","locator":"Main text paragraph 1; One of these subsystems… and Removing a significant portion…","claim_support":["Storage placement depends on indexing; S3 unavailability affects dependent services, supplying specific interface-dependency material"],"limits":["The operator's account. Technical dependencies do not directly establish human-circle objects; telemetry was not independently obtained and current architecture is not inferred."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.6 Feedback and Cross-Circle Cascades; Chinese source lines 2385–2402; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-6/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-6/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-6/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-6/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e5791669f047d998d1bd261e7f3cab1138588d153e7c2369cf19234f25749ad3","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.7":{"title":"11.7 Branching Path Diagrams","lead":"A path diagram should show which starting point leads to which consequence under what conditions, and what material can stop that route. Arrows are not decoration, and nodes are more than “success” or “failure” labels.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2403–2415; original anchor: section-11-7; short quotation, translated from the Chinese: “A node cannot be both its own cause and its own outcome within the same run.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Nodes first have statuses and lineages. A parent node specifies the previous state from which a branch emerges; condition sets and triggering events or actions explain the step; state differences specify changes in resources, relations, rules, or beliefs; affected clocks mark object time and change timescale. A root without a parent must explicitly be marked root, not assigned a fictional antecedent to complete a field.","Support status specifies observation, inference, scenario, awaiting verification, weakening by contrary material, or another status. Probabilities and rankings require corresponding grounds: two listed branches do not mean equal halves; absent a supportable ranking, mark unordered rather than forcing numbers onto every path. Early and contrary signals provide checkable consequences, while next nodes and termination reasons specify how far a branch can proceed.","A directed acyclic graph, or DAG, is a representation rule for this run, not a denial of real feedback. Unfolding the same system at different times into distinct nodes—state S0, response S1, and post-return S2—with arrows going forward represents a real loop without a state node logically proving itself. Object identity differs from state-version identity.","If every edge goes from an earlier ordinal position to a later one, a directed cycle would require the starting ordinal to return to itself after strict increases, a contradiction. Unfolding by time or explicit causal-progression order therefore guarantees this finite graph is acyclic. This establishes representational consistency only, not the truth of causal arrows. If simultaneous updates lack identifiable order, use explicit concurrency rules or one joint-update step; do not fabricate second-level time gaps.","Factual uncertainty asks whether an event occurred; prioritize checking sources, objects, and times. Mechanistic uncertainty asks why an observation arose, requiring signals that distinguish competing mechanisms. The original table's silence-from-fear or silence-from-strategy example reminds us to retain alternatives, not limit causes to these two. Personality labels for silent people prematurely close an unresolved question.","Choice uncertainty concerns possible cooperation, resistance, exit, or other feasible choices by actors. Preserve conditions, strategies, constraints, and authorization without deciding for them, or assuming free choice makes all bounded behavioral prediction impossible. Exogenous disturbances are handled through scenario ranges, stresses, and recovery conditions. “Exogenous” is relative to the model boundary, not mysterious, unstudied, or forever beyond influence.","All four uncertainties may coexist, but must not be blended into an unexplained “60% risk.” A number summarizing them requires an explicit model, conditional decomposition, dependencies, and evidence. Without these, listing the material each type needs is usually more honest and more useful for choosing what to check next.","Merging makes a graph more compact than a tree, but only if subsequent task-relevant states can merge under the declared contract. Equal stocks with different permissions or debts cannot be merged on stock alone. A path may stop because its horizon ends, the question is answered, key material is missing, the model exceeds its domain, or risk crosses a boundary. Stopping neither deletes the branch nor proves it cannot occur."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Actual feedback loop","leftText":"Later states of the same system are affected by return of earlier outputs.","rightTitle":"Self-causal cycle in the graph","rightText":"One state node loops back to explain itself, violating this section's run representation."},{"leftTitle":"Fact awaiting verification","leftText":"Whether the event occurred is unknown.","rightTitle":"Mechanism awaiting discrimination","rightText":"The event or observation is known, but competing causal explanations remain."},{"leftTitle":"Conditional branch","leftText":"What would occur if a condition holds.","rightTitle":"Probabilistic prediction","rightText":"Has an additional probability contract and grounds for occurrence likelihood."},{"leftTitle":"Stop projection","leftText":"An analytical or protective boundary has been reached.","rightTitle":"Impossible in reality","rightText":"Requires stronger real-world exclusion evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Same object","leftText":"Continuing reference to the same system.","rightTitle":"Same node","rightText":"A state record at a fixed time and version."}],"boundaries":["DAG acyclicity certifies no causation and does not require reality to lack loops.","Silence does not automatically mean fear, strategy, or a personality type; other competing mechanisms remain possible.","Actor choices permit conditional analysis without replacing autonomous choice or valid authorization.","Exogenous is a model-boundary label, not a container for every residual.","Retain unknown rankings; branch counts must not manufacture probabilities."],"questions":["If R→A and R→B, with both A/B at 09:00, is acyclicity violated?","Why can S0→S1→S2 represent feedback without forming a cycle?","If a branch stops for insufficient material, may its outcome be called impossible?","After confirming silence, can we explain it directly as a “fearful personality”?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-7/figures/section-11-7.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.7","start_line":2475,"end_line":2487,"startLine":2475,"endLine":2487}],"outputId":"section-11-7","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Use stable node IDs to record parents, conditions, triggers, state differences, and object clocks.","Specify support status, probability or ranking status, positive and contrary signals, successors, and reasons for termination item by item.","Unroll feedback cycles into new nodes at different times; do not force a causal order onto unknown concurrency.","Distinguish factual, mechanistic, choice, and disturbance branches, and match each to verification or discrimination methods.","Merge only under explicit conditions for state equivalence; retain reasons for stopped or unresolved branches."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a two-branch graph with specified fields","text":"Fictional repair root node R: parent=none; conditions=6 pending repairs at 08:00, 2 arrivals in the next hour; trigger=start this conditional analysis; state difference=none yet; clock=08:00 baseline; support=teaching stipulation; ranking=not applicable; early/contrary signals=subsequent arrival and processing records; next nodes=A, B; termination reason=the root itself does not terminate. A: parent R; condition=processing capacity 1; trigger=if the fault persists; state difference=backlog 6→7; clock=09:00; support=conditional model derivation; probability=unassigned; early signal=1 completion under the same measurement convention; contrary signal=3 completions while arrivals remain 2; next node=none; termination reason=the one-hour window ends. B: parent R; condition=processing capacity 3; trigger=if processing does not slow; state difference=6→5; clock=09:00; support=conditional model derivation; probability=unassigned; early signal=3 completions; contrary signal=1 completion while arrivals remain 2; next node=none; termination reason=the same window ends.","assumptions":"There are no other inflows or outflows; arrivals precede processing. Recorded signals are only a proposed checking design and have not actually been observed.","boundary":"The mutual exclusivity of branches A and B depends on the processing-capacity assumptions; it does not imply equal probabilities. The signals' authenticity must also be checked independently."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: time-unrolled feedback is still a DAG","text":"Define the deviation at S0 as 1 and the rule as S_next=2S; the returning effect enters the next state only on each round. Unroll this as S0(time 0, value 1)→S1(time 1, value 2)→S2(time 2, value 4). The order strictly increases along every edge, so the graph is acyclic while expressing how an earlier output affects a later state. Collapsing the three states into a single node S and drawing S→S loses each round's value and clock.","assumptions":"The discrete single-step rule, times, and values are constructed; the unrolling is finite and contains only two steps.","boundary":"Acyclicity is a formal property of the graph. It neither proves that the rule holds in reality nor guarantees system stability."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the same statement “the machine was not turned on” can involve different uncertainties","text":"A fictional record says “the machine was not turned on.” If the record's truth is uncertain, this calls for factual verification. If non-operation is established but its cause is unknown, possibilities include a power outage, an operator's choice, or a safety interlock: mechanistic and choice branches. If we project whether tomorrow's power supply will be affected by heavy rain, that is a disturbance scenario within the model boundary. These should respectively correspond to equipment logs, material distinguishing possible causes, people's feasible choices, and weather conditions, rather than all receiving the label “high risk.”","assumptions":"All situations are teaching stipulations; no actual weather or equipment facts are asserted.","boundary":"Verification channels require permission. Do not access private personal material without authorization merely to fill in the graph."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"To satisfy the DAG requirement, real-world feedback must be deleted from the analysis.","effect":"It can be unrolled over time into new nodes, retaining feedback while avoiding a node being its own cause.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Neither path has an evidential advantage, so each has a 50% probability.","effect":"An absence of ranking or comparable evidence does not generate numerical probabilities.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Drawing every edge in temporal order already proves causation.","effect":"Temporal order is only a necessary recording obligation; mechanisms and competing accounts still need evidence.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If R→A and R→B, with both A/B at 09:00, is acyclicity violated?","answer":"No. Mutually exclusive branch nodes can share a time point; what matters is that no directed path returns to itself.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can S0→S1→S2 represent feedback without forming a cycle?","answer":"The same object has different nodes at different times. Returning effects influence a subsequent state rather than returning to the same state identity.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a branch stops for insufficient material, may its outcome be called impossible?","answer":"No. Record only that the current projection has stopped and what material is missing; ruling it out in reality requires separate evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After confirming silence, can we explain it directly as a “fearful personality”?","answer":"No. Fear, strategy, failure to understand, inability to respond, and other mechanisms must be distinguished using material; a label must not close the inquiry.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-7/figures/section-11-7.png","alt":"S0 | time 0, teaching deviation 1, parent: none; S1 | time 1, deviation 2, parent: S0; S2 | time 2, deviation 4, parent: S1. First identify the branch type: factual/mechanistic, choice/disturbance. Match the discrimination obligations: verification/distinguishing signals, strategy authorization/stress scenarios. Retain conditional status, do not force probabilities, and state reasons for stopping. The top row is a teaching construction using S_next=2S; formal acyclicity does not prove a real mechanism or stability.","caption":"The top row is a teaching construction using S_next=2S; formal acyclicity does not prove a real mechanism or stability."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram. Interaction has not been implemented; references below to selecting, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Time unrolling changes only the node representation, without deleting returning effects. A future interaction could select the number of unrolled rounds, but must not thereby claim to have observed the future.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section specifies the path data structure and types of uncertainty; formal graphs and explicit constructions can be checked directly. Retrospective accounts of historical accidents lack the complete node fields for the frozen run used in this lesson. They cannot supply invented contemporaneous probabilities, contrary signals, or branch authorization."},"openQuestions":["A concrete real-world path graph still needs material on its frozen starting point, event sources, node support, and execution permissions. This lesson does not claim to have generated a digital twin of a real system."],"taskId":"S-11-7","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, Chinese source full-text lines 2349–2361; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.7: terminology, contracts, argumentative obligations, and source-text boundaries; does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.7 Branching path graph; Chinese source lines 2403–2415; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-7/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"c64f3b0873b8570360d58701335e66ce40bf6396c8b5306e4eb7db0601bb4cde","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.8":{"title":"11.8 Variable candidate ledger","lead":"What cannot be explained must be retained, but naming it does not amount to finding its cause. A candidate ledger turns “perhaps something is missing” into a defined research question with competing explanations that can be rejected.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2416–2431; source anchor section-11-8; short quotation translated from Chinese: “cannot autonomously determine that a candidate is true in reality.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical draft.","A residual exists relative to the current variable set, rules, and observation convention. If the model predicts 7 and the record says 8, first retain the difference of 1 and its provenance. It does not uniquely indicate a new entity, personality, or social mechanism. Mismeasurement of existing variables, misaligned clocks, and omitted flows can all produce the discrepancy. Deleting the residual hides failure; immediately assigning it a real variable name and value manufactures a false explanation.","The ledger's first group of fields records the basis for proposing a candidate: source residual, name, possible type, and possible channel. The name should identify a distinguishable candidate, such as “unrecorded arrivals” rather than “mysterious resistance.” Types can include a recording process, an object state, or a mechanism hypothesis; the type itself may remain unverified. The source residual must connect to a concrete run and result. “Something just feels wrong” cannot serve as independent evidence.","The second group concerns test design: observable implications, competing candidates, and a minimal test. Observable implications explain which lawfully obtained material should differ from competing candidates if the candidate holds. Minimal does not mean careless: it means obtaining a distinction sufficient to change the current judgment without expanding risk. If all candidates predict the same signal, more of that signal may still provide no discrimination.","The third group concerns governance and status: privacy risks, current status, evidence, upgrade conditions, and rejection conditions. Record proposed, under testing, supported candidate, rejected, and retired separately. Rejection means the candidate has failed the declared checks. Retirement may also occur because the task no longer needs it or its applicability window has ended; it must not invariably be interpreted as “never existed.” When new evidence changes the status, retain versions instead of overwriting its provenance.","A supported candidate remains a candidate; it next enters the relevant object or variable contract. A claim about a stable personality requires corresponding evidence of persistence across situations. A claim that a circle object is established requires separate checks of the relevant object qualifications; a further claim of grouping gain requires G1 qualification checks. A causal-mechanism claim requires causal identification and material addressing competing explanations. A single improvement in fit cannot clear every status requirement at once.","Systematic residuals in the original representation should trigger multiple distinguishable candidates, rather than one all-purpose variable absorbing the error. Preserve the position and meaning of a participant's account: it can be important material about experience and claims, with checkable parts checked separately. It is neither automatically worthless noise nor automatically the complete facts or another person's inner state. Repetition across cases can suggest a mechanism candidate, but similar accounts may share sources, selection methods, or backgrounds and cannot immediately be treated as a general rule.","Candidates produced by model search should be frozen for the next round of testing, avoiding the pretense of never having seen the result after selecting the best model on that very result. AI suggestions can also generate and compare candidates and, where evidence, purposes, and explicit normative premises allow, support bounded judgments or analytical recommendations. Fluency cannot verify reality, nor can AI grant anyone permission to execute an action.","A candidate proposed after the outcome can improve the next model version but cannot retrospectively support the original prediction. If the original model failed, adding “unobserved resistance” to explain the outcome leaves the old prediction subject to its original contract. Whether the new model adds prospective value requires corresponding subsequent testing; explanatory success cannot directly become predictive success.","The framework can actively detect inadequacy in the current representation and design tests with acceptable risk, but cannot autonomously declare a candidate true. Real-world testing also remains subject to authorization and protection constraints. If the material needed for discrimination cannot lawfully be obtained, retain the unknown, fall back to a bounded answer, or choose a commonly feasible option that does not depend on that unknown; do not expand surveillance in the name of research.","The ledger should separately record “object facts have support” and “grouping gain is supported.” A circle suggested by a residual remains a candidate. New documentation may establish object identity but cannot thereby establish predictive gain. Task gain from a grouping likewise cannot fabricate a personality, unified will, or real-world permissions."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Candidate name","leftText":"Gives the hypothesis a stable reference so it can be tested.","rightTitle":"Real-world existence and value","rightText":"Require corresponding observation and identification; naming does not provide them."},{"leftTitle":"Supported candidate","leftText":"Has passed certain bounded checks.","rightTitle":"Stable personality or causal mechanism established","rightText":"Requires different and more specific contracts; no automatic upgrade is allowed."},{"leftTitle":"Participant's account","leftText":"Material with a position, meaning, and checkable parts.","rightTitle":"Complete factual picture","rightText":"Unknowns, memory errors, and material from other positions may remain."},{"leftTitle":"Rejection","leftText":"Unsupported under the specified test or weakened by contrary material.","rightTitle":"Retired","rightText":"The end of the current use or applicability window can also lead to discontinuation."},{"leftTitle":"Invention after the outcome","leftText":"An observed outcome is used to generate a new hypothesis.","rightTitle":"Support for a prior prediction","rightText":"Must be based on the assumptions and information set frozen at the time."}],"boundaries":["Candidate statuses are not a maturity ladder that advances automatically through a workflow; candidates can split, be downgraded, or be withdrawn.","Missing material does not imply a personality problem, and refusal to disclose does not make a candidate true.","A participant's experience cannot be wholly replaced by objective indicators, nor can it stand for everyone's experience.","Selecting and evaluating on the same data creates selection bias; algorithmic complexity cannot conceal this.","AI's ability to make bounded analytical judgments does not mean it can create facts or authorization."],"questions":["C1 and C2 both yield 8. What should a minimal discrimination examine?","If completions were overcounted by 1 this hour, can we conclude that someone has a stable deceitful personality?","If h=−4 changes 10 to 6, does the old prediction error become zero?","Without access permission, may records be obtained directly on grounds of research necessity?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-8/figures/section-11-8.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Adams's 1846 paper: explicitly stating a residual explanation as a hypothesis","href":"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/7/9/149/983965"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Adams's 1846 paper: explicitly stating a residual explanation as a hypothesis","text":"The publisher's primary paper metadata records that J. C. Adams submitted a paper on irregularities in Uranus's motion on November 13, 1846. Its title explicitly treats perturbation by a more distant planet as a hypothesis and discusses its mass, orbit, and position. This section uses only that evidence as a historical-document example of a candidate explanation with a defined object and observable implications; it does not reconstruct the discovery process.","sourceLabel":"Publication metadata for J. C. Adams's original paper","sourceUrl":"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/7/9/149/983965","sourceLocator":"Publisher page: title, author, submission-date footnote, volume, issue, and pages: MNRAS 7(9), 149–152; DOI 10.1093/mnras/7.9.149","boundary":"The publication metadata was checked; the PDF full text was not successfully obtained. The title does not justify claims that all contemporary calculations were correct, all competing candidates were excluded, or predictions occurred in a particular order. Specific historical facts about Neptune's discovery and observational confirmation lie outside this material's support.","href":"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/7/9/149/983965"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.8","start_line":2488,"end_line":2503,"startLine":2488,"endLine":2503}],"outputId":"section-11-8","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Connect the residual to the frozen model, observations under the same convention, and provenance; do not delete or immediately explain it away.","Propose competing candidates, each with a definition, type, possible channel, and observable differences.","Design a necessary minimal test with acceptable risk, specifying upgrade and rejection conditions first.","Update candidate status according to actual evidence; support still leads into the relevant object, variable, or mechanism contract.","After-the-fact candidates serve only new versions and do not backfill old predictions; separate recommendations from execution authorization."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: an executable but unexecuted candidate ledger entry","text":"Fictional run V1: starting value 6, 2 recorded arrivals, 1 recorded completion, prediction 7, subsequent record 8 under the same convention, residual +1. Candidate C1 is named “unrecorded arrivals”; type=recording process; channel=not entered into the count; observable implication=independent original arrival documentation shows 3 rather than 2; competitors=C2, completions overcounted by 1, and C3, an erroneous endpoint record. Minimal test=check original arrival and completion records for this hour within existing authorization and necessary scope; privacy risk=records may contain names, so remove irrelevant identifiers first; status=proposed; evidence=the residual only, without independent support; upgrade condition=independently checkable material supports 3 actual arrivals and excludes duplicates; rejection condition=reliable, complete arrival material shows only 2 with no omissions. If access permission is not obtained, do not execute the test; retain proposed status.","assumptions":"All numbers, records, and permission scenarios are teaching constructions. This is only a plan; no one's actual records were accessed.","boundary":"If the original documentation is itself incomplete, “not found” cannot directly reject C1. An upgrade supports only the recording explanation for this window."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: two candidates fit, so the result alone does not distinguish them","text":"C1 assumes 3 actual arrivals and 1 completion, giving endpoint 6+3−1=8. C2 assumes 2 arrivals and 0 actual completions, giving endpoint 6+2−0=8. Both explain the observed 8. Repeatedly showing endpoint 8 cannot distinguish them; independent arrival or completion information is needed. If reliable, complete material later establishes exactly 2 arrivals and 0 completions, C2 receives bounded support. This does not establish that an employee is persistently dishonest or that all completion records are unreliable.","assumptions":"There are no other flows or recording errors; the reliability and completeness of the independent material are explicitly stipulated by the construction.","boundary":"The two candidates could also coexist. Here, an error of 1 from each would add up to an endpoint of 9; cancellation would require another error in the opposite direction. The candidate list is not assumed exhaustive."},{"title":"Teaching construction: creating a parameter from the outcome still does not make it the original prediction","text":"A fictional model originally predicts 10; the actual result is 6. Defining a correction term h=−4 after the outcome allows 10+h=6. But without an independent definition and observable implications, h merely renames the residual. A candidate h with mechanistic meaning can be retained for the next round. The original prediction error remains 4 and cannot be recorded as 0.","assumptions":"Both the numbers and the model are constructed; no actual historical effect was estimated.","boundary":"The symbol h is not automatically a G3 historical increment. Nor does this imply that all residual corrections lack value; their definition and subsequent testing are what matter."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A new variable absorbs all old errors, so it is already a real mechanism.","effect":"Its fitting ability may come from arbitrary degrees of freedom, without distinguishable implications or independent testing.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Participants describe subjective feelings, so their accounts never count as material.","effect":"Experience and meaning are themselves relevant objects. Preserving their position and support scope does not make them the complete factual picture.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The candidate written by AI sounds most expert, so it can be upgraded without observation.","effect":"Language quality does not verify reality; the candidate contract and actual evidence are still required.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"C1 and C2 both yield 8. What should a minimal discrimination examine?","answer":"Examine arrival or completion records corresponding to the candidate's implications, from appropriately independent sources and the same window, instead of repeatedly examining only endpoint 8.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If completions were overcounted by 1 this hour, can we conclude that someone has a stable deceitful personality?","answer":"No. Recording mechanisms, attribution of responsibility, motives, and persistence across situations all require independent material.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If h=−4 changes 10 to 6, does the old prediction error become zero?","answer":"It does not become zero; the original error remains 4. The new candidate can only undergo subsequent testing as a new version.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Without access permission, may records be obtained directly on grounds of research necessity?","answer":"No. Keep it unresolved or use already authorized, lower-risk material. A need for information does not create execution permission.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-8/figures/section-11-8.png","alt":"Teaching residual +1: prediction 7, record 8; first check conventions and provenance. C1: unrecorded arrivals; C2: overcounted completions; both can yield 8. Minimal distinguishing material: original arrival/completion records, necessary and authorized. Proposed/under testing: retain unresolved status when evidence is missing; do not assign truth values arbitrarily. Supported or rejected: follow predefined distinguishable conditions; splitting and retirement are possible. Test again in the next version: support still requires the relevant contract; do not backfill old predictions. The numbers are teaching constructions. Status arrows indicate possible continuations of work, not a guarantee that the candidate will gain support.","caption":"The numbers are teaching constructions. Status arrows indicate possible continuations of work, not a guarantee that the candidate will gain support."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Candidates can return to unresolved, split, or retire; the figure shows principal responsibilities without presenting automatic advancement.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The historical paper's original title and submission metadata are verifiable and establish the explicit hypothetical status of its candidate explanation. The public full text was not obtained, so it is only a bounded bibliographic example; numbers and confirmation processes are not invented."},"openQuestions":["The Adams paper's PDF was not obtained; calculation steps and observational confirmation are not reconstructed. The candidate-ledger example performed no real record verification."],"taskId":"S-11-8","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2362–2377; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.8: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"adams1846","title":"Adams: a hypothetical explanation of irregularities in Uranus's motion (original-paper metadata)","url":"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/7/9/149/983965","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1846-11-13","locator":"Title, author, submission footnote dated 1846-11-13; MNRAS 7(9):149–152; DOI 10.1093/mnras/7.9.149","claim_support":["The paper explicitly hypothesizes perturbation by a more distant planet and studies its mass, orbit, and position; submission date is verifiable."],"limits":["Only metadata was read; the full PDF was not obtained. This cannot establish calculation quality, discovery priority, or the complete observational-confirmation process."],"access_status":"metadata_only"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.8 Variable-candidate ledger; Chinese source lines 2416–2431; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-8/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-8/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-8/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-8/diagrams.md","editionNote":"The relevant explanation in this section has been revised for v2; the original research attachments and the scope of external-source review retain their historical status.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"be7eca4f3ebbbedf1ab7222b86fd86fe0e2771e54f0c9797ed336d1c059c296b","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.9":{"title":"11.9 Scenarios, counterfactuals, and simulation","lead":"“What would happen under these conditions,” “what if it had been otherwise,” and “run the rules” are three tasks. All are useful, but a calculated alternative story does not become historical fact through detail, fluency, or decimal places.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2432–2443; source anchor section-11-9; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “One cannot merely delete an unwanted cause while retaining all its consequences.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Scenarios start from explicit conditions and ask which paths could follow if they hold, without asserting that they do. Counterfactuals replace events or mechanisms in an actual situation and reassess outcomes, specifying retained background, changed operation, and downstream conditions updated accordingly. Simulation executes explicit rules and parameters, serving scenarios, counterfactuals, or purely mathematical tests. The three can overlap while retaining different statuses.","Counterfactual comparability does not mean fixing everything. Preserve background that truly should remain, while reassessing beliefs, resources, commands, relations, and downstream behavior affected by the replaced event. Removing an announcement but retaining attendance and execution caused solely by it mixes worlds. Deliberately fixing a mediator poses a different explicit intervention question; it cannot remain the full natural consequences of removing the announcement.","Information available at the time is also a model condition. After replacing a choice, actors may no longer receive original information and may choose different subsequent strategies. Counterfactual actors cannot know actual historical outcomes in advance and therefore behave perfectly. Supported short-range branches do not establish a determinate decades-long alternate history; distant adaptation, institutional change, and new disturbances often enlarge compatible answers.","Ordinary people's actions, endpoint discretion, and organizational constraints may jointly enter process explanations without forced, unidentified causal percentages. Calling two factors important does not estimate each at half. Interaction, path dependence, and different counterfactual definitions may prevent a unique additive allocation of contributions.","Simulation precision has two meanings: numerical error in executing rules, and support for those rules in reality. More digits or samples may reduce the first without supplying unknown mechanisms or parameters. Interval inputs should yield conditional ranges or sensitivity results; an interval without a probability model is not a confidence interval and does not default to a uniform distribution.","The source gives a mechanism-equivalence counterexample: observations always X=M=Y may arise from X→M→Y or from X separately affecting M and Y. Both answer observations identically but differ when M is directly set. More observations of the same kind may estimate common variation more precisely without logically distinguishing the mechanisms.","Answer strength depends on whether compatible mechanisms agree under the changed operation. Agreement permits a single answer within the model scope; disagreement requires feasible answer sets, conditional bounds, or reversal conditions. Direction is robust only if it agrees across the entire reasonable mechanism set, not merely most generated models. If candidates are not exhausted or sufficiently bounded, state the set's limits.","If independent material identifies a bounded treatment's total effect, an unidentified mediator does not invalidate it. This is compatible with the counterexample: what is missing is one mediator-intervention answer, not necessarily evidence for other questions. Register counterfactuals question by question rather than labeling a model “all knowable” or “all unknowable.”","“Remove a person who later holds the position” differs from “this person never existed.” The former can preserve previously developed skills, signed contracts, and others' memories, then model adaptation to departure; the latter changes earlier starting points and all dependent processes. Short-term role-replacement analysis is not a historical answer about lifelong nonexistence, and unidentified counterfactuals cannot quantify actual responsibility."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Scenario","leftText":"Develops paths under given conditions.","rightTitle":"Factual description","rightText":"Reports occurred, sourced events."},{"leftTitle":"Counterfactual under the natural operation","leftText":"Consequences of the changed event update through mechanisms.","rightTitle":"Separate intervention fixing a mediator","rightText":"May be asked separately, explicitly as a different operation."},{"leftTitle":"Numerical computational precision","leftText":"More accurate calculation under given rules.","rightTitle":"Real-mechanism support","rightText":"Depends on empirical evidence and assumptions, not computing power."},{"leftTitle":"Observational equivalence","leftText":"Two mechanisms agree on current observations.","rightTitle":"Interventional or counterfactual equivalence","rightText":"They also agree on the specified operation, a stronger condition."},{"leftTitle":"Short-term departure","leftText":"Preserves earlier history and examines later changes.","rightTitle":"Never existed","rightText":"Changes earlier starting points and adaptation, a different question."}],"boundaries":["Detailed historical narrative does not automatically identify mechanisms needed after replacing a key event.","A robust direction conditional on models remains bounded by the model set and applicable domain.","Without probability grounds, report neither falsely precise percentages nor causal shares.","Deliberately fixing a mediator is not always wrong; the error is hiding the changed operation while claiming the same counterfactual.","Any intervention actually implemented still requires separate authorization; this section analyzes conditions only."],"questions":["At X=1 with M forced to 0, what Y do models A and B give?","How does B2's range change when c narrows from [1,3] to [2,3]?","Is deliberately fixing attendance always impermissible?","Does an unknown mediator effect revoke an independently supported total effect?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9/figures/section-11-9.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.9","start_line":2504,"end_line":2515,"startLine":2504,"endLine":2515}],"outputId":"section-11-9","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["First declare the work's status: conditional scenario, specific counterfactual operation, or simulation of explicit rules.","List what stays and changes, tracing effects on information, resources, relations, and downstream behavior.","Determine the still-compatible mechanism set and compute each mechanism's answer to the same operation.","Agreement yields bounded results; disagreement yields ranges and reversal conditions, without decimal precision hiding unknowns.","Preserve independently identified total effects and separately list material needed to distinguish mediator mechanisms."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Expanding the source's mathematical counterexample: same observations, different mediator interventions","text":"Let X be binary and exogenous. Model A: M=X, Y=M; model B: M=X, Y=X. Naturally, both give (0,0,0) at X=0 and (1,1,1) at X=1. For a unit with actual X=1, directly set M=0 while retaining X=1: A uses Y=M and gives 0; B uses Y=X and gives 1. The counterfactual answer set is therefore {0,1}. More observations of (0,0,0) and (1,1,1) still cannot distinguish them; a design discriminating effects of M or separately grounded structural assumptions is needed.","assumptions":"Deterministic structural equations with no other variables, the same unit's exogenous X retained, and direct setting of M replacing its equation. All are mathematical constructions, without real intervention.","boundary":"Establishes only that observations fail to identify this M intervention, not that all causal questions are unanswerable. Real mechanisms may extend beyond A and B."},{"title":"Mathematical supplement: a particular question may have the same answer","text":"Within the set containing only A and B above, changing the operation to setting X from 0 to 1 gives Y from 0 to 1 in both, a difference of 1. Thus “changing X” has the same answer in these two explicit models, while “forcing M=0 at X=1” does not. Retain the condition “only within these two structural models”; observing X=M=Y alone cannot exclude other confounded models or supply identification evidence for a real total effect.","assumptions":"Uses only the two declared models, no other candidates, with the corresponding equation interventions allowed.","boundary":"A real total-effect claim still needs an independently qualified design or sufficient structural assumptions."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: removing an announcement cannot retain attendance caused solely by it","text":"Let fictional binary announcement A, attendance N, and execution Y follow N=A and Y=N, with actual A=N=Y=1. Naturally removing the announcement sets A=0 and recalculates N=0, Y=0. Additionally forcing N=1 gives Y=1, but changes the question to a double intervention: “no announcement, with another means of forcing attendance,” not removal alone.","assumptions":"The announcement as sole attendance cause and attendance as sole execution condition are constructed assumptions, not applicable to any actual organization.","boundary":"Reality may contain alternative notices or spontaneous attendance; include those mechanisms instead of copying this example's determinate conclusion."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: parameter intervals limit output precision","text":"A fictional two-hour queue starts B0=6, with arrivals fixed at 2/hour and processing c unknown in [1,3]. Within this range no empty-queue truncation occurs, so B2=6+2×2−2c=10−2c, giving B2∈[4,8]. Reporting 6.000 has no more grounds than [4,8]; only independent evidence narrowing c to [2,3] narrows the range to [4,6].","assumptions":"c is a continuous flow rate fixed over two hours, not integer completions of indivisible items; the queue stays nonnegative throughout this range.","boundary":"The interval is a constructed admissible domain, not a real confidence interval; no distribution for c is given."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Two mechanisms fit equally well, so they answer every intervention identically.","effect":"The source models A and B give 0 and 1 respectively under do(M=0), X=1; observational equivalence does not guarantee operational equivalence.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Removing an announcement while retaining attendance solely caused by it fairly holds other conditions constant.","effect":"This is a different operation additionally fixing the mediator, not natural removal of the announcement.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A million simulations yielding three decimal places increase the mechanism's empirical credibility.","effect":"Computational stability does not replace empirical support; wrong models and unknown parameters must remain visible.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"At X=1 with M forced to 0, what Y do models A and B give?","answer":"A gives 0, B gives 1; current observations of common change cannot uniquely answer this operation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How does B2's range change when c narrows from [1,3] to [2,3]?","answer":"From [4,8] to [4,6], using B2=10−2c; narrowing a real interval requires genuinely new evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is deliberately fixing attendance always impermissible?","answer":"A different explicit intervention question may be asked; the double-intervention result cannot impersonate natural consequences of removing only the announcement.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does an unknown mediator effect revoke an independently supported total effect?","answer":"No. Preserve each question's evidence scope; the unknown mediator remains to be distinguished.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-9/figures/section-11-9.png","alt":"Model A: M=X, Y=M, observed X=M=Y; retain X=1 and directly set M=0, replacing M's equation; A gives Y=0 through the mediator. Model B: M=X, Y=X, observed X=M=Y; the same operation retains X=1 and directly sets M=0; B gives Y=1, still depending on X. Mathematical expansion of the source counterexample; {0,1} is nonunique within the two-model set, not two answers with equal probabilities.","caption":"Mathematical expansion of the source counterexample; {0,1} is nonunique within the two-model set, not two answers with equal probabilities."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Two rows compare the same operation. A future switch between X and M interventions must display the operation definition simultaneously without changing natural-observation status.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section centers on formal identifiability and counterfactual-operation definitions; the source's two-mechanism counterexample is fully recalculable. No adequate structural model and counterfactual-discrimination material for a real event was obtained, so historical outcomes or retrospective success narratives do not impersonate identified alternative history."},"openQuestions":["Real counterfactuals require comparable conditions, model scope, and discriminating designs; no person's percentage causal responsibility is assigned and no mediator intervention performed."],"taskId":"S-11-9","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2378–2389; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.9: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.9 Scenarios, counterfactuals, and simulation; Chinese source lines 2432–2443; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"1e309419d3e6b8e4cafd3a73a6ed9e54e5619e1afc45b9d9a7b6f9ddad51b26b","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.9.1":{"title":"11.9.1 Multi-order recursive future projection","lead":"Advancing several steps, using a simulated future as a new starting point, and having modeled people anticipate others' responses are three separately switchable operations. Calling them all “recursion” does not mean more repetition approaches reality.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2444–2451; source anchor section-11-9-1; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “The generation layer may be bold; the state layer must be strict.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Multistep temporal rolling advances states within one run, such as 08:00→09:00→10:00. Simulation-state reentry starts a child run from an explicitly selected simulated node in the parent, posing a new question for this order. Anticipatory reflexivity occurs inside the model: actors anticipate a more distant future or others' responses and change present strategies. It is not the analyst rerunning a program.","Orthogonality means these can exist separately. A numerical queue may roll through hours without actor expectations or child runs; a one-step game may anticipate opponent responses without a long horizon; reusing a forecast inventory state to analyze a new repair strategy permits reentry without psychological expectations. The task determines which dimensions reality needs.","Three orders are the default organizational responsibilities for exploratory projection: first, direct consequences and competing paths; second, changes to action sets, resources, relations, and adaptation; third, changes to institutionalization, lock-in, reversal, and generative conditions. These are not three time points—the first order may span several—nor natural stages, capability levels, or ranks of people.","Parent §11.9 distinguishes scenarios, counterfactuals, and simulation; this section adds limits on reentering future models. §11.9.2 specifies lineage and recasting audits to prevent simulated nodes becoming reality; §11.9.3 governs branching, merging, and pruning; §11.9.4 specifies early stopping. Their four outputs have separate responsibilities, not replaceable by “recurse three times.”","Bold generation expands supported mechanism spaces, competing paths, and preparation for high-consequence possibilities, while permitting clear judgments within evidence. It is not unlimited invention, refusal to conclude, or stronger rhetoric. Strict states preserve source, time, object, and unknowns. Evidence does not upgrade itself: model outputs cannot circulate as new real observations. Separate action authorization means imagined options are not actual execution.","Stop if no next-order question is needed. Summing known accounts, without an independent adaptation or institutional question, should not force a social story to complete three orders. Continue only if a new order answers a previously unanswered question genuinely needed for the task; computational depth itself is not an increment."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Temporal rolling","leftText":"Advances multiple time points in the same run.","rightTitle":"Simulation reentry","rightText":"Starts a child run from a parent simulation state."},{"leftTitle":"Anticipatory reflexivity","leftText":"Actors' expectations within the model alter present strategy.","rightTitle":"Analyst recomputation","rightText":"Rerunning a program does not mean actors know or respond."},{"leftTitle":"Three-order responsibilities","leftText":"Direct consequences, adaptive changes, and institutional/generative conditions.","rightTitle":"Three natural stages","rightText":"The source proposes no such universal stage theory."},{"leftTitle":"Bold generation","leftText":"Explores reasonable alternatives and high-consequence branches.","rightTitle":"Evidence elevation","rightText":"Still requires real material; it does not rise with output count."}],"boundaries":["Do not force recursion on static questions; three orders are not a performance quota to fill.","Without actor material, do not invent their expectations or choices.","A child run still starts from conditional simulation, not current reality."],"questions":["From B0 to B2 and then a new R1, which steps are rolling and which reentry?","Does merely adding inventory require a third-order institutional analysis?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/figures/section-11-9-1.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.9.1","start_line":2516,"end_line":2523,"startLine":2516,"endLine":2523}],"outputId":"section-11-9-1","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["First separately declare temporal rolling, simulation reentry, and anticipatory reflexivity.","Give each order a task-specific independent question, not a date or rank.","When reentering a parent simulation node, preserve its conditional status and all relevant unknowns.","Generate reasonable competing paths and allow bounded clear judgments; generation produces neither real evidence nor execution permission.","Stop without a new question or adequate conditions; do not add stories to fill three orders."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: three independent switches","text":"A fictional queue starts at B0=6, with 2 arrivals and 3 processed per hour. Advancing run R for two hours gives B1=5 and B2=4: this is time advancement. Starting a new R1 from simulated B2=4 and asking ‘if subsequent processing capacity rises to 4, what happens one hour later?’ gives 4+2−4=2: this is reentry. A different model in which workers anticipate tomorrow's backlog and adjust today's feasible schedule includes anticipatory reflexivity; that behavioral rule requires separate grounds and does not follow automatically from the first two calculations.","assumptions":"All values and rules are teaching stipulations; neither increased capacity nor workers' expectations actually occurred. The latter only illustrates a model type.","boundary":"B2=4 does not become a measured 4 by entering R1, and capacity 4 does not mean a schedule change is authorized."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the same order is not one time point","text":"The first order may compare direct backlogs from faults lasting one or two hours; the second asks whether scheduling changes and how existing qualifications, consent, and resources constrain it; only the third asks whether long-term maintenance rules change. Each order may have a different window, and the analysis may stop at the second if third-order materials are absent.","assumptions":"A fictional repair question used only to organize questions, without presupposing actual choices or institutional change.","boundary":"Order does not determine outcome direction, still less make improved long-term rules inevitable."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Simulating 100 consecutive time points has completed 100 orders of recursion.","effect":"Time-step counts, reentry orders, and anticipatory reflexivity are different dimensions.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Several models generate the same future, so it has become real-world evidence.","effect":"Shared assumptions can repeatedly generate the same output without adding real observations.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"From B0 to B2 and then a new R1, which steps are rolling and which reentry?","answer":"B0→B1→B2 within R is time advancement; using simulated B2 to start R1 is reentry. Neither is a new real-world observation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does merely adding inventory require a third-order institutional analysis?","answer":"Not necessarily; without an independent next-order question, recursion should not be forced.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/figures/section-11-9-1.png","alt":"Time advancement: 08→09→10 within R advances the same run. Simulation reentry: take a simulated node from R and open child run R1. Anticipatory reflexivity: anticipated futures within the model change current strategy. These three cards are parallel dimensions; none automatically implies the other two.","caption":"These three cards are parallel dimensions; none automatically implies the other two."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. The three cards express distinct responsibilities this section must preserve, not implemented software or actual processes. If future interaction switches assumptions, conditions and inherited unknowns must be shown simultaneously.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section distinguishes three model operations and responsibilities for recursive work; mathematical constructions suffice to check their logical independence. No reviewable actual multilevel run lineage was obtained, and retrospective historical narratives are not presented as actual recursive experiments."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-9-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 2390—2413 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["11.9.1: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.2: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.3: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.4: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"formal","title":"Historical Chinese research manuscript: the source's task sufficiency, dynamic interfaces, and bounded extrapolation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source, §11.3.1, lines 2261—2268; §11.7, lines 2349—2361; §11.9, lines 2378—2389","claim_support":["Reentry creates no new real-world observation; conditional branches retain unknowns; temporal unfolding and object identity; interface sufficiency is tied to the current task"],"limits":["The bounded derivation constructed here replaces neither real calibration nor execution authority.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.9.1 Multi-order recursive future projection; Chinese source, lines 2444—2451; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"f39f42cc47ed3c81e778d7b0045944b4723e8f61076e47c6b1a169c4a5937226","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.9.2":{"title":"11.9.2 Recursive interfaces and lineage","lead":"When a simulated future enters the next round, what is most easily lost is the preceding ‘if’. Lineage records make every child run carry its parent path's assumptions, unknowns, and losses, rather than inheriting only an apparently certain number.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2452—2457; source anchor section-11-9-2; short quotation: ‘a simulated starting point must not be rewritten as current reality’ (translated from Chinese). The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","A recasting audit checks whether meanings and identities change across runs: a parent's ‘possible stock of 4’ cannot become the child's ‘measured stock of 4’; an object defined at one scale in the parent cannot silently become another whole in the child. Reentry does not regain observational qualification; it continues derivation after explicitly narrowing the conditional scope.","Lineage first marks the current order, parent run, parent path, parent node, and simulated time so the source can be retraced. Recording both the current-order window and cumulative window reveals hidden spans, such as advancing only one day per order while ultimately extrapolating ten years. Model version, inherited assumptions, and added conditions indicate whether rules have changed.","Four classes of fields—preserved, changed, collapsed, and omitted—are especially important. Preserved identifies states carried forward; changed identifies what new operations alter; collapsed identifies details aggregated; omitted identifies what is not carried over and its consequences. Omitted risks cannot be treated as disappeared, nor collapsed averages as identical values for every member.","New unknowns, target-effective variables, residuals, and identity checks ensure the child question remains answerable through this interface. Target-effective variables form a representation sufficient and qualified for this order's task, not necessarily the fewest variables. If collapsed differences alter subsequent actions or constraints, the interface is not closed and must be expanded or analysis stopped.","The return path specifies how to attach child conclusions to the parent and which bounded parent condition they support; stopping reasons prevent unfinished branches from masquerading as resolved. A returned child derivation depends on parent assumptions and cannot become independent evidence proving them in reverse, which would create self-certification.","Child runs must inherit unresolved parent unknowns, losses, and residuals. Selecting the assumption branch ‘the fault has been repaired’ is merely conditioning, not confirmation of the repair. More samples can only improve computational error within the given model; they do not reduce unmodeled bias or automatically turn output frequencies into real-world probabilities."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Conditional branch selection","leftText":"Assume a path and continue analysis.","rightTitle":"Excluding other branches in reality","rightText":"Requires new discriminating materials."},{"leftTitle":"Collapsing","leftText":"Aggregate some differences with an explanation.","rightTitle":"Differences do not exist","rightText":"Collapsing cannot establish absence and may undermine subsequent sufficiency."},{"leftTitle":"Reduced computational error","leftText":"More stable numbers within the same model.","rightTitle":"Increased empirical support","rightText":"Requires new real-world evidence and is not obtained through sample counts."},{"leftTitle":"Returning child conclusions","leftText":"Attach them to the parent conditions on which they depend.","rightTitle":"Self-certifying parent assumptions","rightText":"Outputs dependent on an assumption cannot validate that assumption."}],"boundaries":["Parent risks must not default to zero at a child's starting point.","Register model or object changes rather than changing the question under cover of reentry.","If necessary differences are collapsed, file-interface readability and writability cannot establish semantic closure."],"questions":["If parent x∈[4,8] and child y=x+2, what is the answer without branch selection?","When must individual-item ordering not be collapsed?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/figures/section-11-9-2.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.9.2","start_line":2524,"end_line":2529,"startLine":2524,"endLine":2529}],"outputId":"section-11-9-2","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Before reentry, locate the parent run, path, and node, preserving simulated time and identity.","Declare current-order/cumulative windows, model version, inherited assumptions, and added conditions together.","List preserved, changed, collapsed, and omitted items, checking loss of task-essential differences.","Inherit relevant unknowns, losses, and residuals; check object identity and target-effective variables.","Specify return dependencies and stopping reasons for child conclusions; do not use child derivations to prove parent assumptions."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a traceable reentry entry","text":"Order=2; parent run R0, parent path P低速 (low speed), parent node N10; simulated time 10:00; current-order window 10:00—11:00, cumulative window 08:00—11:00; version v1. Inherited: arrival rate 2, backlog 8 at 10:00 as a conditional derivation, and losses from two hours of uncleared queues. Added: if repair restores capacity to 3 at 10:00. Preserved: the same repair-queue object and arrival definitions. Changed: capacity 1→3. Collapsed: individual-item order. Omitted: cost effects unmodeled. New unknown: whether repair is actually feasible. Target-effective variables: backlog, arrivals, capacity. Residual: differences between parent model and reality remain unchecked. Identity: same queue, same units. Return: attach the conditional 11:00 value 7 to R0/P低速. Stop: the 11:00 window ends and cost questions remain unanswered. Calculation: 8+2−3=7.","assumptions":"All fields are teaching constructions; individual-item order may be collapsed only because this question asks total item counts and has no priority constraints.","boundary":"If an item has a nondeferrable deadline, collapsing order is no longer sufficient; expand the state rather than calculating unchanged and claiming every task is protected."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: reentry cannot erase an interval","text":"The parent run knows only stock x∈[4,8], and the child rule is y=x+2. Without an additional condition selection, the child answer should be y∈[6,10]. Explicitly selecting parent branch x=4 gives child value 6 only on that branch; the global range [6,10] cannot become a definite 6 with a claim that reentry added evidence.","assumptions":"The linear rule and interval are constructed, with no probability distribution.","boundary":"Selecting a branch is not observing x=4 and does not resolve its real-world support."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The child run needs a number, so the parent interval's midpoint 6 is entered as known.","effect":"Input convenience creates no measurement; retain the interval or state the branch-selection condition explicitly.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"After a simulated output returns to the parent run, the parent assumption gains a new piece of evidence.","effect":"That output depends on the parent assumption and is not independent real-world material.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If parent x∈[4,8] and child y=x+2, what is the answer without branch selection?","answer":"[6,10]; choosing x=4 yields 6 only under that condition.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"When must individual-item ordering not be collapsed?","answer":"When deadlines, priorities, or other subsequent constraints depend on individual-item order, aggregate totals are insufficient.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/figures/section-11-9-2.png","alt":"Parent R0/P低速/N10: simulated backlog 8 at 10:00, not measured. Child R1 adds the condition ‘if repaired to capacity 3’, inheriting unknowns and losses. Conditional value 7 at 11:00 returns to the original parent branch without proving repair occurred. Teaching construction 8+2−3=7; child results supply no independent real-world evidence for parent assumptions.","caption":"Teaching construction 8+2−3=7; child results supply no independent real-world evidence for parent assumptions."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. The three cards express distinct responsibilities this section must preserve, not implemented software or actual processes. If future interaction switches assumptions, conditions and inherited unknowns must be shown simultaneously.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section defines data responsibilities for reentry interfaces and lineage. Narratives of actual events cannot supply nonexistent parent runs, versions, or sampling records, so a complete construction demonstrates fields and nonentailment."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-9-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 2390—2413 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["11.9.1: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.2: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.3: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.4: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"formal","title":"Historical Chinese research manuscript: the source's task sufficiency, dynamic interfaces, and bounded extrapolation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source, §11.3.1, lines 2261—2268; §11.7, lines 2349—2361; §11.9, lines 2378—2389","claim_support":["Reentry creates no new real-world observation; conditional branches retain unknowns; temporal unfolding and object identity; interface sufficiency is tied to the current task"],"limits":["The bounded derivation constructed here replaces neither real calibration nor execution authority.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.9.2 Recursive interfaces and lineage; Chinese source, lines 2452—2457; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"1f569d49c94dce880d44c3796cb6a71e4dfddf1e2a1e3c93f75714dcbf856d05","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.9.3":{"title":"11.9.3 Branching, merging, and pruning","lead":"More branches are not always better, nor is cleaner pruning always better. What matters is why each path deserves retention, when it may merge with another, and which consequences might be missed after deletion.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2458—2463; source anchor section-11-9-3; short quotation: ‘low probability alone cannot justify deleting a high-consequence path’ (translated from Chinese). The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","At each order, check substantive grounds for the main path, strongest competing path, low-probability high-consequence path, and residual exit. The main path is not the analyst's favorite story; a competing path must challenge the current explanation; a high-consequence branch may lack an estimable probability but needs mechanism or risk grounds; a residual exit retains outcomes the current model cannot explain rather than serving as an all-purpose black box.","If a category is inapplicable or cannot be constructed, explain why directly. Inventing disasters without grounds to complete four categories adds no comprehensiveness and distorts comparison. Retaining a high-consequence branch indicates a need for study or preparation, not a measured real-world probability; if ‘low probability’ lacks estimation grounds, rewrite it as unknown probability with important consequences.","Branches should correspond to mechanism differences, agent choices, external disturbances, or explicitly typed unknowns. Rephrasing the same sentence several ways is neither an independent mechanism nor additional evidence samples. Register dependence when branches share underlying sources and assumptions rather than voting by branch count.","Merging has four simultaneous requirements: object identity is preserved; key states are equivalent within predetermined tolerance; historical differences do not change subsequent transitions; and later available actions and constraints agree. Identical current totals are only one field within the second requirement, far short of establishing all conditions. Set tolerance beforehand according to task and consequences, rather than loosening it after seeing similar results.","Whether historical differences affect the future requires corresponding evidence or a model contract; do not mechanically preserve every detail of history or delete it all. Retain past payments, outstanding debts, or commitments if they change later resources and actions. If the local model explicitly does not depend on relevant history, merging is permitted for that bounded task while preserving source lineage.","Pruning stops further computation on a branch; it does not declare that branch impossible in reality. State rules beforehand and preserve pruned branches, reasons, possible probability mass, and harm levels. Unknown probabilities remain unknown, not zero; resource-based pruning must be recorded as a computational limit, not disguised as exclusion in reality.","Low probability alone is insufficient to delete a high-consequence path because consequences, irreversibility, rights constraints, and preparation costs may still make it important. Conversely, severe consequences do not permit unlimited expansion of groundless imaginings; preserve substantive mechanism entry and task relevance. After pruning, reports should still show readers where coverage is missing."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Merging","leftText":"Share computation for equivalent states under the subsequent task contract.","rightTitle":"Deleting provenance","rightText":"Sources and different historical conditions should not be lost."},{"leftTitle":"Pruning","leftText":"Stop computing a branch, with reasons and coverage records.","rightTitle":"Excluding real-world possibility","rightText":"Requires factual or mechanism evidence; computational limits are insufficient."},{"leftTitle":"Identical current values","leftText":"Some fields are equal.","rightTitle":"Mergeable states","rightText":"Historical aftereffects, actions, and constraints must also agree."},{"leftTitle":"A high-consequence preparedness branch","leftText":"Has mechanism grounds and warrants preparation.","rightTitle":"Known probability of occurrence","rightText":"Requires additional probability materials."}],"boundaries":["The source does not require forcing four branches at every order or guarantee quantifiable probabilities.","Tolerance cannot conceal key differences near hard protective thresholds.","When historical effects are absorbed by a sufficient current state, bounded collapsing is allowed without copying history indefinitely."],"questions":["For A(10,0) and B(10,6), can both spend 8 after payments?","If computation of a serious branch with unknown probability stops for budget reasons, what should the report retain?","When may two x=3 states merge?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/figures/section-11-9-3.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.9.3","start_line":2530,"end_line":2535,"startLine":2530,"endLine":2535}],"outputId":"section-11-9-3","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Check main, strong competing, high-consequence, and residual-exit paths according to actual grounds rather than filling categories.","Register explainable branch differences and shared sources; do not treat repeated narratives as independent branches.","For merging, jointly check objects, key-state tolerance, historical aftereffects, and action constraints.","Define pruning rules beforehand and retain pruned branches and probability/harm gaps.","A high-consequence branch cannot be deleted solely for low probability; groundless disasters must not be forced into existence either."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: identical stock with different debts cannot merge","text":"Two fictional branches each have current cash 10. A has no due debt; B must pay 6 at the next time point. Subsequent action a costs 8 and requires a nonnegative postpayment balance. A may choose a, but B has only 4 after fulfilling the obligation and cannot. Merging solely on cash 10 wrongly gives B the same action set. In the complete state (现金,到期债务), meaning (cash, due debt), A(10,0) and B(10,6) clearly differ.","assumptions":"Debts, the pay-first order, hard constraints, and action costs are constructed, supplying no actual investment or lending advice.","boundary":"Lawful debt forgiveness or a different window requires new state materials; debts cannot be deleted because the model wants to merge."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: sufficient states may merge","text":"A fictional deterministic system has next step x_next=x+u, permitted actions u∈{0,1}, a task asking only x over the next two steps, and no historical losses, hidden constraints, or other states. Two branches have different histories but both currently have x=3 and identical action sets. Any identical action sequence therefore produces identical future x values; subsequent nodes may be shared under this contract while retaining both parent lineages.","assumptions":"This local model explicitly gives history no additional effect, with identical actions and constraints and tolerance 0.","boundary":"If the task changes to cumulative path cost, different histories may cease to be equivalent and the differences must be reopened."},{"title":"Teaching construction: correct pruning records","text":"A fictional stress scenario has two common load branches and a serious-interruption branch with an explicit interface-failure mechanism but unknown probability. If the computational budget is inadequate, fine-grained numerical expansion of the serious branch may stop, while its mechanism, unknown probability, severe consequences, and materials needing checking remain recorded. The branch cannot be erased and the remaining two normalized as covering all reality.","assumptions":"The scenario and resource limits are purely constructed, with no actual probability estimates; no real stress test was performed.","boundary":"Whether further study is warranted depends on use, costs, and protective responsibilities; diagram simplicity does not replace these judgments."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A and B both have cash 10, so they can share the same subsequent node from now on.","effect":"Due debts change subsequent action sets, violating merging conditions.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The disaster branch has very low probability, so its harms need not be examined.","effect":"Low probability alone cannot delete a high-consequence branch, and the probability itself needs grounds.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"For A(10,0) and B(10,6), can both spend 8 after payments?","answer":"A can; B has only 4 and cannot. They therefore cannot merge solely by cash.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If computation of a serious branch with unknown probability stops for budget reasons, what should the report retain?","answer":"Retain the branch and mechanism, stopping reason, unknown probability mass, harm level, and material needs without declaring it impossible.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"When may two x=3 states merge?","answer":"When the explicit local model’s future depends only on x, actions and constraints match, and history adds nothing; review again if the task changes.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/figures/section-11-9-3.png","alt":"A: cash 10, debt due 0, can spend 8; B: cash 10, debt 6 payable first, leaves 4 and cannot spend 8. Do not merge on 10: actions and constraints differ, so retain each lineage. Entirely mathematical constructions; A and B are parallel comparisons, not sequential state transitions.","caption":"Entirely mathematical constructions; A and B are parallel comparisons, not sequential state transitions."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. The three cards express distinct responsibilities this section must preserve, not implemented software or actual processes. If future interaction switches assumptions, conditions and inherited unknowns must be shown simultaneously.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Merging and pruning are model-management rules requiring declared states, historical dependencies, and risk contracts for validation. Reproducible constructions illustrate necessary and sufficient responsibilities without inventing actual branch probabilities or disaster outcomes."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-9-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 2390—2413 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["11.9.1: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.2: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.3: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.4: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"formal","title":"Historical Chinese research manuscript: the source's task sufficiency, dynamic interfaces, and bounded extrapolation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source, §11.3.1, lines 2261—2268; §11.7, lines 2349—2361; §11.9, lines 2378—2389","claim_support":["Reentry creates no new real-world observation; conditional branches retain unknowns; temporal unfolding and object identity; interface sufficiency is tied to the current task"],"limits":["The bounded derivation constructed here replaces neither real calibration nor execution authority.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.9.3 Branching, Merging, and Pruning; Chinese source lines 2458–2463; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"304c64795b19ca6b80c50416f707e0ff8e72bc02b8d5a40526399b6ca20687a3","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.9.4":{"title":"11.9.4 Early Stopping","lead":"Deeper projection increasingly needs clear stopping points. Stopping should leave a bounded answer—what still holds and what exceeds evidence or authority—rather than merely saying ‘too complex’ or zeroing out all previous conclusions.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2464–2467; source anchor section-11-9-4; short quotation translated from Chinese: ‘The stopping record must state what can and cannot still be said.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","The first stopping class is object/interface failure: object or scale identity changes, key assumptions cannot transfer, or recursive interfaces are not closed. If the parent model stores a team’s total resources but the child asks each person’s protected minimum share, a total-only interface may fail. Continuing loses necessary task distinctions. Expand representation, downgrade to a range, or stop instead of forcing an answer from the old model.","The second class is insufficient mechanisms or discrimination: transitions exceed the domain, branches explode without distinguishing signals, tiny changes reverse conclusions throughout, or residuals exceed use thresholds. Many branches alone need not stop work if distinguishing material allows bounded checks. Sensitivity also does not mean nothing is known; local ranges or common constraints may remain.","The third class is absent increment and calibration overreach: deeper orders add no task gain over shallower orders and a simple baseline, or exceed supported calibration horizons. More computation is not justification. Short-window calibration cannot extend the same ‘90%’ into the distant future; downgrade to conditional scenarios instead of maintaining false precision.","The fourth class concerns rights and authorization. If further analysis needs unauthorized personal material or actual actions, stop that dependent path while preserving lawfully answerable parts. Recommendations, organizing unknowns, and nonintrusive formal analysis are not automatically execution; research value likewise confers no right to bypass authority.","The original also sets a third-order ceiling for this organization of exploration. Stop or downgrade there rather than continuing self-amplification to fill a fourth order; this says neither that the world has only three layers nor that third-order analysis is inherently correct. Any key stopping condition may require stopping sooner, without completing every step.","A stopping record should give triggering facts, affected branches/tasks, remaining bounded results, prohibited extrapolations, and material needed to reopen. Suspension is not permanent unknowability, and retirement does not erase historical results; genuinely new material permits reevaluation in a new version.","This responsibility connects to general stopping in 11.10 and choice stopping in 12.15, but here the focus is reentry depth and interfaces. One failed recursive branch does not automatically revoke independently known stock, actual events, or a qualified short-term total effect; preserving local knowledge is central to a stopping report."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Stop deeper projection","leftText":"No further extrapolation under insufficient conditions.","rightTitle":"Deny all upstream knowledge","rightText":"Independently established bounded facts remain."},{"leftTitle":"Highly sensitive model","leftText":"Small changes can alter conclusions.","rightTitle":"Impossible to study","rightText":"Reversal conditions can still be reported and discriminating observations designed."},{"leftTitle":"Third-order ceiling","leftText":"A stopping rule for the current organization of exploration.","rightTitle":"The world has only three orders","rightText":"The original makes no such ontological claim."},{"leftTitle":"Downgrade to scenarios","leftText":"Remove unsupported probabilities or certainty.","rightTitle":"Delete all outputs","rightText":"Explicit conditional consequences and risk descriptions may remain."}],"boundaries":["Do not treat authorization gaps as mere computational shortages.","Missing long-term evidence does not establish the opposite long-term conclusion.","Complexity, sensitivity, and residual thresholds must match use and protection responsibilities; do not change gates ad hoc to preserve a conclusion."],"questions":["Can total 10 guarantee at least 3 for each of two people?","If y flips as θ crosses 0, can denser sampling determine actual y?","What must a stopping record tell readers at minimum?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/figures/section-11-9-4.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.9.4","start_line":2536,"end_line":2539,"startLine":2536,"endLine":2539}],"outputId":"section-11-9-4","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Before every reentry, check object identity, inherited assumptions, mechanism domain, and interface closure.","Check discriminating signals, sensitivity, residuals, and deeper-order increment over shallower analysis/baselines.","Check horizons, rights, authority, and the third-order ceiling; stop or downgrade at any key boundary.","Record remaining bounded answers, unavailable claims, and reopening material without clearing independently known results."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: limit the answer when the interface is insufficient","text":"The parent state stores only two people’s total resources, 10, while the next-order task requires at least 3 each. Distribution (5,5) satisfies it and (9,1) does not; total 10 cannot distinguish them. Stop the definite inference that everyone meets the minimum while retaining total 10. Reopening needs appropriate authorized distribution material, or a different question depending only on the total.","assumptions":"People, resources, and minimum shares are constructed; no actual personal data were collected.","boundary":"Do not invent 5 per person because the total is sufficient, or revoke the known total because distribution is unknown."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: tiny parameter differences reverse the conclusion","text":"Let fictional binary output y=1 if θ≥0 and y=0 otherwise; only θ∈[−0.01,0.01] is known. Opposite sides of the permitted domain produce different outputs, so no robust direction follows. Report reversal threshold 0 and answer set {0,1}, stopping unique-outcome extrapolation. Only new reliable material establishing θ≥0 can narrow the model answer to 1.","assumptions":"The θ interval and threshold are teaching assumptions, with no probability distribution or measurement performed.","boundary":"A denser parameter grid shows the reversal more clearly but does not establish which side actual θ lies on."},{"title":"Teaching construction: retain earlier parts at the ceiling","text":"A fictional first order calculates one-hour backlog 7 under given capacity; a second order builds only an ‘if scheduling is changed’ branch; third-order institutional change still lacks key material. Stop further reentry, retaining the first-order conditional value and second-order option conditions. Do not turn third-order uncertainty into inevitable institutional improvement or erase first-order arithmetic.","assumptions":"All three orders are model-organization proposals; no schedule or institutional change occurred.","boundary":"Known one-hour conditions do not support a ten-year path; deeper futures need new tasks and corresponding material, not continued generation in the old run."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The third order cannot be answered reliably, so every first-order result must be withdrawn.","effect":"Withdraw only parts dependent on failed conditions; retain independently bounded results.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"We have not reached the third order, so we should continue despite needing unauthorized material.","effect":"Authority and protection boundaries may require immediate stopping; order count is no excuse.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can total 10 guarantee at least 3 for each of two people?","answer":"No. (5,5) and (9,1) share a total but differ in answer; retain the total and stop the distribution conclusion.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If y flips as θ crosses 0, can denser sampling determine actual y?","answer":"No. Without material locating actual θ, report only the set and threshold; denser calculation adds no empirical support.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What must a stopping record tell readers at minimum?","answer":"Triggers, affected scope, remaining bounded conclusions, prohibited extrapolations, and material needed to reopen.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/figures/section-11-9-4.png","alt":"Known total 10, unknown allocation between two people, parent interface stores only the total; child question requires each≥3, satisfied by (5,5), not (9,1). Stop a definite allocation conclusion, retain 10, and require appropriate material to reopen. Teaching construction; a locally insufficient interface does not invalidate all facts.","caption":"Teaching construction; a locally insufficient interface does not invalidate all facts."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. The three cards express distinct responsibilities this section must preserve, not implemented software or actual processes. If future interaction switches assumptions, conditions and inherited unknowns must be shown simultaneously.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section sets stopping and downgrading thresholds for recursive models; formal examples test interface sufficiency and sensitivity. No calibration windows or authority records for actual multilevel models were obtained, and no stopping-experiment effects are fabricated."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-9-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 2390—2413 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["11.9.1: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.2: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.3: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","11.9.4: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"formal","title":"Historical Chinese research manuscript: the source's task sufficiency, dynamic interfaces, and bounded extrapolation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source, §11.3.1, lines 2261—2268; §11.7, lines 2349—2361; §11.9, lines 2378—2389","claim_support":["Reentry creates no new real-world observation; conditional branches retain unknowns; temporal unfolding and object identity; interface sufficiency is tied to the current task"],"limits":["The bounded derivation constructed here replaces neither real calibration nor execution authority.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.9.4 Early Stopping; Chinese source lines 2464–2467; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-9-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"147bd55c79adf7c6f718f55436e8e9b3bee10956a4895f097c4d5032fc756f3b","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.10":{"title":"11.10 Stopping Conditions for Projection Runs","lead":"Stopping does not conceal analysis; it makes the defensible scope explicit. When one bridge fails, stop paths depending on it first. When the shared starting point of every path is wrong, stop all relevant runs.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2468–2473; source anchor section-11-10; short quotation translated from Chinese: ‘Affected propositions and their dependent paths should be suspended or downgraded.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","This section distinguishes local and shared premises. Insufficient support for object facts or grouping gain, unclear events, missing channels, or failed cross-scale mappings first affect dependent propositions. Other independently recorded facts may remain; if shared premises fail, such as misidentified objects or an unreliable overall evidence source, stop all dependent runs. Neither stop everything mechanically nor divide a global failure into local pieces to preserve conclusions.","When a key object lacks bounded support, stop propositions treating it as established while retaining analyst-proposed groupings and existing documentary facts. If event occurrence is unverified, stop definite real-consequence claims, retaining explicit if-branches where appropriate. A missing propagation channel prevents explaining that transmission but need not revoke an independently identified terminal total effect.","Cross-scale mapping often fails when group means fill individual states or local mechanisms are directly extended to institutional outcomes. Downgrade to the scale actually supported, explaining missing aggregation, representation, or transmission relations instead of giving a larger graph automatically stronger explanatory authority.","Growing paths without discriminating signals add stories but no help for the question; extreme sensitivity to tiny parameters removes support for a unique answer. Report thresholds and answer ranges first, seek the observation most likely to change the answer, and stop if safe discrimination is impossible. The original does not prohibit branching; it rejects expansion without discriminatory value.","When privacy or safety risks exceed information value, ‘more complete collection’ cannot justify continuation. Information value is not merely a price: rights boundaries and irreversible harms may forbid offset by total benefit. Stop uses demanding projection directly produce diagnoses, punishments, or authorization; nondiagnostic information organization, questions needed for lawful procedures, and boundaries may remain, without deciding for qualified parties.","If a simple baseline performs equally well or better, stop claiming gain from complex projection; consider returning to the baseline or limiting the complex model to exploration. Comparisons need matched targets, windows, information sets, and scoring. One loss does not make all complex models forever useless, and richer stories do not excuse ignoring testable performance.","A stopping record gives five core answers: where stopping occurs, what was obtained, which unknowns prevent continuation, which next observation has greatest information value, and what risks continuation adds. ‘Most valuable’ is relative to the current question and permitted scope; discrimination alone cannot prioritize unnecessary private material.","Stopping or downgrading preserves old runs, sources, and dependencies. New material permits review in a new version; regenerating the same narrative is no reopening basis. This complements 11.9.4’s recursive stopping: that section particularly limits reentry depth, while this one covers applicability and use in any dynamic run.","Stopping conditions target dependencies on object facts, grouping gain, and mechanism channels separately. Unsupported circle gain may stop dependent complex projection or prompt simpler representation while independently established object facts remain reportable. If event identity, object K, or shared material is wrong, stop every path depending on that shared premise."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Local stopping","leftText":"Stop only propositions and paths dependent on failed premises.","rightTitle":"Stop all relevant runs","rightText":"Applies when shared premises or overall use conditions fail."},{"leftTitle":"Stop the gain claim","leftText":"The complex model has not outperformed a reasonable baseline.","rightTitle":"The model is entirely worthless","rightText":"Other uses such as explanation and exploration require separate evaluation."},{"leftTitle":"Information is discriminating","leftText":"May change the analytical answer.","rightTitle":"It may be collected","rightText":"Still requires necessity, authority, and protection conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Provide procedural material","leftText":"Organize facts, questions, and bounded analysis.","rightTitle":"Diagnose, punish, or authorize","rightText":"Qualification to decide does not arise from projection."}],"boundaries":["Local gaps do not erase independent knowledge; failed shared premises cannot be handled by stopping only one twig.","Baseline comparisons and stopping thresholds must be predetermined or reasonably explicit, not arbitrarily changed after results.","Unresolved records are part of the deliverable and should not be hidden at delivery.","Stopping an unauthorized use does not mean refusing every permissible information service."],"questions":["When must all relevant runs stop?","If both predictions are (4,6) and outcomes (5,5), what is mean absolute error?","Is the most valuable post-stop observation necessarily permissible to obtain?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-10/figures/section-11-10.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.10","start_line":2540,"end_line":2545,"startLine":2540,"endLine":2545}],"outputId":"section-11-10","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Locate failed premises and affected dependencies, first distinguishing local branches from shared premises.","Determine which statements need suspension or downgrading by object, event, channel, scale, and discrimination.","Check risk, use, and authority; do not substitute projection for diagnosis, punishment, or authorization.","Compare baselines fairly and remove unsupported gain claims.","Create the five-part stopping record and specify new material permitting reopening."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: one false message recalculates only dependent branches","text":"A fictional warehouse has measured stock 10 and an independent temperature record 20. Report E predicts resupply 5, producing modeled tomorrow stock 15 and downstream allocation branches. E is later confirmed misissued. Stop or withdraw 15 and allocation inferences dependent on resupply, retaining measured 10 and independent temperature 20. If both sensors and the report later prove to use the wrong object ID shared by the whole model’s object identification, suspend the relevant runs together.","assumptions":"Independence of material and error scope are explicitly stipulated; no actual warehousing action occurs.","boundary":"Actual independence cannot be inferred merely from different field names; check real sources and dependencies."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: equal baseline performance cannot be beaten by complexity","text":"Two fictional frozen models predict (4,6) for two targets, with actual outcomes (5,5). Regardless of the complex model’s node count, both have mean absolute error (|4−5|+|6−5|)/2=1. This use has no evidence of predictive increment; separate explanatory uses require their own evidence, not counting nodes as accuracy.","assumptions":"Predictions, outcomes, and scoring are teaching constructions; two samples cannot establish long-term statistical equivalence.","boundary":"‘Equal this time’ does not rigorously prove equal future performance; it limits only gain claims supported by this material."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a complete stopping entry","text":"Location: stock→timely-delivery path. Obtained: reliable initial stock 10. Unknown: whether transport is open. Block: no usable channel material, so next-day arrival cannot be asserted. Next observation: check corresponding transport status and window within existing authority. Continuation risk: treating unknown as open may create unfulfillable commitments. Give only ‘arrival is possible if the channel is open and delay≤window’, without actually promising or dispatching.","assumptions":"The complete entry is a teaching proposal; no actual logistics query or commitment was sent.","boundary":"An independent local task unaffected by the channel may continue its analysis."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The resupply report was false, so independent temperature 20 must also be deleted.","effect":"No dependency means no collateral erasure; stopping scope follows sources and mechanisms.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The global object ID is wrong; pausing one graph node suffices.","effect":"Failed shared object premises require stopping all relevant dependent runs.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Both models have equal error, but the complex graph looks more professional, so foresight capability improved.","effect":"Complexity and foresight increment differ; test under the same contract.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"When must all relevant runs stop?","answer":"When shared premises or overall use conditions fail and relevant paths all depend on them, not whenever any local unknown occurs.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If both predictions are (4,6) and outcomes (5,5), what is mean absolute error?","answer":"Both equal 1; current material supplies no gain for the complex model and does not prove long-term equivalence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is the most valuable post-stop observation necessarily permissible to obtain?","answer":"No. Necessity, privacy, safety, and authorization need separate checks; otherwise retain unknowns or return to a bounded question.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-10/figures/section-11-10.png","alt":"Teaching report E is wrong: replenishment 5 lacks support. Inventory 15 and allocation paths dependent on E pause; independently measured inventory 10 and temperature 20 remain. If the shared object ID is wrong, all related premises fail and every dependent run stops while object identification is repaired. Stopping records still state local knowledge, gaps, and material needed to reopen. Entirely teaching constructions; source independence and shared dependencies require actual verification, not guesses from field names.","caption":"Entirely teaching constructions; source independence and shared dependencies require actual verification, not guesses from field names."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. The upper row shows local stopping and the lower shared-premise failure; they are not consecutive events in one sequence.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This is a stopping contract for runs and uses. Constructions directly show dependency scope and baseline comparisons. Complete dependencies and authority material for an actual system were not obtained, so no stopping verdict or professional diagnosis is invented for it."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-10","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 2414–2419; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["11.10: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.10 Stopping conditions for projection runs; Chinese source lines 2468–2473; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-10/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"The relevant explanation in this section has been revised for v2; the original research attachments and the scope of external-source review retain their historical status.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"f64eb2cdb1fd3ca06ad0d6f80a698fb812eb42e5cc202b5d82223b6da70c71b4","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.11":{"title":"11.11 An example of a cross-circle cascade","lead":"This is not an investigation of an actual company but the original text's continuing public-resignation teaching scenario. It lays out possible paths through the team, management, family, professional community, and platform, focusing on where conditions break rather than choosing a path for the company.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2474–2483; original anchor section-11-11. Short quotation translated from Chinese: “These enter only the variable-candidate ledger.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","Long hours, care pressure, declining team trust, management's reputational concerns, and community opportunities are all original scenario assumptions, not researcher measurements. Unformed platform public opinion is also just the starting premise. §10.11 first draws the joint objects and dual channels; this section injects publication of a resignation statement to show conditional branching of subsequent states.","The resignation statement contains checkable facts and personal narrative with different support scopes. Relevant working-hours, institutional-text, or handoff-record components may be independently verified. Feelings are material about the person's experience and claims, neither categorically noise nor substitutes for others' inner states or every fact of the event. No specific text is supplied here, so accusations, dialogue, and legal conclusions cannot be invented.","Direct effects also need separate channels: team receipt is arrival; platform receipt of the text creates propagatable material; pressure on management to respond is a stipulated consequence. Propagatability is not wide propagation, nor receipt belief. Actual applications must separately check visibility, responses, and subsequent states.","Path one begins with public denial: external attention may subside briefly or intensify. Further resignations along this branch require internal members to interpret denial as suppression, trust to fall further, and the professional community to create actual opportunity bridges. Every “if” bears independent evidential responsibility. Public denial is not automatically suppression, nor is everyone's interpretation identical.","Path two begins with acknowledgment and verifiable adjustments, distinguishing promises from execution. Only actual changes to resources and appeal procedures allow registration of working-hours and process changes on the organizational clock. Experience–meaning trust may recover more slowly or not at all. Better announcement wording does not establish resources in place, and resources in place do not automatically establish repaired meaning.","Path three improves family-care conditions, potentially reducing post-resignation pressure and changing the employee's available actions. It does not directly change the team. Further return requires channels such as continued information or resource bridging. The original avoids coloring the whole company as improved from one person's better state.","The three paths are neither mutually exclusive nor exhaustive histories. Family conditions may improve alongside company denial or actual adjustment. Management may deny first and reform later; create time- and evidence-specific nodes rather than compress all actions into one moment. These are meaningful mechanism entry points, with other supported paths possible in reality.","Early signals include changes in appeals, working-hours implementation, public expression, recruitment, and resignation. Each needs windows, denominators, and record-generation processes. More appeals may mean more problems or greater willingness to report through improved channels. Public silence may mean no objection, fear, platform exit, or something else. One indicator cannot decide every mechanism.","Contrary signals must target paths: a published policy without resource changes weakens “adjustment implemented”; reduced exposure with increased internal silence does not establish repaired trust; absent actual professional-community bridging blocks that opportunity-transmission segment. Contrary material weakens a specific explanation without automatically proving opposite motives.","Key managers' states, recommendation mechanisms, and legal constraints remain residual candidates. Without actual measurements or legal material, this section fills in no personalities, algorithm parameters, or responsibility verdicts. Projection yields conditional consequences and distinguishing signals only. “Which should be chosen” additionally needs normative premises, option comparison, and foresight responsibilities; execution further requires valid authorization."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Original scenario assumption","leftText":"A fictional starting point illustrating an analytical method.","rightTitle":"Actual company-investigation conclusion","rightText":"Requires actual material absent from this section."},{"leftTitle":"Public denial","leftText":"An observable form of response.","rightTitle":"Members interpret it as suppression","rightText":"A meaning judgment with its own positions and material, not an automatic equivalence."},{"leftTitle":"More appeals","leftText":"Observed counts rise.","rightTitle":"Problems necessarily worsen","rightText":"Recording channels and reporting tendencies may also change."},{"leftTitle":"Reduced family pressure","leftText":"Improved conditions for the individual employee.","rightTitle":"Simultaneous team improvement","rightText":"Requires separate bridging and transmission mechanisms."},{"leftTitle":"Conditional consequences","leftText":"What follows if path conditions hold.","rightTitle":"Ought or authorized","rightText":"Requires normative comparison and independent authorization."}],"boundaries":["The original three branches supply no actual occurrence probabilities. Enumeration counts are not probabilities.","One event may produce opposite outcomes in different circles. External exposure is not a proxy for internal trust.","Unperformed resource changes cannot be recorded as complete. No actual personnel or legal disposition is executed in this example.","Resignation does not diagnose personality, nor are personal choices entirely dissolved into structural determination."],"questions":["Do appeals rising from 5 to 10 directly imply that actual problems doubled?","Can path one and family path three occur together?","May evidenced working-hours reductions be acknowledged before trust recovers?","Does this projection authorize a company to take a personnel action?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-11/figures/section-11-11.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.11","start_line":2546,"end_line":2555,"startLine":2546,"endLine":2555}],"outputId":"section-11-11","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Label the whole example an original teaching scenario, separating assumptions, checkable fact types, and personal narrative.","Enter subsequent conditional paths separately from information receipt, available text, and response pressure.","Check internal interpretations, actual resource adjustments, opportunity bridges, and family spillovers segment by segment. Arrival is not effect.","Register positive/contrary signals and competing explanations in different windows, retaining parallel paths.","Residuals enter only the candidate ledger. Normative preferences and execution require explicit conditions and authorization procedures."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Reading branches in the original teaching scenario","text":"If a company announces a new appeal channel without changing budget, on-duty personnel, or processing authority, register only the announcement and its arrival. “Actual appeal-uptake capacity improved” remains to be checked. If later actual records show resources in place, update the corresponding organizational state without waiting for everyone's trust to recover before acknowledging resource changes.","assumptions":"Announcement and resource arrangements are teaching additions to the original fictional scenario, referring to no actual company.","boundary":"Obtain separate evidence for resources and trust; lack of verification for one cannot categorically negate the other."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: appeal counts depend on reporting channels","text":"Suppose each of two fictional weeks has 20 actual unresolved problems. In week one exactly 1/4 enter the records, yielding 5; in week two 1/2 enter, yielding 10. Doubled observed appeals do not mean doubled problems. With unknown recording probabilities, changed counts may also include changed problems; one cannot conclude everything merely reflects improved channels.","assumptions":"Coverage proportions here are exact aggregate recording fractions, not random sample realizations passed off as expectations. Problem counts and proportions are fictional.","boundary":"Actual use must check definitions, denominators, duplicates, and selection. This example does not deny that actual problems may increase."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the family branch can run in parallel without direct spillover","text":"A fictional employee gains caregiving support and more discretionary time each week while the company continues public denial. If the employee has left the team and transmits no further information or resources, this model has no edge from family improvement directly to team trust. If the professional community later creates a new bridge, new material must establish a separate path.","assumptions":"Exit and bridging status are stipulated. No care details are requested from actual families.","boundary":"Absence of the specified edge limits only this explanation, not all possible actual indirect connections."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Public exposure falls, so internal trust must have recovered.","effect":"External visibility differs from internal meaning. Silence and exposure each have competing explanations.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The company says adjustment began, so working hours and appeal resources should immediately be marked improved.","effect":"An initiation statement is not actual execution; corresponding records are required.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Care pressure decreases, so the company's team state automatically improves.","effect":"A specific bridging channel is required; individual changes do not directly fill collective states.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Do appeals rising from 5 to 10 directly imply that actual problems doubled?","answer":"No. In the construction, actual problems remain 20 while recorded coverage changes from 1/4 to 1/2. Reality requires separate checking.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can path one and family path three occur together?","answer":"Yes. They are not mutually exclusive options; retain parallel paths and check interfaces.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"May evidenced working-hours reductions be acknowledged before trust recovers?","answer":"Yes. Retain material/organizational change and separately check meaning recovery. Neither erases the other.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does this projection authorize a company to take a personnel action?","answer":"No. This is conditional analysis of the original fictional scenario, not a substitute for normative comparison or actual authorization.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-11/figures/section-11-11.png","alt":"Original premise: a public resignation statement, with facts and personal narrative separated. Branch one: public denial; internal/external interpretations may differ, and more resignations require actual bridging. Branch two: acknowledgment and adjustment; promise ≠ execution, resources and trust may change asynchronously. Branch three: improved family conditions initially reduce individual pressure without automatically changing the team. Check signals by channel—hours/appeals/resignations—and recording selection. Output conditional consequences, ledger residuals as candidates, and generate no execution permission. All are original-text and lesson teaching scenarios, without actual company data. The three branches are nonexclusive and have no assigned probabilities.","caption":"All are original-text and lesson teaching scenarios, without actual company data. The three branches are nonexclusive and have no assigned probabilities."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. No forced arrows connect the three paths into a necessary sequence. Read each branch's required bridging conditions rather than infer causation from spatial adjacency.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original explicitly continues an analytical example without an actual company, date, or primary archive. This lesson explains the teaching construction without inventing real people, accusations, working hours, or platform data. External cases cannot replace passage-by-passage explanation of the original example."},"openQuestions":["Actual application requires the resignation text, checkable records, accounts from different positions, actual resource execution, and bridging material, respecting purpose, privacy, and authorization boundaries."],"taskId":"S-11-11","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 2420–2429; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["11.11: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"prior_example","title":"Historical research draft: the same teaching scenario in Part X","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§10.11, Chinese source lines 2195–2202","claim_support":["Original source of joint-object, dual-channel, and different-window assumptions; this section continues that example."],"limits":["The original supplies no real company or event evidence and cannot be treated as an empirical case.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.11 An example of a cross-circle cascade; Chinese source lines 2474–2483; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-11/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"0a71bb304616380a7b179b30ecbd5a8d438d18eb1e09bf9045c934cb94827b00","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.12":{"title":"11.12 Outputs of this part","lead":"A projection deliverable cannot consist only of a final route map. Readers should see starting points, events, assumptions, stepwise changes, unknowns, and stopping points, and know which elements are conditional preparation and which lack forecasting or action eligibility.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2484–2489; original anchor section-11-12. Short quotation translated from Chinese: “An explicit separation statement for prediction and action.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","The frozen joint snapshot specifies object, task, scale, time, evidence cutoff, and key states. Event types and evidence distinguish actual occurrence, reports, plans, and simulations; mechanism and parameter assumptions specify propagation dependencies. Without these three entry points, it is hard to tell whether later paths derive from the same start or switch premises segment by segment.","Update steps and the path graph jointly explain how changes occur. Each path retains conditions, clocks, support status, and early/contrary signals rather than merely “success” or “failure.” Ungrounded probabilities remain unassigned; a complete graph does not require invented numbers. Path lineage and identity continue under preceding contracts.","Residuals and variable candidates expose unexplained areas. Stopping conditions state when extrapolation ends. The outcome write-back plan specifies how future material will be compared with the original run and baseline. Plans are not observed outcomes; a completed write-back field does not mean learning occurred.","Projection helps structured preparation for changing conditions, but “improved foresight capability” is a separate testable claim requiring registration before outcomes, a reasonable simple baseline, corresponding calibration, and continuing write-back. Without these, say only that paths and signals for checking were organized, not that predictions improved. Where material supports a local conclusion, procedural modesty should not prevent a clear answer.","Explain prediction, normative judgment, and execution authority separately. Likelier occurrence does not imply something should be brought about. Under explicit normative premises, analysis may recommend, veto, or defer, fully stating evidence, costs, the opposing case, and revision conditions. Such deliberative judgment does not automatically receive actual execution authority.","“No claim that action should be executed without norms and authorization” does not mean analysts can never recommend. Norms premise comparison of goals, floors, and distribution; authorization determines who may implement actual actions. Missing execution permission still permits an analytical preference with gaps stated, but the two statuses must remain distinct. The next part separates prediction registration from option selection in detail.","Complete output is necessary for checkability, not sufficient for theoretical or empirical correctness. A file with every field may still contain faulty observations, mismatched mechanisms, or uncovered risks. Conversely, explicitly marking necessary fields inapplicable or unknown is better than filling them without grounds."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Structured preparation","leftText":"Knowing which conditions change paths and what to observe.","rightTitle":"Established foresight gain","rightText":"Requires a prior contract, baseline, and outcome evaluation."},{"leftTitle":"Write-back plan","leftText":"A commitment about comparing future material.","rightTitle":"Completed write-back","rightText":"Actual outcomes have arrived and changed corresponding records."},{"leftTitle":"Analytical preference","leftText":"A deliberative opinion under explicit norms and evidence.","rightTitle":"Authorized execution","rightText":"Requires separate valid authority, not automatically generated by preference."},{"leftTitle":"Complete fields","leftText":"Content is locatable and checkable.","rightTitle":"Correct conclusion","rightText":"Still requires support from facts, mechanisms, and applicability conditions."}],"boundaries":["Do not invent parameters, measurements, or authorization to fill every field.","A preference with objections and costs is still a position. State the conditional judgment clearly.","Later factual changes require appended versions, not overwriting the original run."],"questions":["If the actual 09:00 outcome has not arrived, may residual 0 be registered?","With equal effects and risks and an explicit low-cost objective, how may A costing 2 and B costing 5 be compared?","What should a prediction-separation statement distinguish?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-12/figures/section-11-12.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.12","start_line":2556,"end_line":2561,"startLine":2556,"endLine":2561}],"outputId":"section-11-12","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Retain frozen snapshots, input statuses, and mechanism parameters so the starting point remains traceable.","Display update steps, path graphs, and each branch's conditions/clocks/support/positive and contrary signals.","List residual candidates, stopping conditions, and the write-back plan for actual outcomes when they arrive.","Declare separately conditional preparation, predictive-capability evaluation, normative opinions, and execution-authorization status.","Fully explain recommendation grounds, costs, objections, and revision conditions. Complete delivery does not replace factual testing."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a minimal but substantive projection summary","text":"The object is a fictional repair queue, frozen at 08:00 with B=6 and arrivals of 2/hour. The event is “if equipment slows,” not an event already occurring. The rule is next B=max(0,B+2−c), with assumed c=1 or 3. One-step path A gives 7 at 09:00, path B gives 5; the clock is one hour, support is conditional derivation, and no probabilities are supplied. The early signal is actual completions in the same window; the contrary signal is a record corresponding to the other capacity. Both branches share a starting point and are mutually exclusive. No actual reading yet permits residual calculation; the candidate set is empty, not numerical zero. Stop at 09:00 or when channel/object/permission conditions fail. The write-back plan is to obtain actual records with matching definitions, compare absolute error against the prespecified persistence baseline 6, and append a version. Separation statement: no predictive-gain score exists, and no repair or scheduling action is executed.","assumptions":"Numbers, event, and rules are teaching assumptions; no actual system was operated.","boundary":"Real-world support for candidate c is unknown and subsequent records have not arrived; “calibrated” or “zero residual” must not be recorded in advance."},{"title":"Teaching construction: conditional recommendation is not execution","text":"In another fictional comparison, option A costs 2 and B costs 5; all relevant effects and risks are stipulated equal, and the explicit norm is “choose lower cost when the same protection conditions are met.” A may be analytically preferred because it costs 3 less. The counterargument is unexamined hidden maintenance cost; revision conditions are A's additional costs exceeding 3 or differing protection conditions. No actual permissions are supplied, so the advice remains for deliberation, without purchasing or scheduling.","assumptions":"Equal effects and risks are strong teaching assumptions, not a claim that real options require only financial comparison.","boundary":"The preference cannot be copied without explicit values or comparative evidence; cost definitions and rights floors also require actual verification."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A snapshot and path diagram exist, so foresight capability has improved.","effect":"Without preregistration and outcome/baseline evaluation, this is organizational preparation, not evidence of gain.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Without execution authority, even a conditional preference cannot be proposed.","effect":"Advice for deliberation may be offered where evidence and explicit norms permit, retaining the permission gap.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Results have not arrived, so enter 0 for the residual to complete the table.","effect":"Not yet calculable means unknown or not applicable, not zero difference.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If the actual 09:00 outcome has not arrived, may residual 0 be registered?","answer":"No. The residual is not yet calculable and must remain unresolved.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"With equal effects and risks and an explicit low-cost objective, how may A costing 2 and B costing 5 be compared?","answer":"Under these strong assumptions, analytically prefer A, saving 3, while stating the counterargument and revision conditions; do not execute without authority.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What should a prediction-separation statement distinguish?","answer":"Conditional derivation, observed fact, predictive performance, normative judgment, and actual execution permission must remain separate; one level does not automatically upgrade to the next.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-12/figures/section-11-12.png","alt":"Starting point and inputs: frozen snapshot / event evidence, mechanism and parameter assumptions; paths and changes: steps / conditions / clocks, support and positive/contrary signals; unknowns and write-back: residual candidates / stopping, plan for actual-outcome comparison; conditional preparation: does not automatically prove predictive gain; analytical preference: explicit norms and costs, counterarguments and revision conditions; actual execution: still needs valid authorization, not generated by analysis. The upper row shows output responsibilities; lower-row statuses are separate, not an automatic upgrading ladder.","caption":"The upper row shows output responsibilities; lower-row statuses are separate, not an automatic upgrading ladder."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. The lower row has no connecting lines, avoiding portrayal of a conditional recommendation as granted permission.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section is a normative checklist of deliverable contents and uses. A minimal complete construction can test the fields' roles; unrelated real stories are unnecessary as supposed proof of correctness."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-11-12","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2430–2435; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.12: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.12 Outputs of This Part; Chinese source lines 2484–2489; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-12/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-12/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-12/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-12/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"96da74e04c28351b185613500465c50c6c58f71ad4324a394e6ff72044a3cafd","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"11.13":{"title":"11.13 Action Choice and Conditional Updating","lead":"Action is more than choosing from a fixed menu. Acting changes resources, rules, relationships, and what others can do; the next step must begin from these new conditions rather than ask the model to reevaluate the same old menu.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2490–2499; original anchor: section-11-13; short quotation, translated from the Chinese: “Generating another narrative cannot replace state updating.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","At each step identify currently available and excluded actions, specifying whether limits come from capability, resources, time, relationships, institutions, or permissions. Exclusion may mean currently impossible or impermissible; distinguish them. Technically feasible unauthorized actions do not thereby enter the model's implementable action set.","Capability, belief, willingness, and actual action require separate evidence. Ability does not imply willingness; willingness does not imply belief in effectiveness; public intention does not mean execution; and an analyst's recommendation certainly does not mean adoption by the actor. Retain conditional paths for uncertain choices rather than decide for actors to advance another round.","Place possible choices within the actor's information, resources, relationships, habits, and objectives at the time. These may constrain or support strategies without completely determining the person absent identification; group tendencies also cannot fill all individual states. When anticipated responses of others change present strategy, anticipated reflexivity may be explicitly included while retaining its model-assumption status.","For each evidenced choice, specify triggering mechanisms, first changed states, transmission interfaces, affected objects and clocks, and calculate how these differences change the next action set. Concrete state differences may include reduced balances, new rule restrictions, changed relational commitments, updated beliefs, or more/fewer feasible tasks; “the system improves” is insufficient.","Later projections inherit new conditions along with unresolved unknowns and limits. Selecting a hypothetical branch does not mean reality has excluded the others; resources not yet arrived belong only in conditional branches. Giving another model the same material generates another narrative version; without new inputs, rules, or state differences, it cannot be registered as progression of the world.","When actual feedback arrives, first check source, event status, time, and duplicated underlying sources, then compare original and competing paths and simple baselines. Deviations may come from incorrect initial facts, mistaken mechanisms, an actor choosing differently, execution departing from plan, or changed external conditions. These five categories point to different revisions; not every deviation means “people are uncooperative” or “the model needs more complexity.”","Correction should change the states, parameters, mechanism scope, or paths actually used next round. Discovering that approval does not mean arrival, for example, requires revising resource availability, not merely logging “pay attention to authorization.” Saving feedback text while keeping choice rules and used states unchanged does not establish learning. Even an actual rule change still requires testing whether it improves future tasks.","This section connects dynamic projection to Part XII's choice and calibration. It automatically supplies neither value targets nor actual execution permission. Analysis may clearly identify a preferable branch or recommend delay under declared targets, while evidence, norms, feasibility, and authorization remain separately registered."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Able to act","leftText":"Capability, resources, and other conditions make an action technically feasible.","rightTitle":"Will act or has acted","rightText":"Willingness, choice, and actual occurrence each need grounds."},{"leftTitle":"Different choice","leftText":"The actor actually takes another available branch.","rightTitle":"Execution deviation","rightText":"The chosen plan is implemented differently; this is a different error."},{"leftTitle":"Evidenced concrete state update","leftText":"Actual changes need factual material; simulated changes need explicit conditions, each retaining its status.","rightTitle":"Narrative version change","rightText":"Rewording or redescribing with another model does not mean the state changed."},{"leftTitle":"Feedback archived","leftText":"Material is preserved.","rightTitle":"Next-round rule changed","rightText":"The revision actually enters later use; effectiveness still requires testing."},{"leftTitle":"Normative analytical recommendation","leftText":"Compares options under explicit targets.","rightTitle":"Actor adoption","rightText":"Actual decision and execution material cannot be replaced by a recommendation."}],"boundaries":["Do not choose for an actor because the model requires a definite action.","Technical feasibility cannot override permissions; actual unauthorized behavior still enters factual records.","Behavioral explanation automatically neither excuses nor blames; deviations do not diagnose personality.","An implemented revision does not prove permanent immunity to failure; extrapolation remains bounded."],"questions":["With charge 5, after A consumes 3, can B consume 4 next?","Is a plan failing because tools did not arrive the same as personnel actively choosing another plan?","What evidence shows that correction entered the next round?","Does a changed rule establish general learning success?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-13/figures/section-11-13.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"2017 S3 retrospective: explicit reported changes in tool rules","href":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"2017 S3 retrospective: explicit reported changes in tool rules","text":"In its retrospective of the 2017 S3 incident, AWS stated that it modified the tool to remove capacity more slowly and added safeguards preventing subsystems from falling below required minimum capacity. This section uses only the point that “revision should enter actual operating rules.”","sourceLabel":"AWS incident review","sourceUrl":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/","sourceLocator":"Main text paragraph 3, beginning “We are making several changes…”","boundary":"The operator's report of a modified tool, not an independent audit by this lesson or proof of zero future failures. It does not establish completion of every improvement plan.","href":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"11.13","start_line":2562,"end_line":2575,"startLine":2562,"endLine":2575}],"outputId":"section-11-13","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["List available and excluded actions from current conditions, distinguishing sources in capability, constraints, and permissions.","Separate beliefs, willingness, and evidence of occurrence, retaining conditional branches for uncertain choices.","Specify mechanisms and concrete state differences triggered by each choice, then recalculate the next feasible set.","Inherit unknowns and limits; repeated generation is not state change.","After verifying actual feedback, distinguish facts, mechanisms, choices, execution, and external changes; revise items actually used next round and test them separately."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: choice changes the next menu","text":"A fictional robot has charge 5; task A consumes 3, B consumes 4, and permitted waiting consumes 0. Every task must leave nonnegative charge. Initially A, B, and waiting are all technically feasible. Actually doing A first leaves charge 2, making both A and B infeasible next; only waiting or a separately grounded replenishment action remains. Doing B first leaves 1, also preventing A. Treating “both tasks are feasible initially” as permission to perform both sequentially ignores shared resources.","assumptions":"Deterministic consumption, no automatic replenishment, indivisible tasks, and corresponding operating permission are stipulated; this is not actual robot control.","boundary":"Actual charging or resource changes must be registered before updating; replenishment cannot be presumed afterward."},{"title":"Teaching construction: five deviation types must not collapse into one cause","text":"A fictional queue predicts 5 awaiting repair next hour, but the actual record is 8. Factual error: the initial count may not have been 6. Mechanism error: processing rates may vary. Different choice: personnel selected another allowed task. Execution deviation: repair was chosen but tools did not arrive. External change: arrivals suddenly increased. These are competing diagnostic tasks, not grounds to select a cause without material. If tool nonarrival is verified, revise execution and resource states without first changing personality parameters.","assumptions":"Teaching candidates, not events established as having occurred; several may act together.","boundary":"The causal categories are neither mutually exclusive nor exhaustive; actual revision requires sources and discriminating material."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: storing feedback and changing rules can be distinguished explicitly","text":"A fictional old rule chooses action a consuming 3 even when stock x=2, violating nonnegativity. After receiving a verified failure record, merely adding “be careful next time” while retaining the rule selects a again. If the allowed set becomes {a:consumption≤x} and is actually used next round, a is excluded at x=2. Rule change is checkable, but its overall performance in other future contexts still requires testing.","assumptions":"Actions, states, and failure records are teaching assumptions; no actual actions were executed or systems trained.","boundary":"Proves only exclusion of a under the given constraint, not global optimality or general learning success."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A and B are both initially feasible, so A followed by B is also feasible.","effect":"Shared charge changes later options: 5−3=2 is insufficient for B's 4.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model received and saved a failure report, so it learned to avoid failure.","effect":"If next-round states and rules remain unchanged, archiving is not the relevant correction; even changed rules need effect validation.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"An actual outcome differs from prediction, so the actor must lack willingness.","effect":"Facts, mechanisms, choices, execution, and external changes are competing explanations; responsibility cannot be assigned to one cause.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"With charge 5, after A consumes 3, can B consume 4 next?","answer":"No; 2 remains. Actual new replenishment evidence is required to change this judgment.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is a plan failing because tools did not arrive the same as personnel actively choosing another plan?","answer":"They differ: the former concerns execution or resource conditions, the latter a different choice path, requiring revisions at different points.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What evidence shows that correction entered the next round?","answer":"Evidence that the states, allowed set, parameters, mechanism scope, or paths actually used next round were appropriately changed; storing text alone is insufficient.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a changed rule establish general learning success?","answer":"No. First confirm actual application of the change, then examine gain using corresponding later outcomes and baselines.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-11-13/figures/section-11-13.png","alt":"Teaching charge 5, A consumes 3, B consumes 4, each initially feasible; if A actually runs first, charge becomes 5−3=2, not merely a textual update; next-step charge 2 makes both A and B infeasible, with replenishment requiring separate grounds; first verify actual feedback status: facts / mechanisms / choices, execution / external changes; revise corresponding items actually used: states / parameters / rules, mechanism scope / paths; test new outcomes again: archiving is not learning, and modification is not permanent correctness. Robot values are teaching constructions; the lower row shows correction responsibilities, without claims of training or actual testing.","caption":"Robot values are teaching constructions; the lower row shows correction responsibilities, without claims of training or actual testing."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. The upper row shows shared resources constraining subsequent action; the lower separates modification from effect validation.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This firsthand retrospective distinguishes actual tool changes from later plans, illustrating concrete rule updating beyond recording feedback."},"openQuestions":["AWS tool code and independent test results were not obtained; only bounded facts from its public report are retained. This lesson performed no actual scheduling or model training."],"taskId":"S-11-13","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2436–2445; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["11.13: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"aws2017","title":"Summary of the Amazon S3 Service Disruption in the Northern Virginia (US-EAST-1) Region","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/41926/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Retrospective of the 2017 incident","locator":"Main text paragraph 3, We are making several changes…","claim_support":["The operator reports modifications to the capacity-removal tool and minimum-capacity safeguards"],"limits":["No tool code, independent audit, or long-term causal effects were obtained; future plans in the report are not recorded as complete."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"11.13 Action Choice and Conditional Updating; Chinese source lines 2490–2499; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-13/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-13/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-11-13/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-11-13/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"a8a98303f15b5b5f38c95b2f6efaa167f4ad8ffc84934848c90af00939720435","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/part-11.md","filePath":"en/theory/part-11.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/part-11.md"};function u(h,e,p,m,f,g){const t=o("SectionLesson");return r(),c("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=n("div",{class:"theory-source"},[n("p",null,[n("a",{id:"part_11"})]),n("h1",{id:"part-title-11",tabindex:"-1"},[l("Part 11 Event-Driven Dynamic Projection "),n("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#part-title-11","aria-label":'Permalink to "Part 11 Event-Driven Dynamic Projection {#part-title-11}"'},"​")]),n("p",null,"Dynamic projection starts from a joint state with evidential statuses recorded. It analyzes state differences, transmission, feedback, and branching after observed, reported, planned, hypothetical, or simulated events. Actions that actually occur can change reality with or without authorization. They must all be recorded according to the evidence. Whether an action occurred, how it is evaluated normatively, any legal or institutional authority, and permission to carry it out on this occasion must each be judged separately. Projection must specify which changes can be established directly, which are only conditional consequences, and which signals would support or weaken the corresponding paths.")],-1)),a(t,{section:"intro-11-part"}),e[1]||(e[1]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Difference Between Projection and Narrative Continuation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.1 The Difference Between Projection and Narrative Continuation {#section-11-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Narrative continuation can choose what comes next by coherence alone. Structural projection must preserve conditions, channels, competing paths, and the meaning of failure. If key variables are missing, the projection should stop at an unknown or generate variable candidates. It must not fill the gaps with the smoothest story. If several continuations are consistent with existing evidence, keep them as separate branches. If paths are incomparable, do not force an ordering.</p><p>The minimum inputs to projection are a frozen snapshot, event records, mechanism hypotheses, parameter ranges, a clock policy, a propagation policy, unknowns, and stopping conditions. The minimum outputs are a path graph, state differences at each step, support status, early signals, contrary signals, residuals, variable candidates, and write-back requirements.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>What Must Be Frozen</th><th>Consequence If Left Unfrozen</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Joint snapshot</td><td>Actors, circles<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup>, relations, M/Ψ, clocks, and evidence cutoff</td><td>The starting point is rewritten after the results</td></tr><tr><td>Event</td><td>Type, time, source, objects reached, and channels</td><td>Rumors, plans, and facts are conflated</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Direction, carrier, delay, threshold, and failure conditions</td><td>Each outcome can be explained arbitrarily</td></tr><tr><td>Parameter</td><td>Value, interval, level, or explicit unknown</td><td>False precision masks uncertainty</td></tr><tr><td>Stopping</td><td>Conditions involving information, risk, exceeding bounds, and resources</td><td>The story continues indefinitely</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.1"}),e[2]||(e[2]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Event Contract<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn2" id="fnref2">[2]</a></sup> <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.2 The Event Contract[^tn-contract-12] {#section-11-2}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>There are five kinds of event: observed, reported, planned, hypothetical, and simulated. The type must be included in the record. A reported event can affect people who believe it but must not automatically be treated as having occurred. A plan may change expectations without amounting to execution. A hypothetical event serves conditional analysis. A simulated event belongs only to a model run and must not be written back as a fact about reality.</p><p>An event record must include at least the event ID, time or window of occurrence, time of observation, source, affected actors, affected circles, channels, direct changes, evidential status, disputes, competing explanations, reversibility, duration, and subsequent observations.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Event Type</th><th>Permitted Uses in Projection</th><th>Factual Status</th><th>Required Limits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Observed</td><td>Explanation, replay, and forward projection</td><td>Holds within the observation contract</td><td>Sources, omissions, and measurement error</td></tr><tr><td>Reported</td><td>Propagation of belief and conditional projection</td><td>That a report was made does not establish that its contents are true</td><td>The reporting party’s position, verification, and disputes</td></tr><tr><td>Planned</td><td>Expectations, preparations, and strategic interaction</td><td>Existence of the plan does not establish execution</td><td>Authorization, resources, and conditions for cancellation</td></tr><tr><td>Hypothetical</td><td>Counterfactuals and scenario comparison</td><td>No factual status in reality</td><td>Conditions, purpose, and feasibility</td></tr><tr><td>Simulated</td><td>Unfolding paths within the model</td><td>Model state only</td><td>Model version, parameters, and prohibition on extrapolation</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.2"}),e[3]||(e[3]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Joint State Update Formula <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.3 The Joint State Update Formula {#section-11-3}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The joint state can be written as follows.</p><p><code>Ω(t) = &lt;A(t), C(t), R(t), M(t), Ψ(t), Q(t), E≤t, SP(t), W(t), K(t)&gt;</code>.</p><p>An update can be written as follows.</p><p><code>Ω(t+Δ) = F[Ω(t), e(t), u(t), ξ(t) | θ, h]</code>.</p><p>Here <code>e(t)</code> is the event, <code>u(t)</code> is an externally authorized action that actually occurred, <code>ξ(t)</code> is an exogenous disturbance or unmodeled residual, <code>θ</code> denotes the frozen hypotheses about mechanisms and parameters, and <code>h</code> is the historical term. The formula specifies only what must be recorded. It does not assert that there is a unique true F, and the function symbol must not be used in place of specific mechanisms.</p><p>Observed violations, actions beyond authorization, refusals to carry out orders, and unauthorized acts in another’s stead are recorded as <code>observed/reported</code> events under <code>e(t)</code> with their evidence. The actor, consequences, and status of their authority must be recorded separately. The lack of authority must not be grounds for deleting an event from the factual record. Violations that have not occurred enter only <code>hypothetical/simulated</code> branches. The definition of <code>u(t)</code> still requires authorization and actual occurrence. Feasibility in a scenario does not amount to permission in reality. 〔Studies 15.08, 21.03, 21.08, 28.06, 32.08〕</p><p>Each update must distinguish directly observed state differences, inferences supported by the mechanism contract, scenario values set to explore a path, and residuals still unexplained. All four retain their provenance in subsequent paths. Appearing together in a snapshot does not give them the same evidential status.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.3"}),e[4]||(e[4]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-3-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.3.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>States, Observations, and Identified Sets <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-3-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.3.1 States, Observations, and Identified Sets {#section-11-3-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A joint snapshot is a structured representation of the material currently available. It does not directly read every state of the world. Let <code>z(t)</code> denote the actual state yet to be inferred for the current task, and <code>o(t)</code> the record produced by observation rule H, sampling positions, and conditions of data collection. It can be written as <code>o(t)=H[z(t),m(t),ε(t)]</code>. <code>m(t)</code> includes who can enter the record, when they are measured, and which fields are withheld from disclosure for protection. <code>ε(t)</code> is used only when there is a corresponding error model. Unknowns must not all be called random noise. When conditions allow, numerical measurements can use additive error. Text and categorical records use their corresponding generation rules. Ω should preserve the distinction between the records and the judgments, so a change in observation rule is not mistaken for a change in the object.</p><p>The same set of records can correspond to several states or mechanisms. Let <code>Z(o)</code> denote the set of states still compatible with the record under the stated model and error bounds. If the answer to question q is the same in every state in <code>Z(o)</code>, the current material is sufficient for the question. A limited answer can be given without recovering every latent variable. If the values differ, the output should retain their range, any direction that can be identified, or the parts that genuinely remain unknown. Failure to detect a difference in a finite sample does not automatically establish that only one answer remains over the entire set.</p><p>The set also helps plan the next observation. First identify state distinctions that would change the current answer or the comparison of actions. Then check which feasible observations could distinguish them. More precision that leaves the answer unchanged need not help. A low-cost observation that narrows an important branch may be more valuable than expanding the whole variable table. Data collection remains subject to purpose, necessity, and protection. A need for identification does not confer permission to collect data.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.3.1"}),e[5]||(e[5]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-3-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.3.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Event Processing and State Continuation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-3-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.3.2 Event Processing and State Continuation {#section-11-3-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each input retains the time of the event, the time the record was obtained, and the time it entered the model. Material that arrives late can change what is now known about the past, but it cannot rewrite the information set available at the time. When correcting a source, append the link to the correction and recompute the states and paths that depend on that source. Retain local results that do not depend on it. When multiple sources recount one event, retain those sources and a single candidate identity for the event to avoid applying the same shock more than once. Events with similar descriptions must not be merged without verification.</p><p>State updating consists of two operations. An evidence update revises the judgment about <code>z(t)</code>. Advancing the mechanism estimates the change from <code>z(t)</code> to a subsequent state. A credible record that “work stopped yesterday” is an evidence update today. The stoppage itself belongs to yesterday’s real events. Rerunning a model and obtaining another response that work “may have stopped” adds only simulated material. Both kinds of update can change Ω, but their reasons and evidential statuses must be kept separate.</p><p>Concurrent events may be combined in any order only if the operations commute, shared resources are not in conflict, and neither changes the other’s conditions. If two actions compete for a resource, overwrite the same record, or change each other’s available action set, retain branches for the possible orders or specify an explicit rule for concurrency. Similar timestamps do not establish simultaneity. The order in which execution calls return does not by itself reveal the causal order of real events.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.3.2"}),e[6]||(e[6]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Nine-Step Projection Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4 The Nine-Step Projection Loop {#section-11-4}&quot;">​</a></h2></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4"}),e[7]||(e[7]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step One. Freeze the Current State <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.1 Step One. Freeze the Current State {#section-11-4-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Record the evidence cutoff, objects, variables, unknowns, disputes, and model version. Information obtained after the cutoff must not be fed back into the starting point. If it needs to be used, create a new version of the run.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.1"}),e[8]||(e[8]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step Two. Identify Actors and Circles <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.2 Step Two. Identify Actors and Circles {#section-11-4-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Use Parts 9 and 10. If a circle has no grounds for object facts and the grouping gain claimed for it is not supported, retain it as a candidate grouping. If there is insufficient support for a personality hypothesis, retain it as a variable candidate. Do not turn candidates into entities merely to complete the graph.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.2"}),e[9]||(e[9]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step Three. Separate Conditions in the Two Channels <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.3 Step Three. Separate Conditions in the Two Channels {#section-11-4-3}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>List the relevant material conditions separately for the task. Also list the relevant experience–meaning conditions and declare the bridges needed between channels. If missing information from the other channel affects the current judgment, retain the unknown. Do not fill the gap by assuming symmetry. A channel genuinely irrelevant to the current task may be marked not applicable with a reason. Do not collect experiential material for the sake of completing the form. Suspend only propagation that depends on the missing channel. Independently established states and paths can still be carried forward.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.3"}),e[10]||(e[10]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step Four. Declare Clocks, Thresholds, Capacity, and Delay <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.4 Step Four. Declare Clocks, Thresholds, Capacity, and Delay {#section-11-4-4}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Identify each variable’s update clock. If a channel has capacity limits, delay, loss, saturation, reversal, or a recovery window, register them before propagation.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.4"}),e[11]||(e[11]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step Five. Inject an Event or Action <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.5 Step Five. Inject an Event or Action {#section-11-4-5}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A run may include multiple events and actions ordered by time, with their independent sources retained. First determine whether an action occurred, then record the actor, object, consequences, and authorization status separately. Lack of authorization cannot justify deleting a fact about what actually occurred. Plans, proposals, and simulated actions that have yet to occur retain their conditional status and must not be recorded as executed. If a model initiates or carries out a real-world action, valid authorization must be obtained separately. A recommendation for deliberation requires only that its evidence, normative premises, costs, gaps in authority, and conditions for revising the judgment be stated clearly.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.5"}),e[12]||(e[12]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step Six. Propagate Along Declared Channels <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.6 Step Six. Propagate Along Declared Channels {#section-11-4-6}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>First record directly confirmable state differences, then analyze return signals, indirect effects, and cross-circle cascades. An identified total effect of an intervention may enter a bounded judgment about the outcome. Unidentified parts of the internal mechanism remain unknown. The total effect must not be used to fill in missing edges of propagation. Explaining specific propagation requires corresponding material about channels and temporal order. Where there is only co-occurrence or correlation, retain common background, selection effects, and other competing explanations. Store state differences derived conditionally from the model separately from those observed in reality.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.6"}),e[13]||(e[13]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step Seven. Generate Paths at Branch Points <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.7 Step Seven. Generate Paths at Branch Points {#section-11-4-7}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Conditions, thresholds, actor choices, external disturbances, or uncertainty about mechanisms can all produce branching. Keep mutually exclusive paths separate. Retain parallel paths if they can occur at the same time. Create a merge node only when the conditions for merging are clear.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.7"}),e[14]||(e[14]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step Eight. Register Signals, Residuals, and Variable Candidates <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.8 Step Eight. Register Signals, Residuals, and Variable Candidates {#section-11-4-8}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>List early and contrary signals for each path. State differences that the current variables cannot explain enter the residuals. A residual can prompt an entry in the ledger of variable candidates, but cannot automatically supply a value in reality.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.8"}),e[15]||(e[15]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-4-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.4.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Step Nine. Write Back the Results <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-4-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.4.9 Step Nine. Write Back the Results {#section-11-4-9}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When actual results arrive, append which paths are supported, when results diverged from the projection, whether a simple baseline performed better, how well the projection was calibrated, and which mechanisms or variables should be downgraded, split, given revised boundaries, or retired. Write-back does not overwrite the original run.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.4.9"}),e[16]||(e[16]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Propagation, Delay, and Thresholds <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.5 Propagation, Delay, and Thresholds {#section-11-5}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An account of propagation must at least distinguish the arrival of a signal from an effective change of state. If information is seen but does not change a subsequent transition, record only arrival. If it changes beliefs without prompting action, record the change in experience–meaning state. Register a corresponding structural change only if the signal changes resources, behavior, rules, or relationships.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Propagation Factor</th><th>Question for the Record</th><th>Possible Nonlinearity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Channel</td><td>What carrier conveys the impact from the source to the target?</td><td>Channel closure, filtering, or substitution</td></tr><tr><td>Capacity</td><td>How much can be carried per unit of time?</td><td>Saturation, congestion, and queues</td></tr><tr><td>Delay</td><td>When does it arrive, and when does it take effect?</td><td>Delayed feedback or a mistaken finding of no effect</td></tr><tr><td>Threshold</td><td>What conditions must be reached before the state changes?</td><td>Abrupt changes, hysteresis, and cascades</td></tr><tr><td>Loss</td><td>What is lost or changed in propagation?</td><td>Drift in meaning or dissipation of resources</td></tr><tr><td>Direction</td><td>Is the impact symmetric?</td><td>One-way dependence or offsetting effects in the opposite direction</td></tr><tr><td>Recovery</td><td>How does the object return to its previous state or move to a new one after a shock?</td><td>Resilience, cumulative damage, and lock-in</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>When the same event reaches different circles through different channels, the effects can be opposite. Additional resources may reduce load in one circle but intensify competition in another. A public statement may repair external legitimacy but activate shame or distrust within the circle. A projection should record each channel separately. Net effects must not hide differences in distribution.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.5"}),e[17]||(e[17]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Feedback and Cross-Circle Cascades <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.6 Feedback and Cross-Circle Cascades {#section-11-6}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Feedback requires a prior state or output to change a subsequent state, transition probability, or constraint through a return channel. A cascade requires a local change to trigger changes in other circles through membership overlap, bridging, shared resources, top-down institutional transmission, or network propagation. Both require evidence of temporal order and channels.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind of Cascade</th><th>Typical Chain</th><th>What to Observe</th><th>Stopping Condition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Membership</td><td>Overlapping members carry behavior, emotions, or information into another circle</td><td>Actual transmission as distinct from shared background</td><td>Members stop participating or information is not received</td></tr><tr><td>Resources</td><td>One circle takes up, releases, or reallocates resources</td><td>Accounting boundaries, conversion, and loss</td><td>Resources are isolated or an alternative source is available</td></tr><tr><td>Meaning</td><td>An interpretation of the event changes identity, legitimacy, or trust</td><td>Differences in interpretation across positions</td><td>The interpretation does not reach other circles or does not change their experience–meaning states. Effects on action or rules are checked separately</td></tr><tr><td>Institutions</td><td>An appeal, audit, or event changes rules that are then implemented at lower levels</td><td>Decisions, execution, and actual write-back</td><td>The rule is not implemented or is offset locally</td></tr><tr><td>Platforms</td><td>Recommendations, metrics, or public evaluations amplify propagation</td><td>Algorithms, selective visibility, and reflexivity</td><td>Exposure stops increasing or the audience does not respond</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The importance of a cascade does not automatically increase with scale. A small circle can have a large impact through a crucial bridge, while a large circle may have no effective propagation because its channels are blocked. A projection should focus on positions, channels, and thresholds. Membership counts alone are insufficient.</p><p>Two local effects can form a transmission chain only with shared conditions for response. Consider two subgroups of equal weight. In the first, the event changes the mediator, but the mediator does not affect the outcome. In the second, the mediator can affect the outcome, but the event cannot change the mediator. The mean effect on each edge is one-half, yet the effect of the event on the outcome across the entire chain can still be zero. This finite construction shows that the mean edge effects cannot be multiplied unconditionally even if the two studies use the same population. It is also necessary to know whether the successive effects occur in objects, states, and times that allow them to connect.</p><p>A cascade record should therefore check how the output of the preceding segment enters the next one, the scope of support for joint response, capacity and delay, and offsetting and parallel paths. A limited total effect already identified through an eligible comparison can be retained directly. What is added here is the responsibility to explain the specific propagation. It does not require explaining all micromechanisms before acknowledging any effect. When an edge has yet to be identified, retain the independently established upstream changes and terminal effects according to their own evidence.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.6"}),e[18]||(e[18]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>A Graph of Branching Paths <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.7 A Graph of Branching Paths {#section-11-7}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A path node must include at least the parent node, condition set, triggering event or action, state differences, affected clocks, support status, a probability or ordering expression, early signals, contrary signals, the next node, and the reason for termination. The path graph should be a directed acyclic graph. If there is a feedback loop in reality, represent it with new nodes unfolded over time. A node must not be its own cause and effect within the same run.</p><p>Four kinds of uncertainty can produce branches: uncertainty about facts, uncertainty about mechanisms, actor choices, and exogenous disturbances. Different kinds should not be merged into a single vague probability. Give priority to verifying uncertain facts. Address mechanism uncertainty through observations that distinguish the alternatives. For uncertainty about choices, retain strategies and authorization. For exogenous disturbances, use scenario ranges and resilience checks.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Source of Branching</th><th>Example</th><th>Appropriate Output</th><th>Inappropriate Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Facts</td><td>Did the event actually occur?</td><td>Conditional paths before verification</td><td>Choosing one version as fact</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanisms</td><td>Does silence come from fear or strategy?</td><td>Signals that distinguish the alternatives and parallel paths</td><td>Shutting down inquiry with a personality label</td></tr><tr><td>Choice</td><td>An actor cooperates, resists, or exits</td><td>Conditions and consequences of each choice</td><td>Claiming that free choices can be predicted precisely</td></tr><tr><td>Disturbance</td><td>Change in policy, prices, disasters, or technology</td><td>Stress scenarios and conditions for recovery</td><td>Attributing unmodeled residuals to fate</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.7"}),e[19]||(e[19]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Ledger of Variable Candidates <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.8 The Ledger of Variable Candidates {#section-11-8}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Residuals that the model cannot explain must neither be deleted nor immediately named as real variables. The candidate ledger turns “what might we have missed?” into a testable task. Each candidate record must include at least the source residual, name, possible type, possible channel, observable implications, competing candidates, minimal test, privacy and risk, current status, evidence, and conditions for upgrading or rejecting it.</p><p>Candidate statuses include proposed, under test, supported candidate, rejected, and retired. Even with support, a candidate still has to enter the relevant object or variable contract. It must not directly become a stable personality, a supported circle, or a causal mechanism. Candidates proposed after results can improve the next model but cannot retroactively support the original prediction.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Candidate Source</th><th>Permitted Action</th><th>What Must Be Avoided</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Residuals in the system</td><td>Propose several competing candidates and observations that distinguish them</td><td>Absorbing all errors into one mysterious variable</td></tr><tr><td>Account by a person concerned</td><td>Preserve the person’s position, the meaning of their account, and its verifiable parts</td><td>Automatically downgrading it to subjective noise or elevating it to a complete account of the facts</td></tr><tr><td>Recurrence across cases</td><td>Establish candidate mechanisms and limits of extrapolation</td><td>Treating similar narratives as universal laws</td></tr><tr><td>Model search</td><td>Pre-register the next round of testing</td><td>Selecting the best results for reporting after seeing them</td></tr><tr><td>AI suggestions</td><td>Generate and compare candidates. Form judgments and analytical recommendations when the evidence, permitted uses, and explicit normative premises allow</td><td>Letting AI output verify facts about reality or authorize action</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The framework can only identify residuals the current set of variables cannot explain, propose several distinguishable variable candidates, and design a minimal test with acceptable risk for each. It cannot decide on its own that a candidate is true in reality.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.8"}),e[20]||(e[20]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Scenarios, Counterfactuals, and Simulation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.9 Scenarios, Counterfactuals, and Simulation {#section-11-9}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Scenario projection asks, “If these conditions hold, what paths emerge?” A counterfactual asks, “If an event or mechanism had been different, how might the outcome that actually occurred have changed?” Simulation unfolds specified rules and parameters. All three must be kept separate from descriptions of fact.</p><p>Counterfactuals especially require comparable conditions. When removing an event, also state which subsequent states, actors’ information, and circle relations change as a result. Do not remove an unwelcome cause while retaining all its consequences. The precision of a simulation cannot exceed what its inputs and evidence of mechanisms support. When parameters are unknown, use ranges, levels, or sensitivity analysis. Do not generate unsupported decimal places.</p><p>Counterfactuals must preserve the information boundaries at the time. Replacing an event or choice means reassessing the beliefs, resources, orders, and downstream behavior it affects. Do not remove an announcement while holding fixed the attendance and subsequent execution it brought about. Evidence for a short-range branch does not mean an alternative history decades later is already knowable. The actions of ordinary people, the discretion of frontline personnel, and organizational constraints can all enter the explanation. There is no need to assign causal percentages without a basis for identifying them. 〔Studies 03.03, 32.04, 32.08〕</p><p>The strength of a counterfactual conclusion depends on whether mechanisms still compatible with the evidence give the same answer under the altered operation. Two mechanisms can both produce observations of <code>X=M=Y</code>. In one, X changes Y through M. In the other, X changes M and Y separately. This co-movement alone cannot establish whether setting M directly would change Y. More observations of the same kind can improve the precision of the record but need not resolve this equivalence between mechanisms. A design that distinguishes the two mechanisms or independently grounded assumptions are needed. If independent materials already identify the total effect of a specified treatment within stated limits, retain that effect even if a particular mediator has yet to be identified.</p><p>Counterfactual analysis can therefore produce one answer, a set of feasible answers, or conditional bounds. Treat a direction as robust only when all compatible mechanisms support the same conclusion. If the conclusion reverses across plausible mechanisms, report the conditions under which it reverses and list the evidence needed to distinguish the mechanisms as the next observation. Historically, “removing a person from the post they came to occupy” and “that person having never been there” have different starting points and processes of adaptation. They cannot share the same counterfactual answer.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.9"}),e[21]||(e[21]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-9-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.9.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Multi-Order Recursive Projection of the Future <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-9-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.9.1 Multi-Order Recursive Projection of the Future {#section-11-9-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Advancing time over multiple steps, re-entry from a simulated state, and anticipatory reflexivity are three orthogonal dimensions. Advancing time over multiple steps carries a state forward in time within a single run. Simulation re-entry uses a simulated future generated in the first round as the starting point for a child run. Anticipatory reflexivity occurs when actors in the simulation form expectations about the more distant future or other people’s responses and change their current strategies. These three operations must not be conflated under the single term “recursion.”</p><p>Three orders are one default way to organize exploratory projection. The first unfolds direct consequences and competing paths. The second checks how those changes alter the available actions, resources, relationships, and adaptation. The third checks institutionalization, lock-in, reversal, and changes in conditions for generation. The orders mark responsibilities in projection. They do not amount to three points in time, natural stages, or levels of capability. Do not force recursion for static questions or tasks without a distinct question at the next order. Stop when conditions are insufficient.</p><p>The layer that generates possibilities may be bold. The state layer must be strict, the evidence layer must not raise its own status, and the action layer still needs separate authorization. Boldness can expand the space of plausible mechanisms, competing paths, and branches with low probability and severe consequences. It also helps produce clear judgments within the limits of the evidence. The strength of a judgment is supported only by evidence, conditions of applicability, and permitted uses. Tone or the number of generated possibilities cannot make it stronger.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.9.1"}),e[22]||(e[22]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-9-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.9.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Recursive Interfaces and Lineage <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-9-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.9.2 Recursive Interfaces and Lineage {#section-11-9-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Before each re-entry, conduct a recasting audit. Record the current order, parent run, parent path, parent node, simulated time, the current order’s time window and the cumulative window, model version, inherited assumptions, new conditions, what is preserved, changed, collapsed, or omitted, new unknowns, effective variables for the target, residuals, identity across re-entry, the return path, and the reason for stopping. The simulated starting point must not be rewritten as present reality.</p><p>A child run must inherit the unresolved unknowns, losses, and residuals of its parent run. Selecting a hypothetical branch only narrows the conditions. It does not mean material from reality has eliminated the uncertainty. Increasing the number of draws may reduce computational error. It does not increase empirical support about outcomes in reality.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.9.2"}),e[23]||(e[23]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-9-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.9.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Branching, Merging, and Pruning <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-9-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.9.3 Branching, Merging, and Pruning {#section-11-9-3}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>At each order, check whether there are substantive grounds for the main path, its strongest competitor, a path with low probability and severe consequences, and a route for residuals. Retain all conditions for paths that have grounds. If a category is inapplicable or a path of that kind cannot be constructed, explain why. Do not invent an event to fill a category. Branches must correspond to explainable differences in mechanisms, choices by actors, external disturbances, or unknowns of a specified kind. Retaining a path with severe consequences means only that it calls for investigation or preparation. It does not establish that its real-world probability is known.</p><p>Branches may be merged only when object identity is preserved, key states are equivalent within a predetermined tolerance, differences in history do not change subsequent transitions, and the available actions and constraints agree going forward. Pruning rules must be declared in advance. Preserve pruned branches, reasons for pruning, any possible probability mass, and the level of harm. Low probability alone cannot justify removing a path with severe consequences.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.9.3"}),e[24]||(e[24]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-11-9-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.9.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Early Stopping <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-9-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.9.4 Early Stopping {#section-11-9-4}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Stop or downgrade if object identity or scale identity fails, key assumptions cannot be inherited, the transition mechanism falls outside its domain, the recursive interface lacks closure, or branches proliferate without signals to distinguish them. Also stop or downgrade if the results reverse across the board under small changes, residuals exceed the threshold for the intended use, a deeper order adds nothing relative to a shallower one and a simple baseline, the calibrated time window is exceeded, a boundary of rights or authorization is reached, or the third-order limit is reached. The stopping record must state what can still be said and what cannot.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.9.4"}),e[25]||(e[25]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Stopping Conditions for a Projection Run <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.10 Stopping Conditions for a Projection Run {#section-11-10}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Affected propositions and the paths that depend on them should be suspended or downgraded if a key object lacks grounds for object facts or a grouping-gain claim on which a path depends is not supported, the event’s factual status is unclear, a propagation channel is missing, a cross-scale mapping fails, or the number of paths grows without signals to distinguish them. The same rule applies if the model is extremely sensitive to small changes in parameters, privacy or safety risks exceed the value of the information, the projection is asked to generate a diagnosis, punishment, or authorization directly, or a simple baseline already performs as well or better. If a shared premise or the overall conditions for use fail, all related runs stop.</p><p>Stopping is part of the output. It must not be used to conceal failure. The stopping record should state where the projection stopped, what has been obtained, which unknowns prevent continuation, which next observation would be most informative, and what risk further projection would add.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.10"}),e[26]||(e[26]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>An Example of a Cross-Circle Cascade <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.11 An Example of a Cross-Circle Cascade {#section-11-11}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Continue with the public departure from a job in Part 10. The starting snapshot shows that the employee faces long working hours and the pressure of care work, team trust is declining, company management is concerned with reputation, the professional community provides outside opportunities, and public opinion on the platform has yet to form. The event is the employee publishing an account of their departure. Part of its content can be verified, and part is personal narrative.</p><p>Direct effects include the team receiving the information, the platform obtaining a text that can be circulated, and management facing pressure to respond. On the first path, the company makes only a public denial. In the short term, external public opinion may branch toward either fading or amplification. If members inside the company interpret the denial as suppression, team trust declines and further departures occur through bridges in the professional community. On the second path, the company acknowledges the problem and starts verifiable adjustments to resources and appeals. If these are carried out, working hours and processes change on the organizational clock. Trust may recover more slowly on the experience–meaning clock. On the third path, changes in family care conditions ease the employee’s pressure after leaving. This does not directly change the team’s state unless the employee continues to bridge information or resources.</p><p>Early signals can include the number of internal appeals, records of hours actually worked, members’ public statements, and changes in hiring and departures. Contrary signals can include no actual change in resources after a policy is announced, increasing internal silence despite declining external exposure, or the professional community failing to form a bridge. Residuals may come from the states of key managers, the platform’s recommendation mechanism, or unobserved legal constraints. These enter only the ledger of variable candidates.</p><p>The projection still cannot say which path the company “should” take. It provides conditional consequences and signals that distinguish the paths. Forecast registration, comparison of options, normative premises, and authorization are addressed in the next Part.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.11"}),e[27]||(e[27]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-12" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.12</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Outputs of This Part <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.12 Outputs of This Part {#section-11-12}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A qualifying projection output should include a frozen joint snapshot, event type and evidence, mechanism and parameter assumptions, update steps, and a path graph. For every path, it should record conditions, clocks, support status, and early and contrary signals. It should also include residuals and variable candidates, stopping conditions, a plan for writing back results, and an explicit statement separating prediction and action.</p><p>Projection can improve structured preparation for what follows, but it remains conditional. Without registration before results, a simple baseline, calibration, and continuing write-back, it cannot support a claim that foresight capability has improved. Without normative grounds and authorization, it cannot support a claim that an action should be carried out. An analysis based on explicit normative premises can recommend an option, reject it, or recommend deferral. None of these judgments establishes that the option has been authorized for execution. For each such judgment, its grounds, costs, opposing case, and conditions for revision must be stated in full.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.12"}),e[28]||(e[28]=i('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-11-13" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">11.13</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Action Choice and Updating Conditions <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-11-13" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11.13 Action Choice and Updating Conditions {#section-11-13}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For dynamic questions involving actors, having a model evaluate a set of static options is insufficient. At each step, first use the current state to identify actions actually available, actions ruled out, and the source of the restrictions. Then analyze how an actor might choose given their information, resources, relationships, habits, and goals. Capability, belief, willingness, and actual action must be kept separate. Being able to act does not mean the actor will act. Stating an intention does not mean it has been carried out. An analyst’s recommendation does not amount to adoption by the actor.</p><p>For each choice with a basis in the material, specify which mechanism it triggers, which states change first, who is affected through which interfaces, and how these changes alter the set of actions available at the next step. State differences must be expressed in concrete terms such as resources, rules, relationships, beliefs, or feasible actions. Feeding the same materials to another model and generating another narrative cannot substitute for updating a state. If the material does not determine the choice, retain several conditional paths. Do not decide for the actor just to continue the projection.</p><p>Later projection starts from explicitly stated conditional state differences and inherits unresolved unknowns and restrictions. When real feedback arrives, first verify its source and event status. Then compare the original path, competing paths, and the simple baseline. Distinguish factual errors, mechanism errors, different choices of action, deviations in execution, and external changes. The corrected result should change the states, parameters, scope of mechanisms, or paths used in the next round. Keeping only the text of the feedback while retaining the same choice rule does not warrant claiming that the model has learned.</p></div>',1)),a(t,{section:"11.13"}),e[29]||(e[29]=i('<div class="theory-source"></div><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: Circles may be parallel, nested, overlapping, or bridged. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li><li id="fn2" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: A contract here is an analytical agreement registered and frozen before results, akin to pre-registration. <a href="#fnref2" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>',3))])}const v=s(d,[["render",u]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
